\begin{abstract}

Mixture-of-experts (MoE) models have become an important approach for scaling model parameters.
Modern MoE architectures increasingly combine two design choices: fine-grained expert segmentation and shared experts. 
The former increases expressiveness by enabling more expert combinations at a fixed activation ratio, and the latter introduces experts that are active for every token.
We identify an \textit{exploration dilemma} between these designs:  shared experts can make routing less exploratory by reducing the variability of token representations. 
This conflicts with the greater need for exploration induced by fine-grained expert segmentation due to the combinatorially enlarged routing space. Consequently, the additional expressiveness offered by fine-grained segmentation may not be fully realized in practice. 
MirrorMoE is proposed to reconcile this exploration dilemma by shrinking the exploration space through token-adaptive suppression of unnecessary experts.
MirrorMoE consistently improves performance across model scales. Code is available \href{https://github.com/BingcongLi/MirrorMoE/tree/main}{here}.

\end{abstract}


\section{Introduction}



Mixture-of-experts (MoE) models have been instrumental in scaling large language models (LLMs) to trillions of parameters and beyond \citep{lepikhin2020gshard,fedus2022switch}. MoEs adopt a sparse conditional-computation paradigm, where each token activates only a subset of experts, or subnetworks. During pretraining, recent work has shown that MoEs can outperform their dense counterparts under matched total parameter counts, compute, and data budgets \citep{li2026mixture}. At inference, sparse activation allows computational cost to grow sublinearly with total parameters. Thanks to these advantages, MoEs have become a core architecture underlying model families such as DeepSeek \citep{dai2024deepseekmoe,liu2024deepseek}, Kimi \citep{kimik2,kimik3}, and Qwen \citep{qwen1.5,yang2025qwen3}, among many others \citep{jiang2024mixtral,abdin2024phi}.




At the core of MoEs is the MoE layer, which consists of a router and $E$ experts, each implemented as a feedforward network (FFN). 
For each input token, the router selects $K$ experts ($K < E$) for sparse activation \citep{lepikhin2020gshard,fedus2022switch}. Building on this basic design, recent MoEs have increasingly adopted two notable architectural advances.
The first is \textit{fine-grained expert segmentation} \citep{kimik3,liu2024deepseek}. Recent MoEs tend to use larger numbers of both total and activated experts, i.e., larger $E$ and $K$. For example, at the same activation ratio $K/E$, $(E,K)=(64,8)$ is often preferred over $(E,K)=(8,1)$. Since there are $\binom{E}{K}$ expert combinations, the latter yields richer routing space for more expressive token representations \citep{elango2026latentmoe}.
The second advance is \textit{shared experts}. Activated for every token, shared experts capture common knowledge that would otherwise be learned redundantly by routed experts. This allows routed ones to devote more capacity to specialization.
The combination of the above architectural choices have become standard in modern MoEs \citep{dai2024deepseekmoe,liu2024deepseek,qwen1.5}.



This work reexamines the interaction between these two trends from the angle of \textit{routing exploration}. 
The goal of exploration is to search for alternative expert combinations during pretraining to identify (near-)optimal routes for each token.
On the one hand, realizing the expressive potential of fine-grained expert segmentation requires exploring a \textit{combinatorially} larger routing space within each MoE layer.
For example, the number of expert subsets increases from $8$ when $(E,K)=(8,1)$ to ${64 \choose 8}\approx 4.4\times10^9$ with $(E,K)=(64,8)$, i.e., several orders of magnitude increase. 
On the other hand, our analysis reveals an asymmetry during pretraining: shared experts dominate the learned token representations, leaving routed experts with less influence on the layer output. Consequently, even activating different routed experts only leads to limited variation in the learned features, making alternative routes harder to distinguish and thereby undermining the goal of exploration.


This exploration dilemma has tangible consequences for shared-expert MoEs. To see this, we train matched MoE models with and without shared experts at 4 scales, ranging from 100M to 2.4B total parameters. At each scale, we fix the total number of experts $E$ and vary the number of (total) activated experts $K$ over $\{6,8,10\}$. 
A larger $K$ simultaneously increases model expressiveness and demands more strategic exploration due to enlarged routing space. 
As shown in Fig.\ref{fig.scaling}, shared experts no longer provide benefits over their non-shared counterparts as $K$ increases. 
More strikingly, the performance of shared-expert MoEs can even degrade as $K$ increases in Figs.\ref{fig.scaling-498m} and \ref{fig.scaling-2.4b}, 
suggesting that the inefficient exploration hinders realization of the additional expressiveness from larger $K$.


MirrorMoE is proposed to reconcile the conflicting architecture designs above. 
It adaptively suppresses routed experts on a token basis, reducing the exploration space to better match the model's exploration capability. 
Technically, MirrorMoE is derived by mirroring the routing logits via sign negation, hence its name. As shown in Fig.\ref{fig.scaling}, MirrorMoE improves the performance of shared-expert MoEs and attains expressiveness gains from larger $K$. In summary, our contributions are as follows:


\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=0.4cm]
    \item[\ding{118}] We identify an \textbf{exploration dilemma} in modern MoEs: the enlarged routing space induced by fine-grained expert segmentation demands greater exploration, while shared experts make routing less explorative. As a result, the benefits of shared experts diminish as the routing space grows.

    \item[\ding{118}] We propose \textbf{MirrorMoE}, which adaptively shrinks the exploration space to reconcile competing demands above. MirrorMoE is conceptually simple, and its adaptivity is theoretically characterized.


    \item[\ding{118}] We demonstrate that MirrorMoE addresses the exploration dilemma across MoEs of different scales. On the 2.4B model, MirrorMoE improves pretraining and downstream performance by 0.67 and 1.64 points, respectively. Moreover, it enables shared-expert MoEs to consistently outperform their non-shared counterparts and restores the performance gains from increasing $K$; see Fig.\ref{fig.scaling}.

\end{enumerate}



