\subsection{Related work}\label{sec.related-work}




\textbf{MoEs.} Modern sparse MoEs originated with the sparsely gated MoE layer of \citet{shazeer2017outrageously} and were extended to large-scale Transformers in e.g., \citet{lepikhin2020gshard,fedus2022switch}. Later work focused on improving scalability, stability, and expert specialization. GLaM \citep{du2022glam} demonstrated that sparse language models could scale beyond one trillion parameters, while ST-MoE \citep{zoph2022st} improved training stability. MoEs have since become widely adopted. Beyond those mentioned earlier, other representative examples include Gemma4, OLMoE and gpt-oss \citep{team2026gemma, muennighoff2025olmoe, agarwal2025gpt}.
Across this development trajectory, two trends have clearly emerged: fine-grained expert segmentation and shared experts.



\textbf{Tension between expressiveness and pretraining.} A growing line of work shows that a model's expressive capacity may not be readily realized without carefully designed training procedures \citep{lion2026polar,zhang2026ancre,jha2026same,wei2025benefits}. 
Within the context of MoEs, related evidence is provided by \citet{jin2024moe++}, which augments learnable experts with fixed zero, copy, and constant experts. Although standard FFN experts can, in principle, learn these operations, explicitly incorporating them still improves empirical performance. 
The benefit of directly defining these operations, rather than learning them through training, suggests that the available expressiveness may not be fully realized in practice. 
Our work further shows that the additional expressiveness provided by fine-grained expert segmentation is not naturally attained in shared-expert MoEs.


Additional related work on MoE routing and systems is deferred to App.\ref{apdx.related-work} due to limited space.


\section{The Exploration Dilemma in Shared-Expert MoEs}

This section first reviews MoEs and then examines the effect of shared experts on exploration.


\subsection{MoE recap}

MoEs replace the FFN in each Transformer layer with a collection of FFNs, each referred to as an \emph{expert}. Among $E$ experts at layer $l$, there are two types: $S$ shared experts $\{{\cal SE}_{l,s}\}_s$, activated for every token \citep{liu2024deepseek}, and $R\!:=\!E\!-\!S$ routed experts $\{{\cal RE}_{l,e} \}_e$, activated in a token-driven manner. We use $K$ to denote the total number of activated experts, including the shared ones.

For $\h_l^n\in\mathbb{R}^d$, the representation of the $n$-th token entering the $l$-th MoE layer, the routed experts to be activated are selected by a linear projection $\G_l\in\mathbb{R}^{R\times d}$, known as the \emph{router}. The router produces a probability distribution over the routed experts, and those with the highest probabilities are activated. The activated routed experts are collected in ${\cal A}_l(\h_l^n)$:
\begin{align}\label{eq.moe-routing}
    p_{l,e} (\h_l^n)= \frac{\exp(\G_l [e,:] \h_l^n)}{\sum_{j=1}^R \exp(\G_l [j,:] \h_l^n)}, ~~~ {\cal A}_l(\h_l^n) = \topk_{K-S}   \big( \{p_{l,e} (\h_l^n) \}, e \in [R] \big).
\end{align}
Here, $\topk_{K-S}(\cdot)$ deterministically selects $K-S$ experts with the largest probabilities and $[R]:=\{1,\ldots,R\}$ denotes all the routed experts. 
The learned token representation of this layer combines the outputs of both shared and routed experts, where shared experts capture common features and routed experts learn specialized ones, i.e.,
\begin{align}\label{eq.moe}
	\h_{l+1}^n =\sum_{s=1}^S {\cal SE}_{l,s}(\mathbf{h}_l^n) + \sum_{e \in {\cal A}_l(\h_l^n) } \frac{p_{l,e} (\mathbf{h}_l^n)}{ Z(\h_l^n) } \cdot  {\cal RE}_{l,e}(\mathbf{h}_l^n)
\end{align}
where $Z(\h_l^n)=\sum_{j\in {\cal A}_l(\h_l^n) } p_{l,j} (\mathbf{h}_l^n)$ renormalizes the weights of the activated experts, as in \citep{yang2025qwen3} or $Z(\h_l^n)=1$ as in DeepSeek-MoE and Qwen1.5-MoE \citep{dai2024deepseekmoe,muennighoff2025olmoe, qwen-moe}. We follow the latter in this paper.
Vanilla MoEs without shared experts are recovered when $S=0$. For convenience, we omit the dependence of $p_{l,e}$ and ${\cal A}_l$ on $\h_l^n$, as well as the token index $n$ whenever clear from context.

Beyond shared experts, recent MoEs have also shifted toward fine-grained expert segmentation \citep{liu2024deepseek,yang2025qwen3,elango2026latentmoe}. For the same activation ratio, this trend favors configurations such as $R=64, K-S=8$ over $R=8, K-S=1$, enabling a combinatorially richer set of expert compositions and, hence, more expressive token representations. 
However, realizing such expressiveness through pretraining is far from straightforward, where
the challenge also stems from the \textit{combinatorial} expansion of the routing space. Identifying optimal expert combinations therefore requires more exploration. 
This motivates us to revisit exploration in MoE training.

\subsection{Shared experts weaken routing exploration}\label{sec.analysis}

This subsection reexamines the roles of shared and routed experts, reveals an asymmetry in their learning dynamics, and shows how shared-expert dominance can hinder exploration.


To isolate the impact of shared experts, we compare MoEs with and without them using matching datasets, tokenizers, and configurations. The models in this subsection have 121M activated and 498M total parameters, with $E=66$ and $K=8$. We set $S\!=\!2$ for shared-expert MoEs, while MoEs without shared experts amount to $S\!=\!0$. Details are deferred to App.\ref{sec.apdx.model-config}. We select checkpoints at $20\%$, $50\%$, and $100\%$ of pretraining to represent the early, middle, and final stages, respectively.

\textbf{Asymmetric training dynamics in shared-expert MoEs.} We first ablate individual experts to assess their importance. Specifically, we zero out each expert's output and measure the resulting perplexity change, $\Delta\mathsf{PPL}=\mathsf{PPL}_{\mathrm{after}}-\mathsf{PPL}_{\mathrm{base}}$. In Fig.\ref{fig.expert-ablation}, the two rightmost columns in the right-hand plots correspond to shared experts. Across all three checkpoints, ablating a shared expert leads to a substantially larger perplexity increase than ablating a routed expert. This shows that shared experts play a disproportionately important role in model performance.


