Another means to quantify the asymmetric roles of experts is through the so-termed \textit{routing criticality}, which replaces the learned router with uniform random routing and measures the resulting performance change.
 We apply this intervention one layer at a time and report the relative perplexity increase,
$\frac{\mathsf{PPL}_{\mathrm{after}}-\mathsf{PPL}_{\mathrm{base}}}{\mathsf{PPL}_{\mathrm{base}}}$. 
Larger values indicate greater dependence on the learned router. 
As shown in Fig.\ref{fig.routing-criticality}, uniform routing in the early and middle layers of the shared-expert MoE leads to a much smaller perplexity increase than in standard MoEs. 
In other words, \textit{shared experts in these layers capture such a large fraction of the learned representation that the layers are robust to random routing.}



Next, we examine how this asymmetry in expert roles shapes exploration. 
Exploration aims to discover alternative routes that produce diverse token representations. We therefore quantify exploration by measuring how much the learned representation changes under different routing decisions. 
Even before this analysis, the asymmetry between shared and routed experts suggests that exploration may be ineffective: if a highly exploratory routing policy, such as uniform routing, barely changes the output of a shared-expert layer, other routing is also unlikely to produce substantially different representations. We next confirm this intuition using two complementary variance metrics, one induced by experts and the other by routers.




\textbf{Shared experts suppress expert-induced variance.}
We first quantify how much an MoE layer’s output can vary when different experts are activated. To this end, we introduce the notion of \emph{expert variance}. Given an input token $\h_l$ and its learned output $\h_{l+1}$ in \eqref{eq.moe}, we define the expert variance as
\begin{align*}
	\mathsf{ExpertVar}_l = \frac{1}{R}\sum_{e=1}^R  \frac{\| \tilde{\h}_{l+1,e} - \mathbf{\h}_{l+1} \|^2}{\|\h_{l+1}\|^2} ~~~\text{where}~~~ \tilde{\h}_{l+1,e} = \sum_{s=1}^S {\cal SE}_{l,s}(\mathbf{h}_l) +  {\cal RE}_{l,e}(\h_l).
\end{align*}
In other words, $\tilde{\h}_{l+1,e}$ is the representation obtained if only a single expert $e$ is activated.
A larger $\mathsf{ExpertVar}_l$ indicates that changing routed experts can produce substantially different representations compared to $\mathbf{\h}_{l+1} $, indicating greater potential for exploration.
Moreover, $\mathsf{ExpertVar}_l$ bounds the relative change induced by \textit{any} routing policies from above if $Z(\h_l)=\sum_{j\in {\cal A}_l(\h_l) } p_{l,j} (\mathbf{h}_l)$. Let $\hat{\h}_{l+1}$ denote the representation obtained from any set of activated experts and routing probabilities. As shown in App.\ref{apdx.derive-ub}, we can prove that
	$\max_{\hat{\h}_{l+1}}  \|\hat{\h}_{l+1}-\h_{l+1}\|^2/ \|\h_{l+1}\|^2 \leq R \cdot \mathsf{ExpertVar}_l$. 
We evaluate $\mathsf{ExpertVar}_l$, averaged over tokens, for every MoE layer in Fig.\ref{fig.expert-var-norm}. 
Expert variance is smaller across all layers when shared experts are present. This is because shared experts dominate the output, while routed experts can only make relatively small variations. Consequently, shared-expert MoEs are less explorative even with different activated experts. 



