\subsection{Discussions on adaptivity of MirrorMoE}



Lastly, we theoretically characterize the \textit{adaptivity} of MirrorMoE by deriving \textit{sufficient} conditions for both expert suppression and preservation at mirror routing. We begin with the former. When an expert has a sufficiently small $\xi_{l,e}$ and $\rho$ is chosen appropriately, MirrorMoE activates this expert but functionally removes it as negligible aggregation weight is assigned. For convenience, we omit the layer index throughout this subsection.

\begin{proposition}[Sufficient condition for expert suppression] \label{prop.path-pruning}


	For a given token, let $\{\xi_e\}$ and $\{p_e\}$ be probabilities obtained as \eqref{eq.routing_prob}.
	Let ${\cal A}^{p}:= {\cal A }$, i.e., \eqref{eq.mirror-activated}, denote the set of experts activated by MirrorMoE,
	and define ${\cal A}^{\xi}:=\topk_{K-S}(\{\xi_e\}_{e=1}^R)$ as the set that would be activated under the standard routing probabilities $\{\xi_e\}$.
	Let $e_{\min}:=\arg\min_{e\in[R]}\xi_e$ with $\xi_{e_{\min}}=\epsilon$, and let $j:=\arg\min_{e\in{\cal A}^{\xi}}\xi_e$ with $\xi_j=c/R$. As $\epsilon\to0$, $\rho>c/(1+c)$ is sufficient for $e_{\min}\in{\cal A}^{p}\setminus{\cal A}^{\xi}$; that is, MirrorMoE activates this expert while functionally suppressing its contribution during aggregation.

\end{proposition}


This explains the reduced $K_\mathsf{eff}$ as visualized in Fig.\ref{fig.effpath}.
Next, we show the token adaptivity of MirrorMoE by identifying a regime in which no expert is suppressed. Specifically, when the router is uncertain about its choices, MirrorMoE behaves similarly to standard routing.




\begin{proposition}[Sufficient condition for expert preservation]\label{prop.no-path-pruning}
Let $\xi_{\max}:=\max_{e\in[R]}\xi_e$, $\xi_{\min}:=\min_{e\in[R]}\xi_e$, and $\kappa\!:=\!\sqrt{(1-\rho)/\rho}$. If $\xi_{\max}/\xi_{\min}\leq\kappa$, ${\cal A}^{p}\!=\!{\cal A}^{\xi}$. Moreover, with $\bar{\xi}_e$ defined in Def.\ref{def.eff-experrts}, it satisfies $\bar{\xi}_e\geq 1/((K\!-\!S)\kappa), \forall e\in{\cal A}^{p}$, i.e., no selected expert is functionally suppressed.
\end{proposition}



\textbf{Another interpretation for MirrorMoE.} 
The above discussions provide an alternative interpretation of MirrorMoE beyond exploration. 
In MoEs with large $E$ and $K$, not every token may require the full expressiveness from all activated experts. 
If a token can be learned well using fewer experts, the capacity of remaining experts can be devoted to other tokens.
MirrorMoE can therefore be viewed as an approach for tokens to adaptively determine their number of activated experts, where this adaptivity arises from the expert suppression and preservation shown in Props. \ref{prop.path-pruning} and \ref{prop.no-path-pruning}, respectively.




