\subsection{Comparison under enlarged routing space}


Building on the same model configuration, we vary the number of activated experts as $K\in\{6,8,10\}$. Increasing $K$ enables richer expert combinations, thereby enlarging both the exploration space and model expressiveness. As shown in Fig.\ref{fig.scaling}, the standard shared-expert MoE is outperformed by its non-shared counterpart at large $K$. In contrast, MirrorMoE mitigates the exploration dilemma, enabling the shared-expert model to consistently outperform the non-shared MoE across all tested cases. More importantly, the perplexity of MirrorMoE consistently decreases as $K$ increases, suggesting that the additional expressiveness enabled by larger $K$ is realized.

\subsection{Additional experiments}\label{sec.num.ablation}


We use a MoE-100M-A38M with $K=8$ (2 shared and 6 routed experts) for ablation study. 


\textbf{Hyperparameters.} Fig.\ref{fig.hyperparams} compares constant, linear, and cosine schedules for $\rho_t$ in \eqref{eq.routing_prob}, together with initial values $\rho_0\in\{0.1,0.2,0.4\}$. A cosine schedule with $\rho_0=0.4$ achieves the best performance and is therefore used in all preceding experiments.



\textbf{Benefit of adaptivity.} We demonstrate the importance of token/layer adaptivity in MirrorMoE by comparing it with two simple approaches that also have smaller exploration space. Both begin with fewer activated experts and progressively increase their number. Specifically, we set $K=\mathsf{round}(\widetilde{K}_t)$, where $\widetilde{K}_t$ increases from $4$ to $8$ following either a linear or cosine schedule. Here, $\widetilde{K}_t$ includes two shared experts, and the initial value $\widetilde{K}_0=4$ is informed by the number of effective experts in Fig.\ref{fig.effpath}. These baselines use the same $K$ for all tokens and layers and therefore lack adaptivity compared to MirrorMoE. As shown in Tab.\ref{tab.num.adaptivity}, neither matches the performance of MirrorMoE.

\textbf{MirrorMoE is tailored to shared-expert MoEs.} We additionally apply MirrorMoE to MoEs \textit{without} shared experts to further validate our motivation based on the exploration dilemma induced by shared experts. As shown in Tab.\ref{tab.numerical.non-shared-moe} in App.\ref{apdx.moe-ns}, MirrorMoE performs comparably to vanilla training. This is consistent with our analysis: without shared experts, the exploration dilemma is less pronounced, leaving less need to shrink the exploration space.


\section{Conclusion}

This work identifies an exploration dilemma in MoEs that combine fine-grained expert segmentation with shared experts. This tension can prevent the additional expressiveness enabled by fine-grained segmentation from being fully realized while also diminishing the benefits of shared experts. To address this, we propose MirrorMoE, which adaptively shrinks the exploration space to better match the model's exploration capability. MirrorMoE consistently improves performance across scales.


\textbf{Future direction.} Our current approach addresses the exploration dilemma by shrinking the exploration space. A promising future direction is to improve the routing strategy itself for more efficient exploration.

