\begin{paperabstract}
Existing agent benchmarks typically evaluate agents on well-specified tasks defined by issue reports, specifications, or task tickets. Real-world customer delivery, however, poses fundamentally different challenges: forward-deployed engineers (FDEs) must clarify ambiguous requirements, work in live systems, and uncover unstated constraints. To address this gap, we introduce FDE-Gym, a benchmark of 95 end-to-end delivery tasks from 20 customer scenarios across 18 sectors, each adapted by industry experts from real-world delivery projects. In simulated customer environments featuring scripted stakeholders and modifiable customer systems, agents interact with stakeholders and operate on production-like systems to deliver working solutions. Beyond binary task completion, FDE-Gym scores agents on how much their delivered system improves the customer’s own success metric, normalized between 0 (unchanged system) and 1 (reference solution); scores are penalized for stakeholder interactions (messages, trial runs, and waiting time) to discourage excessive or careless engagement. FDE-Gym also provides an online platform where humans can develop and practice FDE skills under the same grading criteria.

We evaluate 13 model configurations from seven providers. In the realistic setting (unstated constraints need to be discovered), the best configuration achieves a mean FDE-Gym score of 0.56, and resolves only 14 of the 95 tasks (a score of at least 0.8 with no incident). Discovering unstated constraints is the primary bottleneck: performance drops sharply when models must uncover them without explicit guidance. However, information discovery alone does not account for the gap: even on the 23 tasks where the best configuration obtains every key fact from stakeholders, it achieves a normalized business uplift of 0.8 or above on only 10.

These results reveal a substantial gap between solving well-specified tasks and delivering effective AI systems for real customers. Closing this gap requires agents that can surface unstated constraints, operate reliably in live systems, and achieve measurable improvements on customer metrics.
\end{paperabstract}

\section{Introduction}

Deploying AI in enterprise settings requires more than meeting stated customer requirements. The challenge often lies in identifying which changes will improve customer operations in practice. One scenario in our benchmark illustrates this problem. A vice president at a drilling operator seeks a generative-AI success story for the November board meeting. The vice president requests a daily summary email and an “Ask your wells” chat box, noting that the downtime-coding system is already in place and therefore outside the project scope.

Yet a solution built strictly to the task instruction can be delivered on time, pass every functional check, and still fail to move the customer’s own business metric by a single point. The bottleneck lies in monthly downtime credit claims. The existing keyword-based script incorrectly attributes some downtime to the contractor, resulting in at least one disputed line each month and suspending the credit for that month. Addressing this problem requires revisiting the downtime-coding system that the sponsor had explicitly excluded from the project scope. The engineer must identify the applicable contractual rule, confirm the interpretation with the cost engineer, and obtain approval for the change from an approver who is unavailable that week.

This is the role of a forward-deployed engineer (FDE), an engineer assigned by a vendor to work within a customer organization. The role involves more than building what the sponsor initially requests. %
An FDE must identify what will actually help and work with customer systems and stakeholders to deliver the improvement.


Existing benchmarks rarely address this last mile of AI delivery, typically assuming that the problem has already been well defined. Software-engineering benchmarks provide issues and hidden tests {\color{navy}[\hyperlink{ref.1}{1}, \hyperlink{ref.2}{2}, \hyperlink{ref.3}{3}]}, deployment benchmarks provide specifications {\color{navy}[\hyperlink{ref.4}{4}]}, enterprise benchmarks provide predefined tasks {\color{navy}[\hyperlink{ref.5}{5}, \hyperlink{ref.6}{6}]}, and clarification benchmarks withhold information from an otherwise correctly specified task {\color{navy}[\hyperlink{ref.7}{7}, \hyperlink{ref.8}{8}]} --- in each case, the underlying problem remains fixed.
Recent benchmarks come closer to real-world delivery.
\ensuremath{\tau}\textsuperscript{\ensuremath{\tau}}-bench {\color{navy}[\hyperlink{ref.9}{9}]} evaluates agents that build customer-service systems from client requirements and deploy the resulting systems against held-out users. A post-training benchmark {\color{navy}[\hyperlink{ref.10}{10}]} assigns agents the role of forward-deployed engineers and evaluates whether the delivered model improves performance. XYEval {\color{navy}[\hyperlink{ref.11}{11}]} tests agents on misleading user requests. However, these benchmarks still address individual aspects of delivery in isolation.

FDE-Gym addresses these challenges in a single benchmark of 95 tasks from 20 customer scenarios across 18 sectors, each adapted by industry experts from real-world delivery projects. The sponsor’s request may target the wrong problem, critical constraints must be discovered through stakeholder interaction, and performance is measured using the customer’s own business metric. A solution that addresses the wrong problem therefore earns no credit.

Delivered systems are deployed without modification and evaluated on held-out production data. The FDE-Gym score measures improvement on the customer’s own business metric after deducting stakeholder contact cost (Section 2.3 details what is charged).
The do-nothing baseline (the unchanged system) scores 0, the expert-authored reference solution scores 1, and literal compliance with the sponsor’s request scores at most 0.2. Experimental results show that current models perform well below the reference solution on this scale. %


To identify where agents fail, we present each scenario at four task instruction levels with progressively less information (from a fully specified problem (L0) down to the sponsor's original request, which may not address the underlying problem (L3)).
The environment, stakeholders, and grader remain unchanged across all four levels. Because only the task instruction varies, differences in performance isolate the effect of the information withheld. We evaluate 13 model configurations from seven providers using this setup. Five main findings emerge.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Even the best model configuration falls well short of the reference solution.} At L3, Claude Opus 5.5 achieves the highest mean score of 0.56 and resolves 14 of 95 tasks, while five of the thirteen configurations resolve none. Selecting the best-performing configuration for each task still yields a score of only 0.70, with 30 tasks resolved.
\item \textbf{Discovering unstated constraints is the primary bottleneck for leading models.} When the relevant rules and constraints are provided, the three leading models score 0.73–0.82. Requiring these models to discover the constraints reduces scores sharply to 0.39–0.62. On the original tasks, L3 scores are at most 0.08 points lower than L1 scores.
\item \textbf{Obtaining the necessary information does not ensure effective delivery.} At L3, leading models obtain 72–85\% of the key facts available from stakeholders, yet the delivered systems achieve only 0.60–0.70 of the reference improvement. Stakeholder contact cost reduces the final scores by a further 0.14–0.22.
\item \textbf{Weak models can leave customers worse off than if no changes had been made.} Claude Haiku 4.5 scores below the do-nothing baseline on 79 of 95 tasks, while systems delivered by Mistral Small 4 fall below this baseline on 51 tasks even before deducting stakeholder contact cost.
\item \textbf{No model performs well across all scenarios.} In five of the 20 scenarios, no model achieves a mean score of at least 0.5. Across tasks within a scenario, the median score range for a leading model is 0.34–0.44.
\end{itemize}

We make three contributions. First, we introduce FDE-Gym, a benchmark that evaluates whether agents can identify the underlying customer problem and deliver a working solution that measurably improves business outcomes. Second, we develop a methodology for constructing scenarios that combines task variants, scripted stakeholders, and predefined scoring anchors to support reproducible evaluation and limit rewards for literal compliance and shortcut solutions. Third, we evaluate 13 model configurations and provide an online platform where humans and agents can practice delivery under the same grading criteria.

\section{Anatomy of a Task}
Each task represents a single customer engagement involving one customer (the organization that hires the agent, whose people are the stakeholders the agent can question), one sponsor (the customer-side person who assigns the task), and one production system to improve. Figure 3 shows the four components of a task.

