\subsection{Environment and Available Information}

The agent starts in a workspace containing the task instruction (the description of the work assigned by the sponsor), a copy of the customer’s production system, relevant documents and historical data, and a staff directory with contact details. The runtime environment provides standard Python and SQLite. Installing additional packages and accessing the general network are prohibited. The delivered system may send network requests only to the company’s LLM gateway, which charges according to usage. Once delivery is complete, the contents of \texttt{system/} are deployed to production without modification. Production continues to call the original entry points, so new functionality must be integrated with these entry points to take effect.

The agent is not explicitly informed that stakeholder contact incurs costs, that some people should not be contacted or are irrelevant to the problem, or that the sponsor’s request may be misdirected. Identifying these issues is a central objective of the evaluation. Relevant clues appear in the staff directory, customer documents, and data, where an FDE would typically look for such information. As in real-world projects, documents may be outdated, legacy code may reflect incorrect assumptions, and important information may not be explicitly stated in the available materials.

\subsection{Task Instruction Levels}

