\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

The four task instruction levels (Table 1) distinguish the challenges of implementation, constraint discovery, and problem clarification by varying the task instruction while keeping the environment and evaluation criteria fixed. The L0 instruction explicitly states the underlying problem, relevant rules and constraints, and scoring criteria, leaving the agent to design and implement a solution. The L1 instruction withholds the rules and constraints, while the L2 instruction also provides only a vague description of the problem. The L3 instruction presents the sponsor’s original request, requiring the agent to assess whether the request addresses the underlying problem. We use L3 as the primary evaluation setting because this level most closely reflects the starting point of real-world customer delivery projects. L0 does not eliminate the need for engineering decisions. By specifying the rules while leaving solution design open, L0 measures what an agent can build when all underlying facts are provided.







\subsection{FDE-Gym Score}

FDE-Gym evaluates a delivered system by the improvement it achieves on the customer’s business metric, measured on held-out production data. For example, an after-sales system is assessed by its net financial benefit, while a drilling system is assessed by the downtime hours correctly attributed to a contractor for compensation claims.

We define the following metrics to assess agent performance on these delivery tasks.

\textbf{Normalized Business Uplift (\textit{U}).} We measure the delivery’s business improvement relative to that achieved by an expert-authored reference solution:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:business-uplift}
U = \frac{\mathrm{KPI}_{\mathrm{delivered}}-\mathrm{KPI}_{\mathrm{baseline}}}{\mathrm{KPI}_{\mathrm{reference}}-\mathrm{KPI}_{\mathrm{baseline}}}
\end{equation}

Here, KPI\textsubscript{baseline}, KPI\textsubscript{delivered}, and KPI\textsubscript{reference} denote the customer’s business metric for the unchanged system, the delivered system, and the expert-authored reference solution, respectively, evaluated on the same held-out data. The unchanged system is the do-nothing baseline. Thus, \textit{U} = 0 represents no improvement, \textit{U} = 1 matches the reference improvement, and \textit{U} \ensuremath{<} 0 indicates an outcome worse than the baseline.

\textbf{Incident Adjustment (\textit{U}\textsubscript{adj}).} If a predefined operational incident occurs, such as an unauthorized action, positive uplift is set to zero while negative uplift is retained:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:incident-adjustment}
U_{\mathrm{adj}} = \begin{cases} U, & \text{if no incident occurs}, \\ \min(U,0), & \text{otherwise}. \end{cases}
\end{equation}

\textbf{Stakeholder Contact Cost (\textit{C}).} Stakeholder contact cost is expressed in the same normalized units as business uplift. It is the sum of the charges incurred by the agent’s contacts with the customer:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:contact-cost}
C = \sum_{k\in K} c_k,
\end{equation}

where \textit{K} is the set of chargeable contacts in the run and \textit{c}\textsubscript{k} is the charge for contact \textit{k} under the task’s predefined pricing and contact rules. Each stakeholder has a hidden per-message rate, with higher charges when the agent exceeds their patience or violates a contact restriction. Unanswered messages are not charged. In scenarios with a trial environment or a calendar, each trial run and each day of waiting is also a chargeable contact.

\textbf{FDE-Gym Score (\textit{S}).} The FDE-Gym Score measures delivery performance in a single evaluation run. It is the incident-adjusted normalized business uplift minus stakeholder contact cost:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:final-score}
S = U_{\mathrm{adj}}-C.
\end{equation}

Without an incident, \textit{S} = \textit{U} \ensuremath{-} \textit{C}. A task is considered \textit{resolved} if its score is at least 0.8 and no incident occurs.

\textbf{Average FDE-Gym Score (\ensuremath{\overline{S}}).} The Average FDE-Gym Score summarizes a model’s delivery performance across tasks and repeated runs. To prevent extreme business losses from dominating the reported average, we floor the incident-adjusted uplift at \ensuremath{-}1 before deducting the full stakeholder contact cost. We then average across tasks and runs:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:aggregation}
\overline{S} = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{1}{R}\sum_{r=1}^{R}\left[\max\left(U_{\mathrm{adj},i,r},-1\right)-C_{i,r}\right].
\end{equation}

Here, \textit{N} is the number of evaluated tasks, \textit{R} is the number of runs per task, and \textit{i} and \textit{r} index tasks and runs, respectively. Each task receives equal weight. The floor applies only to aggregation and does not change the single-run score \textit{S} used to determine whether a task is resolved.

