\section{Building the Benchmark}

\subsection{Scenario Construction from Real-World Delivery Projects}

Figure 1 traces how a real delivery becomes a task in the benchmark. Industry experts adapted real-world customer delivery projects carried out in China, Singapore, and the United States since 2024 into 20 scenarios spanning 18 sectors (Table 2). Each scenario yields an original task and several variants of it, which share a common customer environment but differ in key facts. Each original task and each variant is a separate benchmark task, for a total of 95 tasks. Tasks require agents to deliver an agent, workflow, or service that operates in the customer’s environment. The scenarios are grounded in industry practice, with the relevant regulations, standards, and public sources documented. Companies and individuals are either de-identified or fictional, and some data are adapted from real-world industry sources.

\subsection{Scenario Variants and Underlying Facts}

A single fixed task per scenario may allow models to rely on memorized solutions or recurring patterns. To reduce this risk, experts designed two to seven variants in addition to each scenario’s original task. Variants change key facts, including which rule is outdated, who holds critical information, and whether the sponsor’s request addresses the underlying problem. These changes may require different delivery solutions, so a solution that succeeds on one task of a scenario may fail on another. Across scenarios, no single problem pattern appears in more than one-third of the set. Together, these design choices encourage agents to evaluate the evidence in each task rather than apply a fixed solution.

\subsection{Stakeholder Simulation}

FDE-Gym simulates more than 130 customer stakeholders across its 20 scenarios. Stakeholder responses are scripted for reproducibility, as switching the user-simulation model can shift measured agent success rates by several percentage points {\color{navy}[\hyperlink{ref.14}{14}]}. A small model maps questions to topics, while each stakeholder’s knowledge, misconceptions, and conditions for disclosing information are fixed in advance. The scripts incorporate limited patience, requirements for supporting evidence, dependencies on prior conversations, temporary unavailability, and contact restrictions. Agents must infer stakeholder roles and communication constraints from customer documents.

