\subsection{Withholding Constraints Produces the Largest Observed Score Drop}

\begin{finding}{Finding 2}
Withholding the constraints reduces the scores of the three leading models by 0.28 on the same tasks (95\% interval 0.17 to 0.39). Making the request vague, and then misdirected, reduces them by only a further 0.04 (\ensuremath{-}0.03 to 0.14).
\end{finding}

Figure 2b traces the progression across task instruction levels, and the left half of Table 3 reports every level on the same 20 original tasks; the values quoted in this section refer to these tasks. With the rules given (L0), the three leading models score 0.73–0.82 and resolve 11–13 of 20 tasks. At L1, where the problem is still stated correctly but its constraints must be found, they score 0.39–0.62 and resolve 1–7. The loss has two components. The delivered systems deteriorate, from 0.81–0.90 to 0.63–0.80 of the reference uplift, and discovering the constraints incurs a cost: questions per task rise from 3.9–7.0 to 11.3–13.0, and stakeholder contact cost from 0.07–0.10 to 0.18–0.24.

Beyond this step, the scores of these models are nearly flat. Claude Opus 5.5 scores 0.62, 0.54, and 0.55 at L1, L2, and L3; Claude Sonnet 5.5 scores 0.45, 0.47, and 0.46; and GPT-6 Astra scores 0.39, 0.34, and 0.33. Using paired results for the same 20 tasks and averaging across the three models, the decline from L0 to L1 is 0.28 (95\% interval 0.17 to 0.39) and the decline from L1 to L3 is 0.04 (\ensuremath{-}0.03 to 0.14). The first step therefore costs more than the two that follow, although a further loss of up to 0.14 cannot be excluded, and for an individual model the upper bound is as large as 0.25. Our initial hypothesis was that problem definition would be the bottleneck: an agent given a symptom or a wrong solution would build the wrong system. For the leading models, the data do not support this. One interpretation is that once these models must search for constraints at all, they also discover what the sponsor actually needs. For weaker models, the steps beyond L1 remain costly: across L0–L3, GPT-5.5 scores 0.51, 0.15, 0.04, and 0.06, and Claude Haiku 4.5 scores 0.11, \ensuremath{-}0.11, \ensuremath{-}0.30, and \ensuremath{-}0.25. %

The fully specified level yields a result of its own: even with every rule provided, no model exceeds 0.82 on the same 20 tasks. Knowing the facts does not remove the need for engineering.

\subsection{Recovering Key Facts Does Not Ensure Effective Delivery}

\begin{finding}{Finding 3}
Leading models obtain most of the facts held by stakeholders yet deliver only 0.60–0.70 of the reference uplift; stakeholder contact costs further reduce their scores.
\end{finding}

The grader records which key facts an agent obtained from stakeholders, which allows us to separate information discovery from system building. At L3 the three leading models ask 10.0–12.3 questions per task and obtain 72–85\% of the key facts. Their normalized business uplift is 0.60–0.70. Figure 5a shows the pattern across all thirteen configurations: more facts are associated with better systems, but the leading group sits at the far right of the plot and still well below the reference solution. In the 73 task–model cases where one of the three obtained \textit{every} key fact, the normalized business uplift averages 0.66 and reaches at least 0.8 in 27 of them (37\%). For Claude Opus 5.5 alone, every key fact was obtained on 23 tasks, and the uplift reached at least 0.8 on 10 of them.

\begin{figure}[tbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=1.000\linewidth]{fig6}
\caption{\textbf{(a)} Share of key stakeholder facts obtained against normalized business uplift, at L3. Leading models learn most of the facts and deliver 0.60–0.70 of the reference uplift. \textbf{(b)} Mean model usage cost per run against score at L3.}
\end{figure}

The second component of the finding is stakeholder contact cost. GPT-6 Astra’s delivered systems reach 0.8 on 36 of 95 tasks, but only 9 remain resolved once stakeholder contact cost is deducted; for Claude Opus 5.5 the figures are 40 and 14. Agents are not informed that questions incur a cost, and they ask as if questions were free. Stakeholder contact cost consumes 0.14 of the 0.70 uplift delivered by Claude Opus 5.5 and 0.22 of the 0.60 delivered by GPT-6 Astra. At the other extreme are models that rarely ask: Mistral Small 4 asks 0.7 questions per task and obtains 6\% of the key facts.

\subsection{Weak Models Frequently Score Below the Do-Nothing Baseline}

\begin{finding}{Finding 4}
The weakest models score below the do-nothing baseline on 60–83\% of tasks; on these tasks, delivering nothing would have been the better outcome.
\end{finding}

\begin{figure}[tbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.843\linewidth]{fig7}
\caption{Distribution of each model’s 95 tasks at L3 by score band. Scores of 0.2 or below correspond to the cap on literal compliance with the sponsor’s task instruction.}
\end{figure}

A score of zero is not the floor: a system that pays out claims it should have refused, charges the wrong party, or wakes the wrong engineer performs worse than the system it replaced. Figure 6 sorts each model’s 95 tasks at L3 into four score bands. Claude Opus 5.5 scores below zero on 5 tasks and Claude Sonnet 5.5 on 7, whereas Claude Haiku 4.5 does so on 79 and GPT-6 Luna on 63.%

Part of this reflects stakeholder contact cost, but much of it reflects the delivered system itself. Before any stakeholder contact cost is deducted, the systems delivered by Mistral Small 4 score below the do-nothing baseline on 51 tasks and those delivered by Claude Haiku 4.5 (thinking) on 55, compared with 1–4 for the three leading models. On 23 tasks, the system delivered by Mistral Small 4 caused more harm than the benefit produced by the reference solution. Extended thinking does not help: Claude Haiku 4.5 scores \ensuremath{-}0.33 without it and \ensuremath{-}0.35 with it. For a buyer, the practical implication is that a weak agent is not a cheaper version of a strong one; its expected contribution on these tasks is negative. %

\subsection{Model Performance Varies Substantially Across Scenarios and Task Variants}

\begin{finding}{Finding 5}
On five of the 20 scenarios no model averages at least 0.5, and the same scenario with different key facts yields a different score.
\end{finding}

