\begin{abstract}
We develop a robust reinforcement learning (RL) framework for learning adaptive treatment policies that generalize across patients and remain reliable under errors in the measured biomarker and shifts in the disease dynamics. By modeling the patient as a hidden context, we address generalization and robustness in a unified manner through robust contextual Markov decision processes. Applying our framework to adaptive therapy for metastatic prostate cancer in a simulator calibrated to clinical data, we obtain generalizable and robust policies that substantially outperform the existing adaptive therapy method. In particular, we attain robustness with almost no loss of performance when the biomarker is accurate and the disease evolves as anticipated, and find that the resulting policy Pareto-dominates the existing method across a diverse array of scenarios. Our findings suggest that robust RL holds great promise as a principled methodology for learning reliable adaptive treatment policies.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

Adapting treatment to the evolving condition of a patient has received considerable attention in medicine, with success reported in a broad range of applications \cite{murphy2003optimal,chakraborty2014dynamic,kosorok2019precision,komorowski2018artificial,brown2019six,yu2023reinforcement}. In oncology, adaptive therapy pursues this idea to delay the emergence of drug resistance \cite{gatenby2009adaptive}. Its rationale is that, under continuous treatment, drug-sensitive cells are eliminated and drug-resistant cells are left to grow without competition. By pausing treatment once the tumor has responded and resuming it once the tumor has regrown, clinicians can retain sensitive cells that continue to suppress the resistant ones \cite{gatenby2009adaptive,strobl2021turnover}. This approach was tested in a pilot clinical trial of the drug abiraterone for metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer \cite{zhang2017integrating,zhang2022evolution}. In this clinical trial, clinicians paused abiraterone once prostate-specific antigen (PSA), a blood marker that tracks tumor burden, fell below half of its pretreatment value, and resumed it once PSA returned to the pretreatment value, a protocol we refer to as AT50. Compared with a contemporaneous cohort who received continuous treatment with abiraterone as the standard of care, the median time to progression more than doubled under AT50, while patients received no abiraterone during 46\% of their time in the clinical trial \cite{zhang2022evolution}.

Despite its remarkable success, AT50 is by design a simple heuristic in the sense that it applies the same pair of thresholds to every patient and responds only to the most recent PSA value. In addition, its benefit has so far been observed in only 17 patients, and, based on the models fitted to the same data, other schedules are expected to further improve outcomes \cite{zhang2022evolution}. Existing efforts toward more refined schedules include optimal control of competition models \cite{cunningham2018optimal}, deep reinforcement learning (RL) for controlling the emergence of drug resistance \cite{engelhardt2020dynamic}, and more recently RL policies trained within models calibrated to prostate cancer patients \cite{gallagher2024mathematical,lu2024deep,ma2026single}. Nevertheless, these policies are evaluated within the same models used for their training, and their performance beyond these models remains largely unexamined. Ideally, one would like to learn, in a data-driven manner, a dynamic treatment policy that is more sophisticated than AT50, attains improved performance, and generalizes to the broad population of patients beyond those enrolled in the clinical trial. More importantly, such a policy should remain reliable under the shifts between training and deployment that are common in clinical practice \cite{finlayson2021clinician}.

To be more specific, we identify three properties that a treatment policy learned from data should possess. To begin with, the policy should generalize in the sense that the performance it attains on the patients seen during training carries over to new patients drawn from the same population. From the perspective of contextual and hidden-parameter Markov decision processes (MDPs) \cite{hallak2015contextual,doshivelez2016hidden,kwon2021rl,steimle2021multi}, the natural goal without interacting with a new patient is a policy that is near-optimal on average over the population \cite{ye2023power}, and generalization across populations of environments has been studied extensively in deep RL \cite{kirk2023survey,tobin2017domain}. With a simulation model, we can pursue this goal by training on as many simulated patients as needed, far beyond the 17 patients of the pilot clinical trial, provided that the simulated population is broad enough to contain the patients of interest. In addition, the policy should be robust to errors in its observation. In particular, PSA serves only as an indirect measure of the underlying tumor burden, and radiographic progression can occur without any rise in PSA \cite{bryce2017radiographic}. Consequently, the quantity on which the policy acts may deviate from the quantity it aims to control, while RL policies are known to be sensitive to even small adversarial perturbations of their observations \cite{huang2017adversarial,zhang2020robust}. Most importantly, the policy should retain reliable performance under shifts in the patient dynamics. Indeed, the parameters of the model, including the strength of competition between sensitive and resistant cells on which the success of adaptive therapy hinges, are estimated from the sparse PSA records of a small cohort of patients, and are hence subject to considerable statistical error \cite{zhang2022evolution,browning2026adaptive}. Such shifts are particularly consequential in medical domains, where real patients are scarce and the dynamics of a patient may further change during treatment \cite{antonarakis2014arv7}.

To address these requirements, in this manuscript we propose to study adaptive therapy through the lens of robust contextual MDPs. A contextual MDP \cite{hallak2015contextual} describes a family of MDPs whose transition dynamics are indexed by a context, drawn from a population and hidden from the decision maker. Adaptive therapy fits naturally into this framework once we identify the context with the patient, namely the initial composition of the tumor together with its competition and growth parameters. Each patient then defines its own tumor dynamics, to be inferred by the clinician from the history of PSA measurements and treatment decisions, and AT50 is one particular policy acting on this history. By viewing the patient as a context, we treat generalization and robustness to shifts in the patient dynamics in a unified manner, since both concern the population from which the context is drawn. Specifically, generalization requires the policy to perform well on new contexts drawn from this population, which can be attained by training on sufficiently many sampled contexts, while robustness requires the policy to remain reliable when the context deviates from this population and thereby shifts the transition kernel that it determines.

To attain such robustness, we draw on robust MDPs, in which one seeks the best worst-case performance over an ambiguity set of transition kernels, and which have been studied extensively for standard MDPs \cite{iyengar2005robust,nilim2005robust,wiesemann2013robust,li2022first,morimoto2005robust,pinto2017robust,zhang2020robust}. We carry this viewpoint over to the contextual MDP and formulate robustness as a Markov game between the controller and the context, in which the context may deviate adversarially from the population from which it is drawn \cite{li2023rectangularity,li2026robust}. To account for errors in the observation, we further train the policy under random perturbations of the measured PSA. Within a model of abiraterone therapy fitted to clinical data \cite{zhang2022evolution}, which we refer to as the nominal model, the resulting robust policies substantially outperform AT50. In addition, we attain robustness to shifts in the patient dynamics at \emph{almost no price} in the nominal model, and the resulting policy \emph{nearly Pareto-dominates} the nominally trained policy, i.e., the policy trained in the nominal model with exact observations, under a broad range of realistic errors in the observation and shifts in the patient dynamics. Our contributions can be summarized as follows.

First, we formulate adaptive therapy as a contextual MDP in which the patient is modeled as a hidden context, and find that a policy learned within this formulation substantially outperforms AT50 and generalizes to patients fitted to clinical data. Within the competition model of \cite{zhang2022evolution}, the learned policy extends the mean progression-free time from 29.58 months under AT50 to 50.35 months, while treating in a comparable fraction of months. In addition, on the 32 patients whose initial tumor compositions are fitted to clinical data in \cite{zhang2022evolution}, policies trained on a broad synthetic population constructed without reference to these patients perform comparably to policies trained on a population calibrated to them. Hence, generalization to the patients of interest can be attained by training on sufficiently many simulated patients drawn from a broad population, without tailoring the population to these patients.

Second, we find that errors in PSA of realistic magnitude substantially reduce the benefit of policies that do not account for them. Specifically, under random errors of up to about 20\% of the true PSA value, the nominally trained policy loses a substantial part of its benefit, and AT50 performs only marginally better than continuous treatment. By training under such errors, we obtain a policy that retains the benefit of adaptive therapy in the sense that its performance under such errors nearly matches that without them.

Finally, we formulate robustness to shifts in the patient dynamics as a Markov game between the controller and the context over an ambiguity set of transition kernels \cite{li2026robust}, and find that the resulting kernel-robust policy nearly Pareto-dominates the nominally trained policy and AT50 across all the conditions we consider. Specifically, the kernel-robust policy (1) performs nearly as well as the nominally trained policy in the nominal model, (2) substantially outperforms every other type of policy under shifts in the dynamics, and (3) substantially improves on the nominally trained policy and AT50 even under errors in the observation. Our findings suggest that robustness to shifts in the patient dynamics can be attained at almost no price in the nominal model, and that the resulting policy remains robust against a broad spectrum of errors in the observation and shifts in the patient dynamics. In view of this, we believe that robust RL holds great promise as a principled methodology for learning adaptive therapy policies.

The rest of this manuscript is organized as follows. In Section~\ref{sec:formulation}, we introduce contextual MDPs and their robust counterparts, and formulate adaptive therapy within this framework. We present the numerical experiments in Section~\ref{sec:results}, and conclude the manuscript in Section~\ref{sec:conclusion}.

\section{Problem Formulation}\label{sec:formulation}

In this section, we discuss contextual MDPs and their robust counterparts, and formulate adaptive therapy for metastatic prostate cancer as a concrete instance. Specifically, in Section~\ref{sec:cmdp-general}, we introduce contextual MDPs and establish finite-sample guarantees on the number of sampled contexts needed to learn a generalizable policy. The concrete instance for adaptive therapy is then introduced in Section~\ref{sec:cmdp}, where we adopt the model of abiraterone therapy in \cite{zhang2022evolution}, which is fitted to clinical data from a pilot clinical trial and a contemporaneous cohort. In Section~\ref{sec:uncertainty}, we further introduce the robust counterparts of the contextual MDP against errors in the observation and shifts in the patient dynamics.

\subsection{Contextual Markov Decision Processes}\label{sec:cmdp-general}

We consider a finite-horizon contextual MDP \cite{hallak2015contextual,steimle2021multi} with state space $\cX$, action space $\cA$, observation space $\cY$, horizon $T$, and context space $\cC$. Each context $c\in\cC$ specifies an initial distribution $\mu_c$ over $\cX$, a transition kernel $P_c$, an observation map $O_c:\cX\to\cY$, and a reward function $r_c:\cX\times\cA\to\RR$, where $P_c(\cdot\given x,a)$ denotes the distribution of the next state when action $a$ is taken in state $x$. At the beginning of an episode, a context $c$ is drawn from a population distribution $\cD$ and remains fixed thereafter, after which the initial state is drawn as $x_0\sim\mu_c$, and the state at every period $t$ evolves as $x_{t+1}\sim P_c(\cdot\given x_t,a_t)$. The decision maker observes neither $c$ nor the state $x_t$, and receives at period $t$ the observation $y_t=O_c(x_t)$. Consequently, the action at period $t$ can only be based on the history $h_t=(y_0,a_0,\ldots,a_{t-1},y_t)$ of past observations and actions, i.e., a policy $\pi=(\pi_t)_{t\geq0}$ selects $a_t\sim\pi_t(\cdot\given h_t)$, and $\Pi$ denotes a given class of such history-dependent policies. For each context $c$, let $J(\pi;c):=\EE^\pi_c\sbr{\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}r_c(x_t,a_t)}$ denote the expected cumulative reward of $\pi$, where $\EE^\pi_c$ denotes the expectation over the trajectory generated by $\pi$ under $\mu_c$ and $P_c$. We consider
\begin{align}\label{eq:nominal}
\max_{\pi\in\Pi}\ J(\pi;\cD):=\EE_{c\sim\cD}\sbr{J(\pi;c)}.
\end{align}

In medical applications, the context collects the parameters that determine the dynamics of an individual patient, which vary across patients and are not observed by the decision maker. Since the context is hidden, the decision maker must infer it from the history, and an optimal policy for \eqref{eq:nominal} is in general history-dependent \cite{kaelbling1998planning,kwon2021rl}. In \eqref{eq:nominal}, we average the performance over the population of patients, which is the natural goal when a policy is deployed on a new patient without further interaction \cite{ye2023power}. In practice, the population distribution $\cD$ is unknown, and is accessible only through sampled contexts, such as the parameters of patients fitted to clinical data or of virtual patients generated in a simulator. Given contexts $c_1,\ldots,c_N$ drawn independently from $\cD$, we consider the sample average approximation (SAA) \cite{shapiro2021lectures} of \eqref{eq:nominal},
\begin{align}\label{eq:saa}
\max_{\pi\in\Pi}\ \hat J_N(\pi):=\oldfrac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N J(\pi;c_i).
\end{align}
We proceed to show that an optimal solution of \eqref{eq:saa} generalizes to new contexts drawn from $\cD$ once sufficiently many contexts are sampled. To begin with, we establish such a guarantee for finite state, observation, and action spaces.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:saa}
Suppose $\cX$, $\cY$, and $\cA$ are finite, and $0\leq r_c(x,a)\leq\bar r$ for all $(c,x,a)\in\cC\times\cX\times\cA$. Let $|\cH|=\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}|\cY|^{t+1}|\cA|^t$ denote the number of histories $h_t$ with $t\leq T-1$. Then for any class $\Pi$ of history-dependent policies, any $\hat\pi_N\in\argmax_{\pi\in\Pi}\hat J_N(\pi)$, and any $\delta\in(0,1)$, with probability at least $1-\delta$, we have
\begin{align*}
J(\hat\pi_N;\cD)\ \geq\ \sup_{\pi\in\Pi}J(\pi;\cD)-T\bar r\sqrt{\oldfrac{2(|\cA|-1)|\cH|\log\big(1+8T\sqrt N\big)+2\log(2/\delta)}{N}}-\oldfrac{T\bar r}{\sqrt N}.
\end{align*}
\end{theorem}

In view of Theorem~\ref{thm:saa}, the optimality gap of $\hat\pi_N$ on the population is $\cO\big(T\bar r\sqrt{|\cA||\cH|\log(TN)/N}\big)$ with high probability. Consequently, a policy trained on sufficiently many sampled contexts generalizes to new contexts drawn from $\cD$, provided that $\cD$ is broad enough to contain the contexts of interest. In Section~\ref{sec:res-gen}, we demonstrate such generalization for adaptive therapy by applying policies trained on a broad synthetic population to the patients fitted to clinical data in \cite{zhang2022evolution} without further training. The proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:saa} is deferred to Appendix~\ref{app:proof}.

On the other hand, the number $|\cH|$ of histories, and hence the number of sampled contexts required by Theorem~\ref{thm:saa}, grows exponentially in the horizon $T$. We proceed to show that the exponential dependence on $T$ can be avoided when the expected cumulative reward $J(\pi;c)$ is Lipschitz continuous with respect to the context $c$.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:saa-wasserstein}
Suppose that $\cC\subset\RR^d$ is compact with diameter $\operatorname{diam}(\cC)$ under the Euclidean norm, $0\leq r_c(x,a)\leq\bar r$ for all $(c,x,a)\in\cC\times\cX\times\cA$, and there exists $L_T<\infty$ such that
\begin{align}\label{eq:context-lipschitz}
|J(\pi;c)-J(\pi;c')|\leq L_T\|c-c'\|_2\qquad\text{for all }\pi\in\Pi\text{ and }c,c'\in\cC.
\end{align}
Then there exists a constant $C_d$ depending only on $d$ such that for any $\hat\pi_N\in\argmax_{\pi\in\Pi}\hat J_N(\pi)$ and any $\delta\in(0,1)$, with probability at least $1-\delta$, we have
\begin{align*}
J(\hat\pi_N;\cD)\ \geq\ \sup_{\pi\in\Pi}J(\pi;\cD)-2L_T\operatorname{diam}(\cC)\left(\oldfrac{C_d\log(2N)}{N^{\min\{1/2,1/d\}}}+\sqrt{\oldfrac{\log(1/\delta)}{2N}}\right).
\end{align*}
\end{theorem}

In view of Theorem~\ref{thm:saa-wasserstein}, the exponential dependence on $T$ through $|\cH|$ in Theorem~\ref{thm:saa} is replaced by $L_T$, which grows only polynomially in $T$ when the dynamics are Lipschitz continuous with respect to the context. To be more specific, we establish in the following proposition the Lipschitz continuity \eqref{eq:context-lipschitz} for any class of policies, when the observation map does not depend on the context, and the initial distribution, the transition kernel, and the reward are Lipschitz continuous with respect to the context.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:context-lipschitz}
Suppose $\cC\subset\RR^d$, $\cA$ is finite, the observation map $O_c=O$ is independent of $c$, $0\leq r_c(x,a)\leq\bar r$ for all $(c,x,a)\in\cC\times\cX\times\cA$, and there exists a constant $L\geq0$ such that
\begin{align*}
d_{\mathrm{TV}}(\mu_c,\mu_{c'})&\leq L\|c-c'\|_2,\\
d_{\mathrm{TV}}\big(P_c(\cdot\given x,a),P_{c'}(\cdot\given x,a)\big)&\leq L\|c-c'\|_2,\\
|r_c(x,a)-r_{c'}(x,a)|&\leq L\|c-c'\|_2
\end{align*}
for all $c,c'\in\cC$, $x\in\cX$, and $a\in\cA$, where $d_{\mathrm{TV}}(P,Q):=\sup_B|P(B)-Q(B)|$ denotes the total variation distance, and the supremum is taken over all measurable sets $B$. Then \eqref{eq:context-lipschitz} holds for any class $\Pi$ of history-dependent policies with
\begin{align*}
L_T=LT\left(1+\oldfrac{\bar r(T+1)}{2}\right).
\end{align*}
\end{proposition}

Given Proposition~\ref{prop:context-lipschitz}, we have $L_T=\cO\big(LT(1+\bar rT)\big)$, and hence Theorem~\ref{thm:saa-wasserstein} avoids the exponential dependence on $T$ in Theorem~\ref{thm:saa}. It is interesting to note that Theorem~\ref{thm:saa-wasserstein} does not require finite state, observation, and action spaces. On the other hand, the rate $\cO\big(N^{-\min\{1/2,1/d\}}\log N\big)$ deteriorates with the dimension $d$ once $d>2$. Nevertheless, such deterioration is mild for systems with a context of small dimension. For instance, the context of adaptive therapy in Section~\ref{sec:cmdp} is four-dimensional, which yields the rate $\cO(N^{-1/4}\log N)$. The proofs of Theorem~\ref{thm:saa-wasserstein} and Proposition~\ref{prop:context-lipschitz} are deferred to Appendix~\ref{app:proof}.

\subsection{Adaptive Therapy as a Contextual MDP}\label{sec:cmdp}

We adopt the model of abiraterone therapy in \cite{zhang2022evolution}, which describes the competition between drug-sensitive and drug-resistant cancer cells. Let $S(\tau)$ and $R(\tau)$ denote the numbers of drug-sensitive and drug-resistant cells at time $\tau$, measured in days, and let $u(\tau)\in\{0,1\}$ indicate whether abiraterone is administered. The dynamics are given by
\begin{align}\label{eq:lv}
\oldfrac{dS(\tau)}{d\tau}=r_S S(\tau)\left(1-\oldfrac{S(\tau)+R(\tau)}{\kappa\big(1-\lambda u(\tau)\big)}\right),
\qquad
\oldfrac{dR(\tau)}{d\tau}=r_R R(\tau)\left(1-\oldfrac{\alpha S(\tau)+R(\tau)}{\kappa}\right),
\end{align}
where $\kappa$ is the carrying capacity, i.e., the maximal number of cells supported by the available nutrients and space, and $r_S=\beta\gamma_S$ and $r_R=\beta\gamma_R$ are the growth rates of the sensitive and resistant cells per day, with base rates $\gamma_S,\gamma_R$ and a common factor $\beta$ scaling both rates.

In essence, \eqref{eq:lv} describes the different responses of the drug-sensitive and drug-resistant cells to abiraterone and the competition between them, which together underlie the rationale of adaptive therapy. Specifically, the sensitive cells depend on testosterone for survival and proliferation, while the resistant cells are independent of testosterone \cite{zhang2022evolution}. Since the synthesis of testosterone is inhibited by abiraterone, the sensitive cells utilize nutrients and space less efficiently under treatment, which is modeled in \eqref{eq:lv} by a reduction of their carrying capacity by the fraction $\lambda\in(0,1)$. In contrast, the resistant cells are not affected by abiraterone, and their growth is suppressed only through the competition from the sensitive cells, whose competitive effect on the resistant cells is measured by $\alpha$. Consequently, once the sensitive cells are depleted under continuous treatment, the resistant cells are released from competition and eventually dominate the tumor. In adaptive therapy, treatment is instead paused before such depletion occurs, which allows the sensitive cells to recover during the treatment break and to continue suppressing the resistant ones.

Following \cite{zhang2022evolution}, we set $(\lambda,\gamma_S,\gamma_R,\alpha,\beta)=(0.95,\,0.0156/6,\,0.0091/6,\,6,\,8)$, which are estimated by fitting \eqref{eq:lv} to the longitudinal PSA records of 32 patients, namely 17 patients of the pilot clinical trial and 15 patients of a contemporaneous cohort who received continuous treatment with abiraterone as the standard of care. We refer to $(\alpha,\beta)=(6,8)$, which are shared by all fitted patients, as the nominal values of the competition and growth parameters. We also take the initial cell numbers of each patient from the same fit (see Appendix~\ref{app:details}), and set $\kappa=10^4$ as in \cite{zhang2022evolution}, which only determines the unit of the cell numbers, since rescaling $S$, $R$, and $\kappa$ by a common factor leaves \eqref{eq:lv} unchanged. Since $\alpha$ and $\beta$ are estimated from the records of only 32 patients and are assumed to be shared by all of them, their values are subject to considerable statistical error, and may also differ across patients, as suggested in \cite{zhang2022evolution} by the poorer fits for three patients. Hence, in Sections~\ref{sec:uncertainty} and \ref{sec:results}, we allow both parameters to vary, and seek policies that remain reliable under shifts in them.

Since PSA is measured monthly in the pilot clinical trial \cite{zhang2022evolution}, the clinician decides at the beginning of every month of 28 days whether to administer abiraterone until the next visit, i.e., chooses $a_t\in\{0,1\}$ at visit $t\in\{0,1,\ldots,T-1\}$ and sets $u(\tau)=a_t$ for $\tau\in[28t,28(t+1))$. We take $T=65$, i.e., a horizon of approximately five years, which exceeds the median overall survival in both the adaptive therapy cohort and the standard-of-care cohort of \cite{zhang2022evolution}. At visit $t$, the state is given by the cell numbers $x_t=(S_t,R_t):=\big(S(28t),R(28t)\big)$. Since $u(\tau)$ is constant within each month, the state at the next visit is determined by $x_t$ and $a_t$ through the transition
\begin{align}\label{eq:transition}
x_{t+1}=F_{\alpha,\beta}(x_t,a_t):=z(28),\qquad\text{where}\quad\oldfrac{dz}{d\tau}=g_{\alpha,\beta}\big(z(\tau),a_t\big)\ \text{ for }\tau\in[0,28],\quad z(0)=x_t,
\end{align}
and $g_{\alpha,\beta}(x,u)$ denotes the right-hand side of \eqref{eq:lv} evaluated at $\big(S(\tau),R(\tau)\big)=x$ and $u(\tau)=u$, which depends on $\alpha$ through the competition term and on $\beta$ through the growth rates. We compute $F_{\alpha,\beta}$ by the classical fourth-order Runge-Kutta method with step size $1$ on the grid $\tau\in\{0,1,\ldots,28\}$, and truncate $S$ and $R$ to $[0,\kappa]$ after every step (see Appendix~\ref{app:details}).

We now formulate adaptive therapy as an instance of \eqref{eq:nominal}. For each patient, we take as context $c=(S_0,R_0,\alpha,\beta)$ the initial numbers of sensitive and resistant cells together with the competition and growth parameters. Given the context, the initial distribution $\mu_c$ is a point mass at $x_0=(S_0,R_0)$, and the transition kernel $P_c(\cdot\given x,a)$ is a point mass at $F_{\alpha,\beta}(x,a)$ defined in \eqref{eq:transition}. Consequently, the context determines the course of the disease under any sequence of treatment decisions. The clinician, however, observes neither the context nor the cell numbers, and only measures PSA. Indeed, sensitive and resistant cells are assumed in \cite{zhang2022evolution} to produce equal amounts of PSA, in which case PSA is given by the tumor burden $B_t=S_t+R_t$ at visit $t$ multiplied by the amount of PSA produced per cell. Since the amount of PSA produced per cell is unknown, the pretreatment PSA does not reveal the initial cell numbers $(S_0,R_0)$. We therefore take as the observation the logarithm of the ratio of PSA to its pretreatment value, which is given by $B_t/B_0$,
\begin{align}\label{eq:obs}
y_t=\log\left(B_t/B_0\right).
\end{align}

Under the protocol of the pilot clinical trial \cite{zhang2022evolution}, every patient receives abiraterone at enrollment, and treatment is reinitiated whenever PSA returns to or above its pretreatment value. We impose both requirements on every policy as a safety constraint, which we refer to as the forced-treatment rule. In terms of the observation \eqref{eq:obs}, we require $a_t=1$ at $t=0$ and at every visit with $y_t\geq0$, and impose no restriction on $a_t$ at any other visit. Accordingly, the policy class $\Pi$ in \eqref{eq:nominal} consists of the history-dependent policies satisfying this constraint.

We define progression as the first visit $t\geq1$ at which the tumor burden reaches $1.2B_0$, i.e., exceeds its pretreatment value by at least 20\%, which is the threshold adopted in \cite{strobl2021turnover,gallagher2025deriving,browning2026adaptive}. Since treatment can only be adjusted at visits, the tumor burden may reach $1.2B_0$ during a treatment break while the tumor is still partially sensitive to treatment. Such progression is referred to as premature progression in \cite{gallagher2025deriving}, where the threshold for pausing treatment is derived from the regrowth of the tumor between two consecutive visits to prevent it. Given that premature progression exposes the patient to a tumor burden that would be regarded as progression under treatment, we count progression irrespective of whether treatment is administered. In contrast, progression is declared only when the tumor grows under treatment in \cite{browning2026adaptive}, which concerns intermittent androgen suppression instead of abiraterone and adopts the model of \cite{strobl2021turnover} (see Appendix~\ref{app:pstd}). We define the progression-free time $G^\pi(c)\in\{0,1,\ldots,T\}$ as the number of months completed before progression. Correspondingly, we model progression as an absorbing state with zero reward, and set the reward at every visit $t$ before progression to
\begin{align*}
r_c(x_t,a_t)=\II\big\{B_{t+1}<1.2B_0\big\},\qquad\text{where}\quad(S_{t+1},R_{t+1})=F_{\alpha,\beta}(x_t,a_t).
\end{align*}
Hence the cumulative reward is given by $G^\pi(c)$, and $J(\pi;\cD)=\EE_{c\sim\cD}\sbr{\EE^\pi\sbr{G^\pi(c)}}$. It should be noted that AT50, implemented as in Section~\ref{sec:method}, is a feasible solution of \eqref{eq:nominal}, whose two thresholds are fixed in advance rather than obtained by solving \eqref{eq:nominal}. By applying RL to \eqref{eq:nominal}, we instead search over the entire class $\Pi$ for a policy that adapts the treatment to the whole history of PSA measurements and treatment decisions.

\subsection{Robust Contextual MDPs}\label{sec:uncertainty}

In clinical practice, a policy learned by solving the contextual MDP \eqref{eq:nominal} is subject to uncertainty beyond the variation across patients, which we model through the population distribution $\cD$ of contexts. In particular, the treatment decisions are made based on the measured PSA, which is affected by analytical and biological variation \cite{marques2022critical}, and serves only as an indirect measure of the underlying tumor burden even when measured exactly \cite{bryce2017radiographic}. The response of a patient to the treatment may also deviate from the nominal model in Section~\ref{sec:cmdp}. Indeed, the key parameters of the nominal model are estimated from a small cohort of patients and are hence subject to considerable statistical error, while the patient population and the disease dynamics may shift between learning and deployment \cite{finlayson2021clinician}, or even during treatment. For instance, tumors may develop new mechanisms of resistance to abiraterone during treatment \cite{antonarakis2014arv7}. Since the learned policy tailors its decisions to the PSA trajectories generated by the nominal model, its performance may deteriorate substantially in the presence of such uncertainty. In view of this, we seek policies that are robust against both errors in the observation and shifts in the patient dynamics.

To this end, we model the errors in the observation as random, and account for shifts in the patient dynamics through the lens of distributionally robust optimization (DRO) \cite{delage2010distributionally,bental2013robust,esfahani2018data,blanchet2019quantifying,gao2023distributionally,kuhn2025distributionally}, in which one seeks the best worst-case performance over an ambiguity set of distributions. Following the game-theoretic view of distributionally robust MDPs \cite{li2023rectangularity,li2026robust}, we formulate robustness to such shifts as a Markov game between the controller and an adversary acting on the transition kernel.

\subsubsection{Errors in the Observation}\label{sec:obs-robust}

To account for errors in the measured PSA, we model the observation at visit $t$ as
\begin{align}\label{eq:obs-pert}
y_t=\log\left(B_t/B_0\right)+\delta_t,\qquad |\delta_t|\leq\varepsilon,
\end{align}
where $\delta_t$ denotes the error at visit $t$, whose magnitude is bounded by $\varepsilon$, and the exact observation \eqref{eq:obs} corresponds to $\delta_t=0$. Both the policy and the forced-treatment rule act on the perturbed observation, and hence an error may also delay the restart of treatment required by the forced-treatment rule, which we refer to as the forced restart. Since the measured PSA is affected by analytical and within-subject biological variation \cite{marques2022critical}, we model the errors as random, and refer to them as random perturbations. Specifically, every $\delta_t$ is drawn independently from the uniform distribution on $[-\varepsilon,\varepsilon]$. We then learn policies that account for such errors by solving \eqref{eq:nominal} under the observation \eqref{eq:obs-pert}.

\subsubsection{Robustness to Shifts in the Patient Dynamics}\label{sec:kernel-robust}

To account for shifts in the patient dynamics, we allow the transition kernel of each patient to change in every month within an ambiguity set $\cP_c$ of transition kernels. Specifically, an adversary policy $\sigma$ selects a kernel from $\cP_c$ at visit $t$, possibly at random, based on the observations up to visit $t$, and we define $J(\pi,\sigma;\cD)$ as the expected cumulative reward of $\pi$ under the kernels selected by $\sigma$. We consider the robust counterpart of \eqref{eq:nominal} given by the zero-sum game
\begin{align}\label{eq:kernel-robust}
\max_{\pi\in\Pi}\ \min_{\sigma}\ J(\pi,\sigma;\cD).
\end{align}
Since the kernel is selected anew at every visit based on the realized history, \eqref{eq:kernel-robust} follows the dynamic formulation of robust MDPs \cite{li2026robust,li2026computational}, and hence accounts for shifts in the dynamics that occur during treatment.

To construct $\cP_c$ in adaptive therapy, we consider shifts in the competition and growth parameters $\alpha$ and $\beta$, whose estimates are subject to statistical error and may differ across patients (Section~\ref{sec:cmdp}). Following Remark~1 of \cite{li2026robust}, we draw $K$ pairs of multipliers $m^{(k)}=(m^{(k)}_\alpha,m^{(k)}_\beta)$ of $(\alpha,\beta)$, $k=1,\ldots,K$, once from a nominal distribution $\Qnom$ concentrated around one (see Appendix~\ref{app:details}), and keep them fixed. For a patient with context $c$, the $k$-th generating kernel $F^{(k)}_c$ is given by the transition \eqref{eq:transition} with parameters $(m^{(k)}_\alpha\alpha,m^{(k)}_\beta\beta)$, and $\cP_c$ consists of the mixtures of the $K$ generating kernels whose weights lie in
\begin{align}\label{eq:mixture}
\cW_\rho=\left\{(1-\rho)\,\tfrac{1}{K}\mathbf{1}+\rho\,q:\ q\in\Delta_K\right\},\qquad\rho\in[0,1],
\end{align}
where $\Delta_K$ denotes the probability simplex. The radius $\rho$ can be interpreted as the expected fraction of months in which the patient dynamics are selected by the adversary. Indeed, since the expected cumulative reward is linear in the mixture weights at every visit, it suffices for the adversary to place the weight $\rho$ on a single generating kernel at every visit. Equivalently, at every visit the kernel is drawn uniformly from the $K$ generating kernels with probability $1-\rho$, and is selected by the adversary with probability $\rho$.

Clearly, for $\rho=0$, problem \eqref{eq:kernel-robust} reduces to \eqref{eq:nominal} with the parameters redrawn uniformly from the generating kernels in every month, while for $\rho=1$ the adversary selects the kernel at every visit. In Section~\ref{sec:res-kernel}, we refer to the radius used in training as the training radius, denoted by $\rhotr$, and evaluate every policy at a range of radii $\rho$, which we refer to as evaluation radii.

\section{Numerical Experiments}\label{sec:results}

In this section, we demonstrate that the policies learned from the formulations in Section~\ref{sec:formulation} possess the three properties identified in Section~\ref{sec:intro}. After describing the experimental setup in Section~\ref{sec:method}, we show in Section~\ref{sec:res-main} that a policy learned from the contextual MDP \eqref{eq:nominal} substantially improves on AT50 without administering more drug. Such a policy, however, acts on PSA as if it were exact. In Section~\ref{sec:res-obs}, we find that errors in PSA of realistic magnitude substantially reduce the benefit of this policy, while by training under random perturbations we retain the benefit of adaptive therapy under such errors. We then turn to shifts in the patient dynamics, and show in Section~\ref{sec:res-kernel} that by solving the kernel-robust formulation \eqref{eq:kernel-robust}, we attain robustness to such shifts at almost no price in the nominal model, and in Section~\ref{sec:res-cross} that the resulting policy nearly Pareto-dominates the policy learned from \eqref{eq:nominal} and AT50 across all the sources of uncertainty we consider. Finally, in Section~\ref{sec:res-gen}, we attain on the 32 fitted patients a performance comparable to that obtained with a population calibrated to them, by training on a broad population constructed without reference to these patients.

\subsection{Experimental Setup}\label{sec:method}

We train all policies on a calibrated population $\Dcal$ of virtual patients, obtained by multiplying the initial cell numbers of the 32 fitted patients of \cite{zhang2022evolution} by independent lognormal factors, with the competition and growth parameters kept at their nominal values $(\alpha,\beta)=(6,8)$. For evaluation, we consider two test populations, namely the virtual test population of 1{,}000 patients drawn from $\Dcal$ independently of the training patients, and the 32 fitted patients, whose empirical distribution we denote by $\Dfit$. All methods are evaluated on the same test patients with common random numbers. The construction of $\Dcal$ and the evaluation protocol are given in Appendix~\ref{app:details}.

All learned policies are parameterized by a gated recurrent unit (GRU) network \cite{cho2014learning} acting on features of the history $h_t$, and trained by proximal policy optimization (PPO) \cite{schulman2017proximal} for 2{,}000 updates, each with 512 patients newly sampled from the training population. For each type of policy, we train three policies with different random seeds, and the remaining details are given in Appendix~\ref{app:details}.

We compare the learned policies with two clinical rules, namely continuous treatment, i.e., $a_t=1$ for all $t$, and AT50. In our model, we implement AT50 as the policy that pauses treatment once $y_t<\log0.5$, resumes it once $y_t\geq0$, and otherwise repeats its previous action. Both satisfy the forced-treatment rule.

Since we impose no explicit penalty in \eqref{eq:nominal} on patients who fare worse than under the standard of care, we monitor such patients separately. To this end, we compare the progression-free time $G^\pi(c)$ of each patient with the progression-free time $G^\on(c)$ of the same patient under continuous treatment. We say that $\pi$ harms the patient if $G^\pi(c)<G^\on(c)-3$, and that it benefits the patient if $G^\pi(c)>G^\on(c)+3$, where the margin of three months corresponds to the interval of 12 weeks between two consecutive imaging assessments in the pilot clinical trial \cite{zhang2022evolution}. We refer to the fractions of patients harmed and benefited as the harm rate and the benefit rate, and report them together with the mean progression-free time $\bar G$. For the learned policies, all values are averaged over the three seeds, with per-seed values in Appendix~\ref{app:results}.

