Nominal RL substantially outperforms AT50. Specifically, it attains a mean progression-free time of 50.35 months on the virtual test population, compared with 29.58 months for AT50 and 15.81 months for continuous treatment. Its gain over continuous treatment is 34.54 months, more than twice the gain of 13.77 months attained by AT50. In addition, nominal RL harms 0.2\% of the patients and benefits 91.9\%, compared with 28.4\% and 54.0\% for AT50. On the 32 fitted patients, nominal RL attains 48.43 months, compared with 25.34 months for AT50, and harms none of the fitted patients.

A few remarks are in order. First, the improvement is attained without administering more drug. Nominal RL treats in 30.3\% of the months and AT50 in 28.7\%, while nominal RL divides its treated months into 12.9 courses on average, compared with 6.2 for AT50. Hence nominal RL alternates between treatment and breaks roughly twice as often as AT50, with correspondingly shorter courses and breaks. Second, the improvement holds across patients with different initial resistance (Figure~\ref{fig:tertiles}), a factor that correlates with the time to radiographic progression in both the adaptive therapy cohort and the standard-of-care cohort of \cite{zhang2022evolution}. When we split the virtual test population into tertiles of the initial resistant fraction $R_0/B_0$, nominal RL attains 60.81 months in the lowest tertile and 35.57 months in the highest, compared with 37.28 and 19.93 months for AT50 (Table~\ref{tab:tertiles}). AT50 mainly harms patients with few resistant cells, with a harm rate of 39.9\% in the lowest tertile compared with 10.8\% in the highest, while the harm rate of nominal RL is at most 0.4\% in every tertile. Third, the definition of progression in Section~\ref{sec:cmdp} is essential for an informative comparison. If we declare progression only when the tumor grows under treatment as in \cite{browning2026adaptive}, the growth of the tumor during a break is never penalized, and the rule that withholds treatment whenever permitted attains 53.79 months, the same value as all three seeds of nominal RL trained under this definition, compared with 52.60 months for AT50 (Appendix~\ref{app:pstd}). Consequently, such a definition does not allow us to assess the benefit of adapting the treatment to PSA.

In summary, by learning from data we obtain a policy that substantially improves on AT50 for every level of initial resistance without administering more drug. In addition, since the virtual test patients are drawn independently of the training patients, nominal RL generalizes to new patients from the training population, in line with Theorem~\ref{thm:saa}.

\subsection{Retaining the Benefit of Adaptive Therapy under Errors in PSA}\label{sec:res-obs}

Nominal RL acts on PSA as if it were exact. In clinical practice, however, PSA is subject to measurement errors. We therefore evaluate nominal RL under the random perturbations of Section~\ref{sec:obs-robust} with $\varepsilon=0.2$, which is comparable to the reference change value of about 19\% for total PSA, i.e., the smallest relative change between two consecutive measurements that can be distinguished from analytical and within-subject biological variation \cite{marques2022critical}. We also refer to the policy learned from \eqref{eq:nominal} under the same perturbations as random-perturbation RL, and compare it with nominal RL and the two clinical rules in Figure~\ref{fig:obs}.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics{obs_errors.pdf}
\caption{Policies on the virtual test population in the nominal model and under random perturbations of PSA with $\varepsilon=0.2$. (a) Mean progression-free time. (b) Harm rate. See Table~\ref{tab:obs} of Appendix~\ref{app:results} for the values.}
\label{fig:obs}
\end{figure}

We observe that errors in PSA with $\varepsilon=0.2$ substantially reduce the benefit of the policies that do not account for them. Under random perturbations, nominal RL drops from 50.35 to 32.96 months, and its harm rate increases from 0.2\% to 14.4\%. Similarly, AT50 drops from 29.58 to 16.46 months, only marginally above the 15.81 months of continuous treatment, and its harm rate increases from 28.4\% to 43.8\%. In contrast, random-perturbation RL retains the benefit of adaptive therapy under such errors. Under random perturbations, it attains 44.49 months with a harm rate of 2.0\%, close to the 45.94 months it attains without perturbation.

A few remarks are in order. First, under perturbations with $\varepsilon=0.2$, we can no longer ensure through the forced-treatment rule that treatment is restarted before progression. Indeed, since $\varepsilon>\log1.2$, an error that lowers the measured PSA can postpone the forced restart until after progression. Since AT50 resumes treatment only through the condition $y_t\geq0$, such an error can postpone its restart in every treatment break. Second, we pay a price in the nominal model for accounting for errors in PSA, with 45.94 months for random-perturbation RL compared with 50.35 months for nominal RL.

In summary, policies that do not account for errors in PSA lose a substantial part of their benefit under errors of realistic magnitude, and by training under random perturbations we retain the benefit of adaptive therapy under such errors.

\subsection{Robustness to Shifts in the Patient Dynamics at Almost No Price}\label{sec:res-kernel}

We next turn to shifts in the patient dynamics, which arise when the competition and growth parameters deviate from their estimated values. We model such shifts by the ambiguity set $\cP_c$ of Section~\ref{sec:kernel-robust} with $K=32$ generating kernels drawn from $\Qnom$, and refer to the policy learned from \eqref{eq:kernel-robust} with training radius $\rhotr\in\{0.1,0.25,0.5\}$ as kernel-robust RL.

We evaluate all policies at the evaluation radii $\rho\in\{0,0.1,0.25,0.5,1\}$. At $\rho=0$, the kernel is drawn uniformly from the generating kernels in each month, and for $\rho>0$, each policy is evaluated against a best-response adversary, i.e., an adversary trained by PPO to minimize the reward of the policy while the policy is held fixed. In addition to the 32 generating kernels used in training, referred to as the training kernels, we evaluate the policies on 32 held-out kernels drawn independently from $\Qnom$. Figure~\ref{fig:kernel} shows the mean progression-free time and the harm rate in the nominal model and as functions of the evaluation radius, with the complete values in Table~\ref{tab:dyn-virtual}.

