Time Series Language Models (TSLMs) seek to extend the reasoning and interaction capabilities of large language models to sequential data, enabling models to answer questions, infer temporal relationships, forecast future behavior, and support decisions over time series~\citep{xie2025chatts, langer2026opentslm, guan2026timeomni, yu2026tsrbench}. A central challenge in this emerging paradigm is how temporal signals should be represented to the language model. Existing approaches serialize numerical values into text, learn dedicated time-series embeddings, or project features from specialized temporal encoders into the language-model representation space~\citep{xie2025chatts, langer2026opentslm, guan2026timeomni}. As summarized in Table~\ref{tab:tslm_comparison}, these approaches typically commit to a single time-series representation, with absolute measurement scale either retained directly or provided separately as auxiliary metadata. Yet these representational choices impose fundamentally different inductive biases: representations that retain absolute amplitudes and coordinate information support quantitative readout, whereas normalized representations can suppress such information while emphasizing relative structure and temporal dynamics. Recent evaluations further show that simply rendering a time series as a line plot can already provide strong temporal understanding to general-purpose vision-language models~\citep{yu2026tsrbench, cai2024timeseriesexam}, suggesting that representation---not only model scale or training strategy---is a key determinant of time-series reasoning ability. This motivates a more fundamental question:
    \textit{Can complementary representations improve
general-purpose time-series reasoning?}



We hypothesize that general-purpose time-series reasoning benefits from exposing the model to \emph{complementary views} of the same signal, each preserving different information and inducing different invariances. Rather than requiring a single representation to simultaneously retain all information relevant to downstream reasoning while suppressing nuisance variation, this formulation allows distinct views to provide different inductive biases. We instantiate this principle with two visual representations: a standard line plot and a normalized delay embedding. The line plot \emph{preserves} absolute amplitudes, axis ranges, and measurement scales, providing the information required for questions grounded in observed values. In contrast, delay embeddings reconstruct a time series as a trajectory in phase space using time-lagged observations, revealing geometric structure associated with the underlying dynamics \citep{takens2006detecting}. We normalize this representation before rendering it as a two-dimensional view, thereby \emph{factoring out} affine changes in amplitude while retaining the geometry of the temporal trajectory. Our central hypothesis is that combining these complementary views is particularly useful beyond basic perception, where reasoning, prediction, and decision-making require models to exploit temporal structure while remaining grounded in the observed measurements.


Building on this representational principle, we design a dual-tower TSLM in which each visual view is processed by a dedicated encoder. We retain Qwen3-VL's native vision encoder, initialized from SigLIP-2 \citep{tschannen2025siglip}, to process line plots, and introduce a dedicated DINOv3 encoder \citep{simeoni2025dinov3} for delay embeddings, allowing each view to develop a specialized visual representation before interacting with the language model. The resulting visual features are compressed into a compact set of tokens, 230 for the line plot and 64 for the delay embedding, and concatenated as input to the language model.
Beyond this input-level fusion, we adopt DeepStack \citep{meng2024deepstack} to inject multi-level visual features into the early language-model layers, enabling the complementary representations to interact with language processing throughout the network. 
This architecture provides a compact realization of our central hypothesis while retaining the distinct inductive biases of the two visual views.


To test our representational hypothesis and evaluate the resulting dual-tower TSLM, we conduct experiments on TSRBench~\citep{yu2026tsrbench}, TSExam~\citep{cai2024timeseriesexam}, and ChatTS~\citep{xie2025chatts}. Built on Qwen3-VL-8B~\citep{bai2025qwen3}, our model achieves 53.2\% overall accuracy on TSRBench, substantially narrowing the gap to GPT-5 with text and visual input (55.6\%) while surpassing the strongest evaluated dedicated TSLM (36.7\%). It also outperforms the compared general-purpose and specialized baselines on TSExam and ChatTS numerical alignment. To assess the contribution of the complementary view, we compare against a plot-only variant trained under the same setup. Adding the delay embedding raises overall TSRBench accuracy from 45.2\% to 53.2\%, an 8.0-percentage-point improvement, with gains in reasoning, prediction, and decision-making while leaving perception nearly unchanged. Controlled evaluation-time interventions further reveal distinct invariance profiles: the delay-only model maintains nearly unchanged accuracy under positive rescaling and offset shifts, whereas the plot-only model, whose representation retains the measurement frame, is substantially more sensitive to these transformations. Our contributions are threefold:


\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Complementary representations for time-series reasoning.}
    We establish complementary representation as a design principle for TSLMs, motivated by the observation that quantitative readout requires the original measurement frame, whereas reasoning about temporal dynamics benefits from factoring it out.

    \item \textbf{A dual-view time-series language model.}
    We propose a dual-tower architecture that combines line plots and normalized delay embeddings within a shared language model. Dedicated visual encoders, compact visual tokens, and multi-level feature fusion integrate temporal structure with the original measurement frame.

    \item \textbf{State-of-the-art TSLM performance.}
    With an 8B backbone, our model outperforms all evaluated dedicated TSLMs on TSRBench, achieving 53.2\% accuracy versus 36.7\% for the strongest baseline. It also leads the evaluated models on TSExam and multivariate ChatTS numerical alignment, achieving 89.9\% and 86.8\% accuracy, respectively.
\end{itemize}


