\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{Time-Series Representations.}

Time series admit multiple representations that expose different properties of the underlying signal. Beyond the raw sequential domain, classical approaches transform temporal observations into alternative coordinate systems, including frequency and time-frequency representations, recurrence-based representations, and delay embeddings that reconstruct trajectories in phase space~\citep{takens2006detecting}. A related line of work represents time series as images, enabling spatial models to operate on temporal structure. \cite{wang2015imaging} introduced Gramian Angular Fields and Markov Transition Fields for encoding temporal sequences as two-dimensional images, demonstrating their utility for classification and imputation. More recently, \cite{naiman2024utilizing} showed that image transformations based on delay embeddings and short-time Fourier transforms can provide effective interfaces between time series and modern vision-based diffusion models, supporting generation across both short and long sequences. Collectively, these works demonstrate that alternative representations can expose useful and distinct structure in temporal signals. Our work builds on this perspective but shifts the focus from selecting a representation for a particular predictive or generative task to \emph{combining complementary representations for general-purpose time-series reasoning}: we jointly expose the model to views designed to retain different information and induce different invariances.

\paragraph{Time-Series Language Models.}

A growing body of work investigates how pretrained language models can be adapted to temporal data. Early approaches primarily focused on forecasting, either by serializing numerical observations as text~\citep{gruver2023large}, prompting language models with textual descriptions of temporal sequences~\citep{xue2023promptcast}, or aligning and reprogramming time-series representations to exploit pretrained language-model representations~\citep{zhou2023onefitsall, sun2023test, jin2024timellm}. More recent work has broadened this paradigm from task-specific prediction toward \emph{Time Series Language Models} that accept time series as a modality and support natural-language understanding and reasoning. ChatTS~\citep{xie2025chatts} aligns learned time-series embeddings with an LLM through instruction tuning, while OpenTSLM~\citep{langer2026opentslm} investigates both soft-prompt and cross-attention interfaces for reasoning over multivariate temporal signals. TimeOmni-1~\citep{guan2026timeomni} instead represents numerical sequences directly within the language-model token space and targets complex temporal reasoning, whereas TS-Reasoner~\citep{yu2026tsreasoner} aligns representations from a pretrained time-series foundation model with an LLM. In parallel, benchmark studies have shown that general-purpose vision-language models can reason surprisingly effectively over time series presented as line plots~\citep{cai2024timeseriesexam,yu2026tsrbench}, providing an alternative to explicitly learned time-series interfaces. Despite these diverse strategies, existing TSLMs predominantly expose the language model to a single primary representation of each temporal signal. Our work instead treats representation as a source of complementary inductive biases, jointly providing views that deliberately preserve different information and exhibit different invariances.


\section{Method}


\subsection{Problem Formulation and Overview}
\label{sec:problem_formulation}

Let $Q$ denote a natural-language query and
$\mathcal{X}=\{\mathbf{x}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{x}_n\}$ a collection of $n$ input time series, where
$\mathbf{x}_i=(x_{i,1},\ldots,x_{i,m_i})\in\mathbb{R}^{m_i}$ may have a different length and numerical range. Given $(Q,\mathcal{X})$, a Time Series
Language Model (TSLM) generates a natural-language answer $A=(a_1,\ldots,a_{|A|})$ autoregressively:
\begin{equation}
P(A\mid Q,\mathcal{X}) = \prod_{j=1}^{|A|}
P(a_j\mid a_{<j},Q,\mathcal{X}) \ .
\end{equation}
Rather than encoding each $\mathbf{x}_i$ through a single representation, we construct two complementary visual views, $\mathcal{V}(\mathbf{x}_i)=\{V_i^{\mathrm{plot}},V_i^{\mathrm{delay}}\}$. The line-plot view $V_i^{\mathrm{plot}}$ retains the original numerical scale and measurement frame, whereas the normalized delay view $V_i^{\mathrm{delay}}$ factors out affine amplitude variation and represents the signal through its local temporal trajectories. As illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:model_architecture}, the two views are processed by dedicated visual encoders and projected into a shared language-model representation space. Their visual tokens are jointly provided to a single Qwen3-VL-8B language-model backbone, with multi-level visual features additionally injected through DeepStack~\citep{meng2024deepstack}. The model is trained in two stages, first aligning the delay-embedding stream with the language model and subsequently instruction-tuning the language model for time-series reasoning.


