\subsection{Complementary Visual Representations}
\label{sec:representations}

Our representation design exposes each time series through two complementary views with different information-preserving properties. The first retains the original measurement frame through a standard line plot, while the second represents the series through overlapping temporal windows after removing its absolute amplitude and offset.


\paragraph{Line-Plot View.} For each time series
$\mathbf{x}_i=(x_{i,1},\ldots,x_{i,m_i})$, we render the raw observations as a two-dimensional line plot without amplitude normalization (Figure~\ref{fig:line_plot_view}). Consequently, the plot retains the original value range and its position within the coordinate system, including the numerical values displayed along the vertical axis. This representation therefore preserves information about absolute amplitudes and differences in scale that would be removed by per-series normalization. For multivariate inputs, each series is rendered as its own plot rather than overlaid, so series with different ranges keep their own measurement frames.


\paragraph{Normalized Delay-Embedding View.}
Delay embeddings~\citep{takens2006detecting} describe a time series through its local histories: overlapping windows, each shifted slightly forward in time, are stacked side by side into an image~\citep{naiman2024utilizing}. Because windows overlap, each feature of the signal traces a diagonal line across the image: periodicity appears as parallel stripes, trends as smooth gradients, and local events as isolated streaks (Figure~\ref{fig:delay_view}). Temporal structure thus becomes visual texture that pretrained vision encoders can readily perceive.

Concretely, for each variate $\mathbf{x}$ of length $m$, we extract $W$ overlapping windows of length $\ell$ with evenly spaced starts covering the whole series. The $c$-th window is the array starting at index $p_c = \operatorname{round}\!\big(c\,\tfrac{m-\ell}{W-1}\big)$:
\begin{equation}
V_c = \big(x_{p_c},\, x_{p_c+1},\, \ldots,\, x_{p_c+\ell-1}\big), \qquad c=0,\ldots,W-1 ,
\label{eq:delay_map}
\end{equation}
where $\ell=\min(\max(\lfloor\rho m\rfloor,\ell_{\min}),\ell_{\max})$ is a fixed fraction $\rho$ of the series length, so windows cover a comparable portion of short and long signals, with bounds $\ell_{\min}$ and $\ell_{\max}$ guarding against extreme lengths. For $m<\ell_{\min}$, we set $\ell=m$. Each window is linearly interpolated to $H$ values, and the $W$ resampled windows form the columns of the min--max normalized delay map $V^{\mathrm{delay}}\in[0,1]^{H\times W}$. Every variate, regardless of its length, is thus represented by an image of the same size (hyperparameters in Appendix~\ref{app:delay_details}).

Since resampling is linear and the map is min--max normalized, any transformation $a\mathbf{x}+b$ with $a>0$ yields the same delay map. The line plot therefore retains the measurement frame, whereas the delay map exposes temporal structure independently of positive affine changes in amplitude.


\subsection{Dual-View Architecture}
\label{sec:architecture}

Our architecture integrates the two views into a single Qwen3-VL-8B language-model backbone~\citep{bai2025qwen3} through dedicated visual encoders (Figure~\ref{fig:model_architecture}).

\paragraph{Line-Plot Encoder.} Line plots are processed by the native Qwen3-VL vision pipeline without modification, which merges spatial features over $2\times2$ neighboring patches and projects them to the language-model hidden dimension. This yields $230$ plot tokens $\mathbf{Z}_i^{\mathrm{plot}}$ per series, together with three intermediate feature maps for DeepStack fusion. The encoder remains frozen throughout training.

\paragraph{Delay-Embedding Encoder.} For each time-series variate, we process its normalized delay map $V^{\mathrm{delay}}$ with a dedicated DINOv3~\citep{simeoni2025dinov3} ViT-L/16 encoder denoted by $E_{\mathrm{delay}}$. Its spatial patch features are merged in $2\times2$ groups and mapped to the language-model hidden dimension by a two-layer projection module $M^{(0)}$, yielding $64$ delay tokens $\mathbf{Z}^{\mathrm{delay}} = M^{(0)}\big(E_{\mathrm{delay}}(V^{\mathrm{delay}})\big)$ per variate. To match the multi-level DeepStack interface of the plot stream, we additionally extract intermediate features $E^{(k_i)}_{\mathrm{delay}}(V^{\mathrm{delay}})$ after blocks $k_i$, where $(k_1,k_2,k_3)=(6,12,18)$, and merge and project each with a dedicated module $M^{(i)}$ of the same form as $M^{(0)}$, giving multi-level delay features $\mathbf{D}_i = M^{(i)}\big(E^{(k_i)}_{\mathrm{delay}}(V^{\mathrm{delay}})\big)$, $i\in\{1,2,3\}$, aligned one-to-one with the $64$ delay tokens and injected into the language model as described below.


\paragraph{Dual-View Fusion.} Both streams enter the language model in the same way. For each time series, $\mathbf{Z}_i^{\mathrm{plot}}$ and $\mathbf{Z}_i^{\mathrm{delay}}$ occupy separate visual spans, each framed by \texttt{<|vision\_start|>} and \texttt{<|vision\_end|>} tokens, alongside the textual query $Q$, so the two views interact within a single autoregressive language model rather than through separate heads or an ensemble. Beyond this input-level fusion, we extend DeepStack to the delay stream. Following the DeepStack mechanism of Qwen3-VL~\citep{meng2024deepstack, bai2025qwen3}, the three multi-level features of each stream are added directly to the hidden states of the first three language-model layers, one feature per layer, at the positions of that stream's own visual tokens. The plot and delay streams thus inject into the same layers but at disjoint token positions. Rendering and tokenization details are provided in Appendix~\ref{app:implementation}.



\subsection{Training}
\label{sec:training}


Following the staged training paradigm common in multimodal language models and recent TSLMs~\citep{xie2025chatts, langer2026opentslm}, we train our model, initialized from Qwen3-VL-8B~\citep{bai2025qwen3}, in two stages that separate the alignment of the new delay stream from task-level instruction tuning. In \textbf{Stage~I (modality alignment)}, the Qwen3-VL vision encoder and the language model remain frozen, and we train LoRA adapters~\citep{hu2022lora} on DINOv3 together with the projection $M^{(0)}$, which maps the final DINOv3 patch features to delay tokens, and the projections $\{M^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{3}$, which map the intermediate DINOv3 features to the multi-level DeepStack features. This stage is trained on captioning data, encouraging the delay stream to produce representations that the frozen language model can interpret. In \textbf{Stage~II (instruction tuning)}, the DINOv3 adapters are merged, the entire visual pathway is frozen, and LoRA is applied to the language model, which is trained on time-series question answering to reason jointly over both views. Both stages use the standard next-token prediction loss computed over the answer tokens only. The objective and hyperparameters are given in Appendix~\ref{app:training_details}. 



\section{Training Data}
\label{sec:training_data}


We construct a dedicated training corpus for each of the two stages described in Section~\ref{sec:training}. Stage~I uses caption supervision to align the new delay-embedding stream with the language model, whereas Stage~II uses a heterogeneous instruction corpus to train perception, numerical readout, forecasting, and temporal reasoning. Most time series are generated procedurally, with labels obtained directly from generator parameters or exact functions of the realized signal rather than from a teacher model. The only real-world time-series sources are CaTS-Bench~\citep{zhou-etal-2026-cats} in Stage~I and a small subset of sports win-probability series from GAMETime~\citep{tan2025inferring} used for abductive reasoning in Stage~II. Table~\ref{tab:training_data} summarizes the data composition.


