\paragraph{Stage I: Caption Alignment.} The alignment corpus contains 55,696 examples from three sources. CaTS-Bench~\citep{zhou-etal-2026-cats} contributes real-world short time series from diverse physical and socioeconomic domains together with reference descriptions. To provide broader attribute coverage, we additionally construct 32,000 ChatTS-style captions from the ChatTS time-series generator~\citep{xie2025chatts}. Trend, seasonality, noise, and local events are sampled from the generator's attribute space, and captions are rendered directly from those attributes. Finally, we construct 7,701 structural captions by generating series with our TimeSeriesExam fork~\citep{cai2024timeseriesexam} and deterministically rendering one-sentence descriptions from the underlying generative components, parameters, noise processes, and injected anomalies. The three sources are
sampled with equal probability during Stage~I, balancing real-world diversity, free-form synthetic descriptions, and tightly controlled constructive
supervision.

\paragraph{Stage II: Instruction Tuning.} The instruction corpus contains 182,815 examples spanning complementary task families. We include the released ChatTS \texttt{ift} and \texttt{sft} training sets~\citep{xie2025chatts} for open-ended univariate and multivariate question answering. Using our TimeSeriesExam fork, we further generate the TSExam perception set, 20,640 multiple-choice questions covering pattern recognition, anomaly detection, similarity, noise, and causality, together with an 11,000-example numerical corpus of open-ended questions whose answers are exact statistics or dynamical quantities computed from the
realized series. The largest component is an in-house 100,000-example reasoning corpus designed to develop broad reasoning capabilities over time series. It spans numerical, deductive, inductive, abductive, causal, etiological, temporal-relation, forecasting, and event-prediction tasks, with examples generated from procedural rules, dynamical systems, and domain-specific simulators. Because instruction-tuned models are sensitive to the task interface, we align the
mixture of reasoning tasks and their question--answer formats with the capabilities evaluated by TSRBench~\citep{yu2026tsrbench}; the underlying
time series, questions, and answers are generated independently and no benchmark instances are used for training. For tasks requiring explicit derivation, supervision additionally includes deterministic rationales constructed from the simulator state, while the remaining tasks use direct multiple-choice supervision.

\paragraph{Gold-by-Construction Supervision and Benchmark Separation.}
For all in-house synthetic data, supervision is derived from known generative
parameters or exact computations on the realized signal. Captions describe
the sampled components, multiple-choice labels follow directly from the
generating mechanism, numerical targets are exact signal reductions, and
reasoning rationales are rendered from structured intermediate facts. This
design avoids using an external language model to infer labels for the
synthetic data. Importantly, no TSRBench or TimeSeriesExam evaluation examples
are included in training.
The TSExam perception set, structural captions, and numerical corpus are generated with our fork of the public generator and contain freshly sampled series whose lengths differ from those in the benchmark.
Similarly, although the reasoning corpus covers the reasoning task families
and question--answer formats evaluated by TSRBench, all training instances
are generated independently and no benchmark examples are used for training.
We additionally verified the reasoning corpus against the benchmark for exact
series overlap, shared sports-game instances, and exact question--answer
matches. ChatTS evaluation data are likewise excluded; only the released
training splits are used. Additional generator details, per-task statistics,
target formats, and held-out splits are provided in
Appendix~\ref{app:data}.





\section{Experiments and Results}
\label{sec:experiments}


\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:benchmarks}

We evaluate on three benchmarks that together probe time-series understanding from low-level perception to high-level reasoning. \textbf{TSRBench} \citep{yu2026tsrbench}, our primary benchmark, comprises 4,125 multiple-choice questions over 15 tasks spanning Perception, Reasoning, Prediction, and Decision-Making. \textbf{TSExam} \citep{cai2024timeseriesexam} contains 746 multiple-choice questions on pattern recognition, noise understanding, anomaly detection, similarity, and causality, and \textbf{ChatTS} \citep{xie2025chatts} measures both fine-grained categorical and numerical alignment and higher-level reasoning over univariate and multivariate series. We compare against two families of models. General-purpose vision-language models receive the series as line plots: Qwen3-VL-8B~\citep{bai2025qwen3}, our own backbone, isolates the effect of our design and training, while GPT-5 serves as a substantially larger proprietary reference rather than a size-matched competitor. We further compare against representative dedicated TSLMs: ChatTS-14B~\citep{xie2025chatts}, TS-Reasoner-7B~\citep{yu2026tsreasoner}, and TimeOmni-1-7B~\citep{guan2026timeomni}. Table~\ref{tab:tsrbench_main} reports task-level TSRBench accuracy together with our representation ablations, 
and Figure~\ref{fig:cross_benchmark_summary} summarizes performance across all three benchmarks.
Complete task-level results and evaluation procedures, including the source of each baseline result, are provided in Appendix~\ref{app:full_results}.


