\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Generalization}
\label{sec:cross_benchmark}

Finally, we evaluate whether the gains observed on TSRBench transfer to benchmarks emphasizing different aspects of time-series understanding. Figure~\ref{fig:cross_benchmark_summary} summarizes overall TSExam accuracy, categorical and numerical alignment on the univariate (A) and multivariate (B) ChatTS datasets, and TSRBench overall accuracy; per-task results and ChatTS reasoning evaluations are in Appendix~\ref{app:full_results}.
Our model attains 89.9\% accuracy on TSExam, exceeding the compared general-purpose and dedicated TSLM baselines. On ChatTS, it achieves 80.5\% numerical accuracy on univariate sequences and 86.8\% on multivariate sequences, with the largest advantage appearing in the multivariate setting. Together with the TSRBench results, these evaluations indicate that the benefit of the dual representation is not confined to a single benchmark: the model remains strong across perceptual, numerical, and broader temporal reasoning tasks, despite using a Qwen3-VL-8B backbone.





\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

Time-series reasoning requires both numerical readout and understanding of temporal dynamics, yet these capabilities favor different representational properties. This tension motivated our central question: can complementary representations improve general-purpose time-series reasoning? We establish complementary representation as a design principle for TSLMs and instantiate it in a dual-view model that integrates measurement-preserving line plots and normalized delay embeddings through dedicated visual encoders and a shared language model. The resulting model raises TSRBench accuracy from 45.2\% for the plot-only variant to 53.2\%, surpassing all evaluated dedicated TSLMs. The gains are concentrated beyond basic perception and are not reproduced when both encoders receive line plots or both receive delay embeddings. The model also leads the evaluated baselines on TSExam and multivariate ChatTS numerical alignment, achieving 89.9\% and 86.8\% accuracy, respectively, demonstrating strong performance across perceptual, numerical, and broader temporal reasoning tasks.

The STFT comparison in Appendix \ref{app:alternative_representation} further shows that the preferred secondary representation varies across evaluations: delay embeddings perform better on TSRBench, while STFT provides small advantages on ChatTS numerical alignment and average reasoning accuracy. This suggests that representation choice should reflect the capabilities a model must support. The intervention analysis likewise distinguishes access to an invariant view from invariance of the combined model, which retains both normalized structure and absolute measurements. Future work could explore additional representations and encoder combinations, including more than two views and query-dependent mechanisms for combining them.



