\begin{abstract}
Large language models (LLMs) can generate differential diagnoses for complex cases, but a useful second opinion must also be factually grounded, clinically coherent, actionable, and safe. We developed a multidimensional benchmark using 300 diagnostically resolved NEJM--MedQA cases: 110 for physician reference ratings and Judge calibration and 190 for held-out evaluation. Eight single-model pipelines generated independent role-specific opinions and interactive-consilium reports under free-form and structured clinical-reasoning conditions. A physician-calibrated LLM Judge evaluated 3,040 final reports and the corresponding structured primary-role outputs. Model identity separated report quality more strongly than response format. Structured final reports had higher overall mean scores in seven of eight model pipelines, with their clearest advantage in management (2.740 vs.\ 2.436). Compared with the structured pre-consilium primary-role opinion, the complete structured synthesis had higher pooled scores across all five shared criteria, with the largest increases in problem prioritization, factual grounding, and reasoning coherence. GPT-5.6-Sol led every evaluated component and reached consensus in the fewest review rounds. These findings show that the benchmark can distinguish model capability, the contribution of multidisciplinary synthesis, criterion-specific effects of response structure, deliberation dynamics, and inference requirements within one common-case evaluation.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{large language models \and clinical reasoning \and second opinion \and multidisciplinary consultation \and LLM-as-a-judge}

\section{Introduction}
Large language models have already demonstrated substantial progress on complex diagnostic tasks. GPT-4 generated clinically meaningful differential diagnoses for cases from the \emph{Case Records of the Massachusetts General Hospital}, a long-running clinicopathological-conference series published in the \emph{New England Journal of Medicine} (NEJM) \citep{brinkmann2024caserecords,kanjee2023gpt4}. We refer to cases from this series as ``NEJM cases.'' Subsequent studies reported strong performance on challenging clinical cases across multiple specialties \citep{rutledge2024diagnostic,hirosawa2024generative}. In the study by McDuff et al., the Articulate Medical Intelligence Explorer (AMIE) system was evaluated on 302 NEJM cases and demonstrated strong performance both when independently generating differential diagnoses and when used by physicians as a decision-support tool \citep{mcduff2025amie}. These findings suggest that contemporary LLMs are already capable of making a meaningful contribution to diagnostic reasoning in complex clinical cases.

However, a clinical second opinion is not reducible to generating a differential diagnosis or selecting the most likely diagnostic hypothesis. A second opinion may change the representation of the patient's condition, identify missing or contradictory information, reprioritize diagnostic hypotheses, alter the diagnostic workup or treatment strategy, and highlight previously underestimated risks. Studies by Payne et al. and Meyer et al. have shown that repeat expert review may lead to changes not only in diagnosis but also in treatment, with management changes in some settings occurring more frequently than diagnostic changes; moreover, it is not always possible to determine objectively which of the two opinions was more accurate \citep{payne2014secondopinion,meyer2015secondopinion}. Thus, for LLMs, high diagnostic accuracy alone does not imply a high-quality clinical second opinion: diagnostic agreement does not fully capture factual grounding, the coherence of reasoning, the quality of diagnostic and therapeutic planning, uncertainty, or clinical safety.

One approach to improving LLM performance is to explicitly structure the reasoning process. In radiology tasks, separating the structuring of clinical information from subsequent diagnosis generation improved accuracy compared with conventional zero-shot chain-of-thought prompting \citep{sonoda2025structured}. A two-stage diagnostic framework in which a distinct verification stage was introduced between the preliminary and final diagnoses also produced moderate, model-dependent improvements on MedQA and NEJM cases, while increasing answer consistency and reducing the frequency of several reasoning errors \citep{shao2025verification}. However, the evidence remains heterogeneous. Prompts designed to imitate intuitive, analytical, Bayesian, or differential-diagnostic styles of physician reasoning did not produce consistent accuracy gains, particularly for stronger models \citep{savage2024reasoning}. More generally, the presence of an elaborate textual rationale should not be treated as evidence that the internal process leading to the answer was itself correct: chain-of-thought explanations can retrospectively rationalize decisions that were in fact driven by information not acknowledged in the explanation \citep{turpin2023unfaithful}. Structured clinical reasoning should therefore be evaluated as a distinct empirical intervention rather than assumed to be superior merely because the response is longer or appears more ``physician-like.''

A second approach is to distribute analysis across multiple agents representing different medical specialties. Its natural clinical analogue is the multidisciplinary team (MDT), in which specialists from different disciplines contribute complementary information to diagnosis and treatment planning. Studies of real-world MDTs, particularly in oncology, suggest that team discussion can alter diagnosis and management, although the magnitude of the effect varies substantially across studies and depends on information completeness, team structure, workload, leadership, and case complexity \citep{lamb2011mdt,basta2017mdt,koco2021mdt,soukup2020mdt}. This logic has already been transferred to LLM-based systems. MDAgents dynamically selects between individual and collaborative modes depending on estimated medical complexity \citep{kim2024mdagents}. Multi-Agent Conversation (MAC) employs multiple physician agents and a supervising agent that iteratively discuss clinical cases \citep{chen2025mac}. Model Confrontation and Collaboration (MCC) implements structured debate among heterogeneous LLMs \citep{sun2026mcc}. EvoMDT combines specialized agents, retrieval, and a coordinating component \citep{liu2026evomdt}. These systems demonstrate the feasibility of multi-agent medical reasoning and motivate the use of a multidisciplinary LLM council, but they do not by themselves establish that inter-agent interaction is the causal source of performance improvements.

This distinction is particularly important because multi-agent systems generally consume more computational resources than a single generation. Repeated sampling and self-consistency can improve reasoning performance without any message exchange between agents \citep{wang2023selfconsistency}, while Self-Refine improves responses through repeated cycles of critique and revision performed by the same model \citep{madaan2023selfrefine}. In non-medical settings, multi-agent debate has improved reasoning quality and factual accuracy \citep{du2024debate}; however, compute-matched analyses suggest that its advantage over self-consistency may be substantially smaller than its advantage over a single model response produced without iterative critique or inter-agent exchange \citep{wunderlich2026compute}. Deliberation itself may also introduce new classes of errors: consensus pressure can suppress a correct minority position, motivating consensus-free and trajectory-aware approaches \citep{cui2026freemad}. Role assignment constitutes another independent variable. Specialized physician roles are intuitively appealing, yet studies of role and persona prompting indicate that assigning a role label alone does not guarantee improved objective performance and may make outputs sensitive to clinically irrelevant persona attributes \citep{zheng2024personas,araujo2025personas}. A rigorous medical evaluation should therefore attempt to disentangle the effects of additional sampling, role diversity, aggregation, and inter-agent interaction itself.

A separate challenge is how quality should be evaluated. Contemporary studies of medical LLMs increasingly rely on physician-developed rubrics rather than diagnostic agreement alone \citep{cabral2024clinical,goh2024diagnostic}. Scalable automated evaluation methods are also emerging: LLMEval-Med combines expert-designed checklists with LLM-as-a-judge evaluation \citep{zhang2025llmeval}, MedThink-Bench evaluates expert-curated clinical reasoning trajectories using reference-informed LLM judging \citep{zhou2026medthink}, and studies of clinical summarization have reported high agreement between some reasoning models and physician evaluators \citep{croxford2025judge}. These findings make automated evaluation a promising measurement tool, but they do not eliminate the need for physician reference ratings, criterion-specific validation, or analysis of judge-model bias and stability.

In this study, we develop a benchmark of LLM-generated clinical second opinions using 300 published NEJM cases from the NEJM-MedQA diagnostic-reasoning dataset \citep{savage2024reasoning}. Response format, inference method, role composition, and model choice are treated as separate comparison dimensions. The benchmark has two evaluation checkpoints. At the first checkpoint, every case-specific clinical role produces an independent, standalone second opinion. At the second, all role-specific opinions are provided to an interactive multidisciplinary consilium that synthesizes a case-level final opinion through iterative review and revision. The interactive consilium protocol provides a fixed synthesis stage across these comparisons. This design supports controlled comparisons without requiring benchmark users to reproduce one fixed generation architecture. A fixed set of 110 cases is used for physician annotation and judge calibration, the remaining 190 cases are reserved for held-out evaluation.


The main contributions of this work are:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item a benchmark that evaluates role-specific opinions and final consilium reports as distinct clinical products on the same cases
    \item a multidimensional clinical-quality framework that separates factual grounding, problem prioritization, data use, reasoning coherence, differential diagnosis, final-conclusion quality, management, and safety
    \item controlled comparison of model identity, response structure, and synthesis stage together with deliberation dynamics and inference requirements
    \item a physician-developed rubric and criterion-wise calibration of an LLM Judge for scalable evaluation of open-ended clinical second opinions
\end{enumerate}

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related-work}

\subsection{Second Opinions and Multidisciplinary Teams}

In clinical practice, second opinions are evaluated by more than whether they change a diagnosis. A systematic review of patient-initiated second opinions showed that repeat expert assessment can lead to changes in diagnostic formulation, treatment, and patient satisfaction \cite{payne2014secondopinion}. An analysis of a national second-opinion program likewise demonstrated that disagreement with the original assessment may involve both diagnosis and management \cite{meyer2015secondopinion}. These findings support evaluating a second opinion as a multidimensional clinical product rather than solely by agreement on the final diagnosis.

A similar logic emerges from the literature on multidisciplinary teams (MDTs). Systematic reviews in oncology show that MDT discussion frequently changes management, although its effect is heterogeneous and depends on workflow organization, the quality and completeness of the available information, and team composition \cite{lamb2011mdt,basta2017mdt,koco2021mdt}. Observational studies of team decision-making further suggest that collective deliberation should not be equated with a guarantee of correctness \cite{soukup2020mdt}. Accordingly, consensus itself cannot serve as a surrogate endpoint for quality in a multidisciplinary LLM council. Related medical-AI evaluation work formalizes the same concern by comparing algorithmic diagnoses against multiple expert opinions rather than a single majority label, thereby accounting explicitly for inter-expert variability \cite{kopanichuk2026beyond}. More informative questions concern which facts, hypotheses, investigations, risks, and treatment options are preserved, revised, or lost during the process of council deliberation and synthesis.

\subsection{LLMs on Complex Clinical Cases: From Diagnosis to the Full Decision Trajectory}

Early studies showed that contemporary LLMs can solve difficult diagnostic problems based on published clinical cases. Kanjee et al. evaluated GPT-4 on diagnostic challenges, while Cabral et al. compared model-generated clinical reasoning with physician reasoning \cite{kanjee2023gpt4,cabral2024clinical}. Similar findings have been reported in series of complex case reports and clinical scenarios \cite{hirosawa2024generative,rutledge2024diagnostic}. The AMIE study extended this line of work to 302 challenging published case reports and a physician-assistance setting, demonstrating the potential of LLMs both for differential diagnosis and for supporting physicians \cite{mcduff2025amie}. Complementary clinical AI systems have addressed narrower but clinically important tasks, including diagnosis prediction from longitudinal health records and automated mapping of free-text diagnoses to ICD codes \cite{umerenkov2025ai,nesterov2025ruccod}. These systems illustrate the broader clinical-decision-support context in which generated second opinions would operate.

High diagnostic accuracy, however, is not equivalent to comprehensive response quality. Hager et al. developed MIMIC-CDM using 2,400 real-world cases and evaluated not only diagnosis but also information gathering, interpretation of laboratory findings, adherence to diagnostic and treatment recommendations, robustness to the order and amount of available information, and instruction-following ability \cite{hager2024limitations}. Their results indicate that strong diagnostic accuracy does not guarantee adequate performance across the full clinical decision-making process. Similarly, a randomized trial of management reasoning found that GPT-4 assistance can improve physician performance on open-ended patient-care tasks, but that its effect needs to be evaluated separately from diagnostic accuracy and with consideration of interaction time \cite{goh2025patientcare}.

In 2026, Medieval introduced a broad multidisciplinary blinded expert evaluation framework for diagnosis and management across 685 real and simulated clinical questions, including explicit recording of fabricated medical facts \cite{chen2026medieval}. This work is particularly relevant as a precedent for multidimensional evaluation of free-form clinical responses. Reliability also requires recognizing when the available evidence is insufficient: uncertainty-aware selective-prediction methods for medical text can improve diagnostic reliability by allowing a system to abstain from uncertain decisions \cite{vazhentsev2026uncertainty}. Taken together, these studies support separately assessing case-fact utilization, quality of explanation, differential diagnosis, diagnostic workup, treatment, risk, uncertainty, and safety rather than relying on a single aggregate measure of diagnostic accuracy.


\subsection{Structured Clinical Reasoning, Verification, and Test-Time Inference}

Medical studies of structured prompting suggest that the format of reasoning can affect output quality even when the underlying model remains unchanged. Sonoda et al. compared direct and structured prompting on the same radiology cases and observed improved diagnostic performance with an explicit clinical-reasoning structure \cite{sonoda2025structured}. Savage et al. showed that diagnostic reasoning prompts can elicit more interpretable clinical rationales, although the generated chain of thought should not be interpreted as a faithful record of the model's latent internal reasoning process \cite{savage2024reasoning}. Shao and Zhang employed a two-stage framework with predefined verification steps on MedQA and NEJM cases and found that an explicit verification stage could improve diagnostic performance and uncertainty handling \cite{shao2025verification}.

Strong inference-time baselines are essential for interpreting such gains. Self-consistency demonstrates that simply increasing the number of independent generations and subsequently aggregating them can itself improve reasoning performance \cite{wang2023selfconsistency}. Self-Refine demonstrates the value of iterative self-critique and answer revision by a single model \cite{madaan2023selfrefine}. Therefore, when a structured or council-based condition uses more model calls, more tokens, or additional critique stages, superior performance cannot automatically be attributed to the structure or to inter-agent interaction. General-domain equal-compute studies of test-time scaling further highlight the importance of comparing debate, mixtures or ensembles, and self-consistency under comparable inference budgets \cite{wunderlich2026compute}. Recent medical work has further treated the decision pipeline itself as an inference-time optimization target, using LLM-guided evolution to improve accuracy--cost trade-offs in interactive consultation and other clinical decision tasks \cite{sviridov2026llm}. Although these findings are methodological rather than direct clinical evidence, they provide an important basis for designing appropriate baseline comparisons. 

\subsection{Medical Multi-Agent Systems and the Computational Confounding Problem}

Multi-agent architectures in medicine are developing rapidly. MedAgents organizes LLM roles as a set of discipline-specific experts that first provide independent assessments, then engage in multiple rounds of discussion, and finally converge on a shared decision \cite{tang2024medagents}. MDAgents adaptively selects a solo, group, or more complex collaboration mode based on estimated task complexity and achieves strong results across several medical benchmark settings \cite{kim2024mdagents}. MAC employs multiple doctor agents and a supervisor to diagnose rare diseases and subsequently recommend additional investigations \cite{chen2025mac}. More recent systems extend this paradigm through confrontation and debate or structured MDT-style orchestration \cite{sun2026mcc,liu2026evomdt}. Related deployed systems include CLARITY, which combines structured dialogue control with collaborative LLM agents for patient routing, clinical consultation, and triage \cite{shaposhnikov2025clarity}.

These studies establish the feasibility of multi-agent approaches but do not demonstrate that interaction itself is responsible for performance gains. A multi-agent arm typically introduces several changes simultaneously: more generations, larger context windows, a supervisor or aggregator, different prompting strategies, and in some cases retrieval or structured knowledge. MDAgents also modifies the workflow itself according to case complexity, meaning that its group mode does not cleanly isolate the effect of interaction \cite{kim2024mdagents}. In a large benchmark of agentic clinical decision systems, Liu et al. found only moderate accuracy improvements over standalone LLMs despite more than a tenfold increase in token usage and more than a twofold increase in latency \cite{liu2026agentbenchmark}. These findings make inference cost and compute-matched controls essential for interpreting the performance of a multidisciplinary LLM council.

Role labels should likewise not be equated with validated medical specialization. In most medical-agent studies, specialization is induced through prompting rather than through dedicated training and external validation of agent competence. General-domain studies of persona prompting have produced mixed results, further limiting strong claims that assigning a specialist role automatically creates stable domain-specific expertise \cite{zheng2024personas,araujo2025personas}.

\subsection{Human--AI Collaboration, Automation Bias, and Clinical Safety}

Even a strong standalone LLM does not guarantee improvement in a physician--AI team. In a randomized diagnostic trial, access to GPT-4 did not produce a statistically significant improvement in the primary diagnostic-reasoning outcome compared with conventional resources, despite strong exploratory performance of GPT-4 alone \cite{goh2024diagnostic}. In a subsequent randomized management trial, physicians using GPT-4 achieved higher scores on patient-care tasks but required more time, and the physician-plus-GPT condition did not automatically outperform GPT alone \cite{goh2025patientcare}. In a 2026 randomized controlled trial, both first-opinion and second-opinion AI workflows improved diagnostic performance relative to conventional resources, yet the collaborative workflow again did not demonstrate automatic superiority over the AI-alone condition \cite{everett2026teammate}.

Systematic reviews and meta-analyses published in 2026 further support the heterogeneity of human--LLM collaboration. For several diagnostic endpoints, LLM-assisted clinicians outperform clinicians alone on average, but effect sizes vary across models, study designs, and case types, while risks of bias and publication bias remain substantial \cite{wang2026humanllmcollab,chen2026diagnosistriagemeta}. These studies concern clinician outcomes and human--AI interaction, which are outside the scope of the present benchmark; here, physician ratings are used as an expert reference for the quality of the generated material itself.

A separate concern is overreliance on incorrect AI advice. In a randomized vignette study, Jabbour et al. found that systematically biased AI recommendations reduced clinicians' diagnostic accuracy, and visual explanations did not eliminate this effect \cite{jabbour2023impact}. Safety evaluation should therefore account not only for factual or therapeutic errors in an LLM-generated response, but also for the possibility that a plausibly formulated second opinion may shift a physician's decision in the wrong direction.

LLM-generated explanations can themselves influence clinical acceptance: Umerenkov et al. found that explanations increased physicians' agreement with presented diagnoses while also containing nontrivial errors \cite{umerenkov2023deciphering}. Persuasiveness and clinical correctness should therefore be evaluated separately.

\subsection{LLM-as-a-Judge and Physician Expert Evaluation}

Automated evaluation of free-form medical responses is attractive because physician review is expensive and variable, but the available evidence does not support treating LLM-based evaluation as an unconditional surrogate for ground truth. Croxford et al. reported high agreement between LLM-as-a-judge and human evaluation in the relatively constrained task of clinical summarization \cite{croxford2025judge}. Zhou et al. likewise investigated automated expert-level evaluation of medical reasoning \cite{zhou2026medthink}. More recent studies, however, suggest substantial task- and criterion-dependence. In a multicenter study of real de-identified cases, ratings from more than 400 physicians differed substantially from matched AI evaluators \cite{shi2026physicianjudge}. Williams et al. compared five LLM judges with clinicians and found that even the best-performing judge reproduced human ratings for only a subset of eleven criteria, while jury-style aggregation produced only limited improvements in agreement \cite{williams2026judge}.

These findings motivate a more conservative validation framework: physicians remain the primary expert reference, while an LLM judge should be validated against physician ratings separately for each evaluation criterion. Agreement on one dimension, such as factual completeness, should not be assumed to generalize to treatment appropriateness, safety, or uncertainty calibration.


\subsection{Positioning of the Present Study}

The closest prior studies address individual pairs of components in our setting, but we did not identify a published study that, on the same set of real published clinical cases, evaluates standalone role-specific second opinions and subsequent multidisciplinary synthesis while independently varying response structure, sampling and aggregation, and council interaction, and while validating an LLM judge against physicians criterion by criterion. Sonoda et al. provide a direct precedent for a same-case free-form versus structured comparison, whereas MAC, MDAgents, and other medical multi-agent systems compare single- and multi-agent variants without exposing all intermediate opinions as benchmark units or fully separating interaction from inference budget \cite{sonoda2025structured,chen2025mac,kim2024mdagents}. Accordingly, the contribution of the present study is not a presumption that multidisciplinary deliberation is intrinsically superior, but an empirical decomposition of the stages and design choices that produce a clinical second opinion.


\section{Methods}

\subsection{Study Design}

We conducted a benchmark-development and evaluation study using published, diagnostically resolved clinical cases. The generation pipeline comprised three stages: (i) case-specific assignment of clinical roles, (ii) independent role-specific analysis before consilium (the \emph{pre-consilium} stage), and (iii) multidisciplinary synthesis (the \emph{consilium} stage). The dataset's separate \texttt{Answer} field was not supplied to the generation models and was reserved for evaluation.

Physician evaluation comprised three independently scored components (Figure~\ref{fig:study-design}). First, physicians evaluated the clinical reasoning within the relevant role-specific pre-consilium output, excluding its clinical opinion. Second, they evaluated the clinical opinion of the same role-specific output. Third, they evaluated the final case-level consilium conclusion. The same eight quality criteria were applied independently to each component using a 0-3 scale, a score assigned to one component was not carried over to another. Three additional ratings-panel adequacy, case complexity, and overall quality across all stages - were assigned once per case assessment.

The reference pipeline supported two pre-consilium prompt conditions---free-form narrative responses and structured, schema-constrained responses with expanded clinical-reasoning guidance---and two inference topologies (single and $N+1$). All multidisciplinary synthesis used the interactive consilium protocol described below. The conditions included in each analysis were specified prospectively. Model identifiers, prompts, generation parameters, and prerequisite artifacts were recorded for reproducibility.

