\subsection{NEJM Clinical Case Dataset, Selection Rationale, and Prespecified Split}
\label{sec:clinical-case-dataset}



We used the \texttt{nejm\_test} configuration of NEJM--MedQA, which mirrors the supplementary data of Savage et al. \citep{savage2024reasoning,link2024nejmmedqa}. The source dataset includes 300 extended clinicopathological cases selected from 310 recent Case Records after excluding ten without a definitive diagnosis or within the applicable context limit \citep{brinkmann2024caserecords,savage2024reasoning}. We used 300 DOI-unique cases published in 2015--2023. Case descriptions contained a median of 1,036 words (IQR, 852--1,197; range, 231--2,167).

We retained the clinical presentation and normalized metadata but removed source-study instructions, examples, prior model responses, and grades. The separate \texttt{Answer} field was withheld during generation and used only for evaluation. Role assignment and both response conditions received the same DOI, title, and case description; the consilium received an identifier-stripped case, the fixed role opinions, and the review history.

We chose this corpus because its long, diagnostically resolved narratives support open-ended assessment of reasoning, management, and safety, not only diagnostic agreement. The published diagnosis was used for diagnostic concordance but not as exhaustive ground truth for management. Because Case Records favor unusual and difficult cases, results should not be interpreted as performance in routine practice \citep{kanjee2023gpt4,savage2024reasoning,mcduff2025amie,shao2025verification}.

Before evaluation, we reused a prespecified fixed DOI manifest defining 110 cases for physician annotation and Judge calibration, the 190-case held-out evaluation cohort was its exact set complement, with source-dataset order preserved. Here, ``held-out'' means that the 190 evaluation cases were not used for physician annotation or Judge calibration in this study.

\subsection{Role Assignment And Independent Role-Specific Second Opinions}

For each case, the configured role-assignment procedure selected between one and five clinical roles, designated one as primary, and provided a brief case-specific rationale for each role and for the panel as a whole. Exact duplicate role names were rejected case-insensitively, and the primary role was required to match one of the selected roles. The primary-role designation was retained as metadata and did not alter subsequent generation: every selected role produced an opinion. Role-specific prompts received the role name but not its selection rationale or primary-role status.

Every selected role received the same case input and produced its opinion without seeing the opinions produced for other roles. Consequently, each output could be read and evaluated independently as a second opinion from that prompted clinical perspective. The role-specific outputs were fixed before the consilium and were not regenerated during discussion.

In the free-form condition, the model produced a self-contained clinical opinion and was instructed to state missing information and assumptions. In the structured condition, the required fields were: subjective and objective information, clinical syndromes and involved mechanisms, differential diagnosis with reasoning, diagnostic and treatment tactics and final clinical opinion. The structured condition combined a prespecified response schema with more detailed clinical-reasoning instructions. Comparisons with the free-form condition therefore reflected the overall difference between the two prompting approaches, rather than the isolated effect of response format.

\subsection{Interactive Multidisciplinary Consilium Protocol}

We use \emph{consilium} to denote structured multidisciplinary review of a clinical case. The reference workflow adapted the five-stage logic of MedAgents from multiple-choice medical question answering to generation of a self-contained clinical second opinion \citep{tang2024medagents}. It was an adaptation rather than a literal reproduction: the output was an open-ended clinical report, roles addressed both diagnosis and management, and all role-specific opinions were retained.

The interactive protocol first synthesized the case and fixed role-specific opinions into a shared report. In each round, every role independently reviewed the current report and returned either a \texttt{yes} vote or a \texttt{no} vote with a clinically relevant critique and proposed revision. From the second round onward, reviewers also received the reviews and moderator decision from the immediately preceding round. If every role voted \texttt{yes}, the controller recorded consensus. Otherwise, the moderator considered the objections, checked them against the case, and either revised the report or recorded that no substantive change was warranted.

The cycle ended when all roles accepted the report, the moderator reported no substantive change, a revised report exactly repeated a previously seen report state, or five review rounds were completed. When dissent persisted through the fifth review and the moderator made a substantive revision, that revision was retained at the maximum-round stop without a sixth role vote. The finalizer then received the case, all fixed role-specific opinions, the last retained report, all specialist reviews, and summaries of the moderator's revision decisions. It produced a self-contained final opinion while retaining unresolved disagreement and uncertainty. The controller recorded the number of completed rounds, consensus status, and stop reason. The full protocol is specified in Algorithm~\ref{alg:interactive}.

The final free-form output was a self-contained narrative opinion. The structured output contained a consilium summary, resolved and unresolved disagreements, final differential diagnosis, diagnostic and management plans, safety warnings, final second opinion, and confidence level. Structured voting and termination records were used in both response formats. Complete interactive-consilium reports for the same calibration case are provided for the free-form and structured conditions in Appendices~\ref{app:example-report-free} and~\ref{app:example-report-structured}, respectively.


\subsection{Construction of the Physician-Annotation Corpus}
The physician-annotation corpus comprised 110 cases. Each case was processed through both the free-form and structured pipelines using the same finalized role panel.

Before generating the corpus, we screened open-weight models offered through OpenRouter in July 2026 \citep{openrouter2026models}. Eligibility required public weights, support for structured outputs or reasoning, sufficient context for the longest prompts in our pipeline, reliable provider access, and a feasible projected cost. We selected the four highest-ranked eligible models when the OpenRouter catalogue was sorted by the Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index.

GLM-5.2 \citep{zai2026glm52} ranked highest and combined a long context window with broad provider availability, so it was assigned to combine the workers' outputs and conduct the interactive consilium. MiniMax M3 and DeepSeek-V4-Pro \citep{minimax2026m3,deepseek2026v4pro} were the next two eligible models and offered long context windows at comparatively low inference cost. Kimi K2.6 \citep{kimi2026k26} was the next-ranked eligible model and added a third independent developer and model family to the worker panel. Thus, the strongest eligible model performed the final synthesis, while the next three provided independent opinions without relying on a single model family. Role assignment and role-specific pre-consilium generation used a 3+1 procedure: the three worker models independently completed each task, and GLM-5.2 consolidated their outputs.
In both pipelines, the pre-consilium opinions were then passed to the interactive consilium. GLM-5.2 generated one response at each step, with a maximum of five review rounds. This produced 110 final consilium conclusions per pipeline.


\subsection{Single-Model Evaluation}

The 190-case held-out cohort was generated separately with \ConsModelCountWord{} candidate models: \ConsModelList{}. Within each model-specific configuration, the same candidate model performed role assignment, generated every role-specific opinion, and conducted every review, revision, and finalization call. The role assignment for a case was reused by its free-form and structured pipelines.

All models received the same ordered held-out case set and the same stage- and response-format-specific prompts and schemas. Generation used temperature~0, top-$p$~1, seed~42, and at most five review rounds. Model-specific maximum output allowances were retained: 1,024, 2,048, or 4,096 tokens for role assignment, 16,000 or 16,384 tokens for pre-consilium generation and 16,000 or 32,768 tokens for the interactive consilium. Endpoint routing and additional decoding controls were also fixed per model and recorded in the run manifests.

The descriptive process and Judge-scored analyses used these \ConsModelCountWord{} configurations. Role-level Judge scoring was performed only for the primary role in the structured pipeline, whereas final consilium conclusions were scored in both the free-form and structured pipelines; role-level Judge scores were not produced for free-form outputs.


\subsection{Evaluation Criteria}


We used a physician-developed measurement framework comprising eight response-quality criteria and three separately interpreted panel- or case-level ratings (Table~\ref{tab:criteria}). The framework was informed by literature on multidisciplinary clinical review~\citep{lamb2011mdt,basta2017mdt,koco2021mdt,soukup2020mdt}, clinical LLM assessment~\citep{tang2024medagents,kocaman2025clever,mccoy2025assessment,brodeur2026performance}, uncertainty~\citep{savage2025uncertainty}, and safety~\citep{asgari2025framework}. The eight response-quality criteria covered factual grounding, problem prioritization, data use, reasoning coherence, differential diagnosis, the final clinical conclusion, management, and safety. Their applicability was determined by the component being assessed.

The eight response-quality criteria used a 0--3 ordinal scale: 0 - indicated that the criterion was not met, 1 - a major deficiency, 2 - a mostly adequate response with a noticeable limitation and 3 - full and clinically convincing satisfaction. Panel adequacy used the same numeric range but evaluated expertise coverage rather than response quality. Case complexity used 0, 1, 2, and 3 to denote low, moderate, high, and very high complexity. Overall quality was a physician-reported global assessment of all consilium stages.

To account for characteristic LLM errors, an additional rule was introduced. If the final output contained a fabricated clinically significant fact - such as an examination result, numerical value, date, diagnosis, complication, or treatment recommendation absent from the source documents and not designated as a hypothesis - the ``Factual accuracy and sources'' criterion was assigned 0 points. If such a fact served as the basis for differential diagnosis, the final conclusion, or management strategy, the corresponding criteria were scored no higher than 1. This rule ensured that critical factual errors could not be offset by apparent logical coherence, structural completeness, or formatting quality.

