\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:relwork}
\vspace{-0.1cm}
Classical continual learning restricts where updates go using stored data or gradients from
earlier tasks \citep{Kirkpatrick_2017, lopezpaz2017gem, chaudhry2019agem, farajtabar2020ogd,
saha2021gpm, wang2021adamnscl}. These constraints require data that is unavailable at LLM scale
and task boundaries that deployed fine-tuning does not have, whereas SST needs neither.
A complementary line reduces forgetting through the data instead of the update.
Self-distillation rewrites supervision into the model's own distribution \citep{shenfeld2026sdft},
and on-policy RL forgets less because its updates stay close to the base policy
\citep{shenfeld2025razor, pathtakenrlvr}. Those methods induce a favorable update geometry through
on-policy data. SST, by contrast, constrains weight update, and as a result can compose with on-policy methods (Table~\ref{tab:qwen3-main}). We defer discussion of many other related works to Appendix~\ref{sec:related}.

\subsection{Comparison with subspace-protecting methods}
\label{sec:subspace-baselines}

Two recent methods share our starting point of protecting the top singular directions of
pretrained weights while adapting elsewhere. Both differ from SST in the same structural way.
They rewrite the update as a product of trained factors and constrain the factors, whereas SST
trains the weights directly and constrains the one quantity that reaches them, the applied
update.

Sculpting \citep{nayak2025sculpting} freezes the top singular vectors of each weight matrix and
projects the gradient before every AdamW step. Its projection removes only block (1) of
Eq.~\ref{eq:blocks}, the component that reads from and writes onto protected directions at once.
The cross blocks (2) and (3), which touch a protected direction on one side, are never removed.
The projection moreover acts on the gradient, rather than the update as suggested by our
controlled study. Even removing all three blocks from the gradient leaves $69\%$ of the applied
update in protected directions (Figure~\ref{fig:toy-bars}).

OPLoRA \citep{xiong2025oplora} freezes the weights and trains a LoRA adapter, the product $BA$ of
two low-rank trainable matrices, sandwiched between fixed projectors, $\Delta W = Q_U\, BA\, Q_V$
in our notation. This is the projection $\Pi$ of
Eq.~\ref{eq:proj} applied to a low-rank product rather than to the optimizer's step, and it
confines the update fully to the free subspace. The amount
protected is set by the projector rank $k$, and their experiments protect only the top
$16$-$128$ directions while adapting at low rank in everything that remains, nearly the inverted
regime from SST, which protects $99\%$ of the spectrum and adapts densely in the tail. By the
chain rule through the fixed projectors, the gradient components along protected directions never
reach the trainable factors, and the applied update is projected again by construction. This
corresponds to scheme (3) in \cref{sec:applying-projection}, which our controlled study found
mis-scales the step (Figure~\ref{fig:toy-bars}), with Adam's step sizes moreover set in factor
space rather than for the weight update.
\looseness=-1

Another difference is that neither method considers refreshing the protected subspace.
SST introduces refresh as part of the method, re-anchoring the
basis every $K$ steps, and studies it as an explicit retention-adaptation control - swept from no
refresh to R10 throughout our experiments (\cref{sec:refreshing-basis}).

We compare both methods on the four-domain setup under their official settings
(Table~\ref{tab:baselines-4dom}). Both retain more capability than SFT but lose $18$-$26$ points
of prior capability on average where no-refresh SST loses about one, at comparable in-domain
accuracy. Giving OPLoRA our protection level ($k{=}0.99d$) recovers much of the gap, yet it still
trails SST on three of the four domains (Table~\ref{tab:qwen3-main}).
Under data scaling across Medicine, Pathology, and FinQA with official settings, both OPLoRA and
Sculpting forget substantially more as training continues, while SST stays much closer to the
base model (Appendix~\ref{app:baselines}, Figure~\ref{fig:finqa-datascale}). We therefore keep SFT and SDFT, the dominant fine-tuning
recipes in practice, as the primary baselines throughout the paper, while comparing against
Sculpting and OPLoRA in this main setting and on data scaling.
\looseness=-1

\vspace{-0.2cm}
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Catastrophic forgetting is usually treated as the price of learning new tasks. Our work studies it instead as a
property of \emph{where} the update lands, directly controls it, and mitigates forgetting. Confining the applied
optimizer step to the complement of each weight matrix's top singular subspace (Eq.~\ref{eq:proj}) turns
recipes that destroy prior capability into ones that retain it. The same
operator works across recipes (SFT, SDFT, and Muon), from $1.7$B to $32$B parameters, and across data scale, where SFT degrades monotonically and SST stays flat. It also turns sequential fine-tuning from destructive into cumulative and keeps the model $2$--$4\times$ closer to its base
distribution in KL, thus preserving behavior that accuracy alone does not capture, such as a thinking
model's deliberation length and self-distillation's fabricated reference
solutions.

\textbf{Limitations and Future Work.}
SST changes the feasible update geometry, its optimal
learning rate need not equal that of unconstrained SFT. Establishing fully
tuned learning--retention frontiers remains important future work. Scaling SST to models with hundreds of billions of
parameters and to longer task sequences is an important next test. Additionally, using information about the update for each batch to dynamically update the protection rather than refreshing at fixed intervals is a next step to make the algorithm more efficient.

