\section{Extended Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

Classical continual-learning approaches often restrict the update direction: regularization penalizes movement along Fisher-important directions
\citep{Kirkpatrick_2017}, while projected-gradient methods enforce constraints by replacing gradient $g$ with a projection $\Pi(g)$ built from stored data, past-task gradients
\citep{lopezpaz2017gem, chaudhry2019agem, farajtabar2020ogd}, activation subspaces
\citep{saha2021gpm}, the feature-covariance null space \citep{wang2021adamnscl}, or earlier tasks'
adapters \citep{wang2023olora}. Continual learning for LLMs differs from these settings in several ways. These methods estimate subspaces from data, but reconstructing a distribution representative of an LLM's pretraining corpus is infeasible at scale. Truly continual deployment may also lack explicit task boundaries. SST instead preserves a subspace derived from the weight spectrum (Eq.~\ref{eq:proj}), requiring neither stored examples nor task boundaries.

Several parameter-efficient fine-tuning methods also construct adapters from singular directions. PiSSA \citep{meng2024pissa} initializes and trains LoRA factors from the principal singular components, prioritizing high-energy directions rather than protecting them. MiLoRA \citep{wang2025milora} is closer in motivation: it retains the principal components and adapts minor singular components, but represents the update through a fixed low-rank factorization. CorDA \citep{yang2024corda} first forms a context-oriented decomposition using input-activation covariance and initializes low-rank adapters from task- or knowledge-oriented components. In all three methods, the spectral choice is encoded in a parameter-efficient factorization derived at initialization. SST instead projects the dense update actually proposed by the optimizer into the two-sided non-principal complement. It therefore constrains the realized weight change after stateful optimizer preconditioning, does not rely on a trainable adapter factorization or task-specific covariance estimate, and can refresh its protected basis during training.

A complementary line leaves the optimizer
alone and reduces the distribution shift driving forgetting: self-distillation rewrites the supervision
into the model's own output distribution \citep{shenfeld2026sdft}, and online RL forgets less because
its updates stay KL-close to the base policy \citep{shenfeld2025razor, tiwari2026learningfastslowllms}, both consistent with
forgetting tracking distributional movement \citep{kotha2024,
luo2025empiricalstudycatastrophicforgetting}. These methods act on the data, whereas SST acts on the update, and the two
compose (Table~\ref{tab:qwen3-main}).


