\subsection{Model Merging}
\label{app:merge}

If a constrained update stays in the low-energy complement of the weight spectrum, then two
updates trained independently should interfere less when added back together. We test this with model merging experiments. In particular, we fine-tune a separate single-domain expert for Science, Medicine, and FinQA, then form
$W_{\mathrm{merged}}=W_{\mathrm{base}}+\sum_d (W_d-W_{\mathrm{base}})$ over all the $252$ weight
matrices. We copy every other parameter from the base model. No merge coefficients are tuned and no
data is used to fit the combination. Each merged model is then scored in-domain against \emph{its own}
single-domain expert under identical decode settings. We report the interference caused by
merging rather than the raw accuracy gap, which already favors SST.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.55\textwidth]{fig_merge.pdf}
\caption{Interference from task-arithmetic merging. Each bar is the mean change in task accuracy
relative to the same method's own single-domain experts.}
\label{fig:merge}
\end{figure}

\emph{SST experts compose, SFT experts collide.} Figure~\ref{fig:merge} shows that independently trained SFT experts interfere strongly when their task vectors are combined, and the interference compounds as more experts are merged. SST experts compose much more cleanly: pairwise merges remain close to their corresponding single-domain experts, and the three-expert merge degrades substantially more slowly. Each SST expert modifies only the low-energy subspace, so adding their updates leaves the protected principal directions unchanged. Unconstrained experts can each alter these directions, causing their changes to accumulate during merging.


\subsection{Two-stage continual learning with SDFT}
\label{app:continual-sdft-two-stage}

Figure~\ref{fig:continual-2stage-sdft} adds plain SDFT to the same two-stage setup as
Figure~\ref{fig:2stage-delta}. SDFT improves target learning over SFT in all three chains,
but its retention is chain-dependent. It reduces forgetting on Science $\rightarrow$ Pathology
and Pathology $\rightarrow$ FinQA, yet loses $20.0$ points on FinQA $\rightarrow$ Science.
SST+R10 gives the most consistent retention--learning trade-off.

For Pathology $\rightarrow$ FinQA, we regrade the saved SDFT generations by prioritizing the final
boxed numeric answer before the existing \texttt{Answer:} fallback. This corrects an
evaluator-format mismatch, giving $75.2$ target accuracy ($+4.7$ over base); the source-task score,
and therefore the $5.6$ points of forgetting, is unchanged.

