\subsection{Instruct tuning without forgetting}
\label{app:instruct}

We also study a non-thinking instruction model with shorter sequences, where forgetting is less severe. Table~\ref{tab:qwen25} reports Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct \citep{qwen25} on ToolAlpaca \citep{toolalpaca}, HuatuoGPT-o1 \citep{huatuogpto1}, and SciKnowEval \citep{sciknoweval}, matching the SDFT setup \citep{shenfeld2026sdft}. All cells use rank fraction $r{=}0.99$ except Science, which uses $r{=}0.90$. On this model and domain, the tail left free at $r{=}0.99$ was too small to fit the task. This is the only setting in the paper that required a looser protected rank.

\emph{SFT learns the task but forgets.} Table~\ref{tab:qwen25} shows that standard fine-tuning improves the target task but reduces prior capability, with the clearest losses in instruction following and knowledge. SDFT moderates this effect in some settings but is not consistently protective across domains.

\emph{SST holds capability at matched fit.} Adding SST recovers much of the lost prior capability on Tool Use and Medical while remaining competitive on in-domain accuracy. Science is a narrower trade-off: SST variants can improve task learning, but no single variant dominates both learning and retention. Table~\ref{tab:qwen25} gives the exact per-domain and per-benchmark values. The capability average covers HellaSwag \citep{hellaswag}, TruthfulQA-MC1 \citep{truthfulqa}, MMLU \citep{mmlu}, IFEval \citep{ifeval}, and WinoGrande \citep{winogrande}.

