\subsection{KL regularisation does not prevent forgetting}
\label{app:kl-reg}

\emph{Penalising the policy does not mitigate forgetting.} The natural alternative to constraining
\emph{where} an update may move is to leave the update unconstrained and instead penalise how far
the resulting policy drifts. We fine-tune Qwen3-8B on FinQA and on Science with
$\mathcal{L}=\mathrm{CE}+\lambda\,\mathrm{KL}(\pi_\theta\,\|\,\pi_\mathrm{base})$, a reverse KL over
the response tokens against a frozen copy of the base model, at
$\lambda\in\{0.001,0.01,0.1\}$, plus a $\lambda{=}0$ control on FinQA.
Table~\ref{tab:kl-reg} and Figure~\ref{fig:kl-reg} show that reverse-KL regularisation does not provide a reliable learning--retention trade-off. Weak regularisation leaves forgetting essentially unchanged, while stronger regularisation can reduce target-task learning without restoring prior capability. The effect also varies across tasks, unlike the consistent retention obtained by constraining the update subspace. The table reports the exact per-task and per-benchmark values.

