\section{Introduction} \label{sec:intro}

\emph{Strategic reasoning} in multi-agent systems (MAS) hinges on determining what outcomes groups of agents (termed coalitions) can bring about through their interactions. In many multi-agent settings, especially safety-critical systems, properties of interest go beyond plain reward signals and include \emph{temporally extended goals} such as \emph{reachability/liveness} (e.g., eventually reaching a target region or completing a mission), or \emph{safety invariants} (e.g., always avoid unsafe regions, or never violate constraints)~\cite{amodei2016concrete,garcia2015comprehensive}. Similar objectives are naturally captured by temporal logics~\cite{manna1990hierarchy} which provide precise languages and semantics to specify and verify such properties. Languages relevant to specification of strategic properties include Alternating-Time Temporal Logic ($\ATL$~\cite{alur2002alternating}), Dynamic Coalition Logic~\cite{galimullin2021dynamic}, Probabilistic Alternating-$\mu$ Calculus~\cite{song2019probabilistic}, Game Description Logic ($\GDL$~\cite{de2010situation}), and See-To-It-That ($\STIT$~\cite{belnap1988seeing}). However, such logics typically resolve non-determinism by restricting to coalitional modalities which aim to universally and unilaterally (inevitably) force a property to be true.

On the other hand, \emph{rational synthesis}~\cite{fisman2010rational,ummels2006rational} aims to find strategies that are self-enforcing through stable equilibria, instead of quantifying against all possible behaviour (including irrational behaviour) by other agents who have their own objectives. Therefore, rational synthesis is typically less conservative than adversarial synthesis.

Settings we consider can be represented formally as \emph{(general-sum) stochastic games}, which model multiple decision-making agents that interact over time in a dynamic environment with probabilistic behaviours. Each agent repeatedly selects an action, and the combination of agents' non-deterministic choices and the environment's probabilistic transitions determines the evolution of the game. Stochastic games have been applied in domains as diverse as \emph{cybersecurity}~\cite{zhao2020finite}, \emph{epidemics}~\cite{huang2022game}, \emph{auctions}~\cite{manvi2023nash}, and routing in \emph{stochastic traffic networks}~\cite{yang2024markov}. 

We use an expressive specification style in which \emph{each agent} has its own probabilistic temporal objective as a logical formula interpreted on finite traces. Finite traces have a natural correspondence with reinforcement learning (RL) as tasks are often trained and executed episodically. Rationality is captured by imposing that the learned joint strategy profile satisfies a specific solution concept (e.g., ($\varepsilon$-)Nash equilibrium, subgame-perfect equilibrium), where each agent's payoff reflects the preferred direction of its satisfaction probability. Our approach emphasises learning a single committed strategy profile, allowing tractability by not requiring an explicit transition model of the underlying stochastic game which generally grows combinatorially with respect to the number of agents involved.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
Our contributions are as follows:  \begin{inparaenum}[(i)]
  \item We formulate a learning-based rational-synthesis objective for finite-horizon stochastic games in which each agent has a probabilistic $\LTLf$ objective and utilities encode the preferred direction of satisfaction probabilities;
  \item We compile per-agent $\LTLf$ objectives into deterministic reward automata and show that terminal automaton rewards have expectation equal to satisfaction probability;
  \item We provide theoretical analysis for the reward-automaton encoding, discounting effects, finite-sample satisfaction certification, and finite-horizon equilibrium existence and convergence;
  \item We evaluate satisfaction and strategic stability on 8 benchmark environment instances.
\end{inparaenum}

For reasons of space, proofs are deferred to Appendix~\ref{appsec:technical_details}. A high-level overview for our approach is presented in Appendix~\ref{appsec:overview}.

\section{Preliminaries}
\label{sec:prelims}

\paragraph*{Multi-agent Reinforcement Learning.}
We model multi-agent RL problems as an $n$-agent \emph{concurrent stochastic game} (also known as a {\em Markov game}~\cite{littman1994markov}) $\mathcal{M} = (\mathcal{S}, s_0, \mathcal{A}^1, \dots, \mathcal{A}^n,\allowbreak d^1,\dots,d^n,\allowbreak \mathcal{P}, \mathcal{R}^1, \dots, \mathcal{R}^n, \gamma)$, where $\mathcal{S}$ is the finite {\em state space} and $s_0 \in \mathcal{S}$ is the {\em initial state}; $\mathcal{A}^i$ is the finite {\em action space} of agent $i \in [1, \dots, n]$; 
$d^i:\mathcal{S}\to 2^{\mathcal{A}^i}\setminus\{\emptyset\}$ is the \emph{protocol} (action-availability) function of agent $i$, where $d^i(s)$ denotes the set of legal actions for agent $i$ in state $s$; the set of legal joint actions at $s$ is $d(s)=\prod_{i=1}^n d^i(s)\subseteq \mathcal{A}$;
$\mathcal{P}: \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{A} \to \Delta(\mathcal{S})$ is the (partial) {\em transition function} (defined only for joint actions $a \in d(s)$), with $\Delta(\mathcal{S})$ being the set of probability distributions over $\mathcal{S}$;  $\mathcal{R}^i: \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{A} \to \mathbb{R}$ is the (Markovian) reward function of agent $i$; and $\gamma$ is the discount factor.\\
A (stochastic) \emph{policy} $\pi^i$ (also known as a {\em strategy}) of agent $i$ is a function $\pi^i : \mathcal{S} \to \Delta(\mathcal{A}^i)$ with $\operatorname{supp}\pi^i(\cdot\mid s)\subseteq d^i(s)$ for every $s$. Then, the joint action space of agents is $\mathcal{A}=\prod_{i=1}^n\mathcal{A}^i$, and the joint policy of other agents is $\pi^{-i}(a^{-i}|s) = \prod_{j \neq i}\pi^j(a^j|s)$, where $a^{-i}$ is the joint action except agent $i$'s action. Each agent $i$ has their own goal to maximise their expected cumulative discounted rewards, defined as:
\begin{equation}
\mathop{\mathbb{E}}\limits_{\substack{s_{t+1}\sim\mathcal{P}(\cdot|s_t, a_t^i, a_t^{-i}), \\a_t^i\sim\pi^i(\cdot |s_t), a_t^{-i}\sim\pi^{-i}(\cdot |s_t)}}\left [ \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\gamma^t\mathcal{R}^i(s_t,a_t^i,a_t^{-i})  \right ]
\end{equation}
The decentralised stochastic game, from the perspective of each agent, is \emph{non-stationary},  as the other agents concurrently update their policies. General-sum stochastic games have computational solution concepts which assess stability under equilibria, since they account for the actions of the remaining agents.

A \emph{labelled stochastic game} simply extends a stochastic game with a labelling function $\mathcal{L}: \mathcal{S} \to 2^{\Ap}$ which maps each state $s$ to a set  $\mathcal{L}(s) \subseteq \Ap$ of atoms, which denotes which atoms are true at $s$. We denote a joint set of strategies for a coalition $C \subseteq [1, \dots, n]$ as $\sigma_C$, and for all agents in the concurrent stochastic game, we denote the \emph{strategy profile} $\StrategyProfile \defeq  (\sigma_1, \dots, \sigma_n)$ as the set composed of each agent's strategy.

We denote a \emph{finite path} as $\pi=s_0a_0s_1\cdots a_{T-1}s_T$, where $a_t\in d(s_t)$ and $\mathcal P(s_{t+1}\mid s_t,a_t)>0$ for every $0\le t<T$. The horizon $H$ bounds the number of transitions, so $T\le H$ and the associated label trace has $T+1$ positions. Let $\FPath_{\mathcal M, s}$ denote the set of finite paths from $s$. For a strategy profile $\StrategyProfile$, let $\Pr^{\StrategyProfile}_{\mathcal M,s}$ be the induced probability measure on complete finite paths $\FPath^{\text{comp}}_{\mathcal M,s}$.  We refer to \cite{baier2008principles} for the standard construction of the probability measure $\Pr^{\StrategyProfile}_{\mathcal M,s}$ induced by a strategy profile. We learn a single strategy profile using \algo{IPPO}~\cite{schulman2017proximal,de2020independent}, \algo{IQL}~\cite{Tan1993}, \algo{Nash-Q Learning (NQL)}~\cite{hu2003nash}, and \algo{PR2-IQL}/\algo{PR2-IPPO} variants~\cite{wen2019pr2} (further details in Appendix~\ref{appsec:PR2}).

\paragraph*{Probabilistic Linear Temporal Logic on Finite Traces.}
We use Probabilistic $\LTLf$ ($\PLTLf$)~\cite{maggi2020temporal} to specify each agent's probabilistic temporal objective on finite traces, matching the episodic structure of RL tasks. Each completed episode supplies a finite sequence of state labels on which a temporal goal is evaluated: for example, reaching a target before the episode ends while avoiding collisions throughout. The probabilistic requirement then constrains how often that goal is satisfied under a fixed strategy profile. Equilibrium constraints capture rationality through utilities reflecting each agent's preferred direction of satisfaction.
The grammar of Probabilistic Linear Temporal Logic on Finite Traces ($\PLTLf$) is  defined as follows:
\begin{definition}[$\PLTLf$ Formula]
  $\PLTLf$ formulas are constructed inductively from the set of atoms $\Ap$, where $p \in \Ap$, $\top$ denotes true and $\bot$ denotes false, $d \in [0,1]$ is a rational constant, and $\bowtie \: \in \{ <, \leq, >, \geq \}$. Formally, the grammar is:
    \begin{align*}
      \varphi &::= \top
        \mid p
        \mid \neg \varphi
        \mid \varphi \land \varphi
        \mid \Next \, \varphi
        \mid \varphi_1 \, \Until \, \varphi_2
      \\
      \psi &::= \Prob_{\bowtie d}[\varphi]
    \end{align*}
\end{definition}
$\Next$ denotes temporal Next and $\Until$ denotes temporal Until. The remaining Boolean and temporal operators 
are syntactic sugar defined as usual: Eventually $\Eventually$ as $\Eventually \varphi \equiv \top \Until \varphi$ and Always $\Always$ as $\Always \varphi \equiv \lnot \Eventually \lnot \varphi$.
In multi-agent systems, agents with no specific objectives can be included in a residual coalition with a trivial objective always achievable with probability of 1 (e.g., $\top$). The full finite-trace semantics are given in Appendix~\ref{appsec:pltlf_semantics}. We provide the probabilistic clause of $\PLTLf$ as:
\[
\begin{gathered}
\mathcal M,s,\StrategyProfile \models \Prob_{\bowtie d}[\varphi]
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\\
\Pr^{\StrategyProfile}_{\mathcal M,s}\big(\{\pi\in\FPath^{\mathrm{comp}}_{\mathcal M,s}\mid
\mathcal M,\pi\models \varphi\}\big)\ \bowtie d.
\end{gathered}
\]

\paragraph*{Rational Synthesis.}
Classical logics with strategic coalition modalities (such as $\ATL$~\cite{alur2002alternating} and $\PATL$~\cite{chen2007probabilistic}) assert temporal goals against \emph{all} behaviours of the other agents. This treats other coalitions as strictly adversarial, which can lead to overly conservative solutions. In settings where other agents have their own objectives, it is more natural to assume they act \emph{rationally} and restrict their behaviour to strategy profiles satisfying a solution concept (e.g., (strong) Nash equilibrium, subgame-perfect equilibrium)~\cite{fisman2010rational,condurache2016complexity}. We write $\StrategyProfile \in \Sol_\zeta(s;\utilVec)$ to denote that $\StrategyProfile$ satisfies solution concept $\zeta$ at state $s$ for the payoff vector $\utilVec$, where the utilities encode each agent's preferred direction of satisfaction.

\begin{definition}[Satisfaction Probabilities and Utilities]\label{def:objective_utilities}
For agent $i$ with requirement $\Obj_i=\Prob_{\bowtie_i d_i}[\PathObj_i]$, define $p_i(s,\StrategyProfile)\defeq\Pr_{\mathcal M,s}^{\StrategyProfile}(\PathObj_i)$ and $f_i:[0,1]\to[0,1]$ by
\[
f_i(p)\defeq\begin{cases}
p, & \bowtie_i\in\{>,\ge\},\\
1-p, & \bowtie_i\in\{<,\le\}.
\end{cases}
\]
The utility is $u_i(s,\StrategyProfile)\defeq f_i(p_i(s,\StrategyProfile))$. Write $\utilVec(s,\StrategyProfile)\defeq\big(u_i(s,\StrategyProfile)\big)_{i=1}^{n}$.
\end{definition}
Definition~\ref{def:objective_utilities} makes utility maximisation favour larger satisfaction probabilities for lower-bound requirements and smaller probabilities for upper-bound requirements. We target the following notion of $\varepsilon$-Nash equilibrium:

\begin{definition}[$\varepsilon$-Nash Equilibrium] \label{def:eps_ne}
    A strategy profile $\StrategyProfile=(\sigma_1,\dots,\sigma_n)$ is an \emph{$\varepsilon$-Nash equilibrium} ($\EpsNE$) at state $s$ iff, for every agent $i$ and every alternative strategy $\sigma_i'$, we have 
    $
      u_i\!\left(s,(\sigma_i',\sigma_{-i})\right)\le u_i(s,\StrategyProfile)+\varepsilon,
    $
    where $\sigma_{-i}$ denotes the strategies of all agents except $i$. For $\varepsilon=0$, $\StrategyProfile$ is a \emph{Nash equilibrium} ($\NE$).
\end{definition}
The largest gain any single agent can obtain by deviating unilaterally measures how far a profile is from equilibrium:
\begin{definition}[Exploitability]\label{def:exploitability}
    The \emph{exploitability} of a strategy profile $\StrategyProfile$ at state $s$ is
    $
      \Exploit(s,\StrategyProfile)\defeq \max_i\sup_{\sigma_i'}\big[u_i\big(s,(\sigma_i',\sigma_{-i})\big)-u_i(s,\StrategyProfile)\big].
    $
\end{definition}
By Definitions~\ref{def:eps_ne} and~\ref{def:exploitability}, $\StrategyProfile$ is an $\EpsNE$ at $s$ if and only if $\Exploit(s,\StrategyProfile)\le\varepsilon$.

\paragraph{Problem Statement.}
We consider a finite labelled concurrent stochastic game $\mathcal M$ with initial state $s_0$, finite horizon $H$, and a solution concept $\zeta$, such as $\EpsNE$. The transition probabilities are unknown, and strategies are learned through episodic interaction with the environment. Each agent $i\in\{1,\dots,n\}$ has an $\LTLf$ path objective $\PathObj_i$ and a probabilistic requirement $\Obj_i=\Prob_{\bowtie_i d_i}[\PathObj_i]$. Let $\Sigma_i$ denote agent $i$'s admissible strategy space and $\Sigma\defeq\prod_{i=1}^{n}\Sigma_i$. Each agent seeks an extremal satisfaction probability at equilibrium: a maximum for a lower-bound requirement and a minimum for an upper-bound requirement, while meeting its threshold.

\begin{definition}[Equilibrium Profiles]\label{def:equilibrium_profiles}
The set of admissible profiles satisfying solution concept $\zeta$ for the utilities in Definition~\ref{def:objective_utilities} is
\[
\mathcal E_\zeta\defeq\left\{\StrategyProfile\in\Sigma\;\middle|\;\StrategyProfile\in\Sol_\zeta(s_0;\utilVec)\right\}.
\]
\end{definition}
Definition~\ref{def:equilibrium_profiles} captures stability under the agents' individual payoff-maximising preferences. For agent $i$, the acceptable equilibrium profiles are $\mathcal F_\zeta^i\defeq\{\StrategyProfile\in\mathcal E_\zeta\mid p_i(s_0,\StrategyProfile)\bowtie_i d_i\}$.

\begin{definition}[Rational Strategy Synthesis]\label{def:rational_synthesis}
Given $(\mathcal M,s_0,H,\allowbreak (\Obj_j)_{j=1}^{n},\zeta)$, admissible strategy spaces $(\Sigma_j)_{j=1}^{n}$, and an agent $i$, the \emph{rational strategy synthesis problem for agent $i$} is to find a strategy profile $\StrategyProfile^\star=(\sigma_i^\star,\sigma_{-i}^\star)\in\mathcal F_\zeta^i$, if such a profile exists.
\end{definition}
Definition~\ref{def:rational_synthesis} requires agent $i$'s threshold to hold at an equilibrium profile. The other agents' objectives determine their strategic preferences, but their thresholds do not necessarily need to hold. Correspondingly, the feasibility question is whether $\mathcal F_\zeta^i\neq\emptyset$, since the existence of equilibria alone is insufficient for the existence of threshold-constrained Nash equilibria. Proving infeasibility may require ruling out all admissible equilibrium profiles, which is outside the scope of model-free rational learning.

An equilibrium profile is additionally said to be \emph{jointly satisfying} if it belongs to $\mathcal F_\zeta\defeq\bigcap_{i=1}^{n}\mathcal F_\zeta^i$, so that every agent's probabilistic threshold holds at the same profile. Each agent uses a threshold-constrained Nash equilibrium as its primary solution concept, as opposed to a jointly satisfying Nash equilibrium.

We investigate model-free MARL approaches in which agents concurrently learn strategies towards their respective synthesis objectives. For each learned profile, we assess each agent's probabilistic requirement using finite-sample confidence bounds and the profile's strategic stability using finite-budget approximate best-response search.

\section{Encoding Quantitative Objectives} \label{sec:encoding}
We outline how to translate $\PLTLf$ objectives into Markovian reward signals for applying standard (model-free) multi-agent RL algorithms, while preserving the \emph{single strategy profile witness} requirement of strategy synthesis.

We compile each $\LTLf$ path objective into a deterministic reward automaton, then take the synchronous product of these automata with the stochastic game. Terminal acceptance rewards recover satisfaction probabilities (Theorem~\ref{thm:coherence}), and the product makes the objectives Markovian while preserving payoffs and equilibria (Theorem~\ref{thm:markov_sufficiency_product}). Discounting results quantify how terminal rewards relate to satisfaction probabilities under fixed and early-terminating horizons. Sampling results provide finite-sample certificates for probabilistic thresholds. Algorithm~\ref{alg:approach} summarises the procedure.

\input{algos/approach.tex}

\paragraph{Reward Automata.}
For each agent, a deterministic finite automaton (DFA) tracks whether the labelled trace of an episode satisfies the $\LTLf$ path formula inside its probabilistic constraint. Its state summarises the history relevant to that formula and is updated as each new state label is read. For example, the automaton in Figure~\ref{fig:dfa} remembers whether a breach has occurred and whether a collision has made the objective impossible. At episode end, its accepting states identify successful traces, and averaging these binary outcomes estimates satisfaction probability. Constructing this automaton takes $\TWOEXPTIME$ in the worst case~\cite{de2015synthesis}. In practice, temporal objectives are often simple and avoid deep temporal nesting, allowing existing tools to construct their automata in milliseconds. Thus, the worst-case complexity need not make compilation a practical bottleneck. Figure~\ref{fig:dfa} illustrates a simple example.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \input{figures/diagrams/dfa.tex}
  \caption{DFA for $\Eventually(\mathsf{intruder\_breach})\land\Always(\lnot\mathsf{collision})$ in \env{Stochastic Pursuit Evasion}. State $0$ is initial and state $2$ is accepting. Acceptance is evaluated at episode end.}
  \Description{Three-state reward automaton. State 0 waits for an intruder breach without collision, state 2 records success, and any collision leads to the absorbing failure state 1.}
  \label{fig:dfa}
\end{figure}

\begin{definition}[Deterministic Reward Automaton]
  Given a $\LTLf$ formula, the corresponding \emph{reward automaton} is a deterministic finite automaton over the alphabet $\Alphabet\defeq 2^{\Ap}$, represented as the tuple $A_{\varphi} = (M, m_0, \Alphabet, \delta, F)$, where $M$ is the set of states; $m_0 \in M$ is the initial state; $F \subseteq M$ is the set of accepting states; and $\delta: M \times \Alphabet \to M$ is the total transition function.
\end{definition}
For every finite word $\tau\in\Alphabet^+$, we have:
$
\tau,0\models \varphi \; \text{iff} \; \delta^\ast(m_0,\tau)\in F,
$
where $\delta^\ast$ is the usual extension of $\delta$ to words.  The automaton records acceptance at the terminal timestep. Agent $i$'s learning reward applies $f_i$ to this indicator. For each objective $\varphi$ we define an episodic (terminal) reward functional on complete paths:
\[
\mathcal R_\varphi(\pi)\ \defeq \ \mathbf 1\big[\delta^\ast(m_0,\trace(\pi))\in F\big]
\ =\ \mathbf 1\big[\mathcal M,\pi\models \varphi\big].
\]
This is a Bernoulli random variable under the induced measure $\Pr^{\StrategyProfile}_{\mathcal M,s}$.  Theorem~\ref{thm:coherence} states coherence between the expected return induced by a reward automaton and the satisfaction probability.

\begin{restatable}[Coherence]{theorem}{coherencethm}
\label{thm:coherence}
For any strategy profile $\StrategyProfile$ and state $s$:
$
\mathbb E_{\pi\sim\Pr^{\StrategyProfile}_{\mathcal M,s}}\!\left[\mathcal R_\varphi(\pi)\right]
\;=\;
\Pr^{\StrategyProfile}_{\mathcal M,s}(\varphi).
$
\end{restatable}
Proof in Appendix~\ref{appsec:proof_coherence}. For multiple agents, the individual reward automata are run synchronously in a \emph{composite reward automaton}, whose state records each agent's automaton state and whose terminal reward vector records component-wise acceptance.

\begin{definition}[Composite Reward Automaton]
\label{def:composite_reward_automaton}
Let $\PathObj_1,\dots,\PathObj_n\in\LTLf(\Ap)$, and for each $i\in\{1,\dots,n\}$ let
$\mathcal A_{\PathObj_i}=(M_i,m_0^i,\Alphabet,\delta_i,F_i)$ be a deterministic reward automaton.
The \emph{composite reward automaton} is the deterministic monitor
$
\mathcal A_{\PathObjVec}\ \defeq \ \big(M_c,m_0^c,\Alphabet,\delta_c\big)
$
where $M_c\defeq \prod_{i=1}^n M_i$,
$m_0^c\defeq (m_0^1,\dots,m_0^n)$, and
$
\delta_c\big((m_1,\dots,m_n),\ell\big)
\defeq
\big(\delta_1(m_1,\ell),\dots,\delta_n(m_n,\ell)\big).
$
Acceptance is tracked component-wise via the sets $F_i$:
for a composite state $\bm m=(m_1,\dots,m_n)$ we write $\bm m\models F_i$ iff $m_i\in F_i$.
\end{definition}

Definition~\ref{def:composite_reward_automaton} synchronises the individual monitors. Along an episode $\pi=s_0\bm a_0s_1\cdots s_T$, initialise $\bm m_0=\delta_c(m_0^c,\mathcal L(s_0))$ and update $\bm m_{t+1}=\delta_c(\bm m_t,\mathcal L(s_{t+1}))$, so $\bm m_t$ records the label prefix through $s_t$.

\begin{definition}[Time-Augmented Product Game]\label{def:product_stochastic_game}
Let $\mathcal M$ be a finite labelled stochastic game, $\mathcal A_{\PathObjVec}$ its composite reward automaton, and $H$ the horizon. Let $\kappa:\mathcal S\times\{0,\dots,H\}\to\{0,1\}$ encode the original stopping rule, with $\kappa(s,H)=1$ for every $s$. The stopping time is $T\defeq\min\{0\le t\le H:\kappa(s_t,t)=1\}$ (any information needed by this rule is included in $s$). The \emph{time-augmented synchronous product} $\widehat{\mathcal M}_H$ has state space
\[
\widehat{\mathcal S}_H\defeq\mathcal S\times M_c\times\{0,\dots,H\},
\]
where $x=(s,\bm m,t)$ is formed of the environment state, composite monitor state, and elapsed time, with initial state $x_0=(s_0,\delta_c(m_0^c,\mathcal L(s_0)),0)$. A state $x$ is terminal exactly when $\kappa(s,t)=1$. No further action or transition occurs there. At each non-terminal $x$, agent $i$ retains its original legal actions $d_H^i(x)\defeq d^i(s)$, and we write $d_H(x)\defeq\prod_i d_H^i(x)$. For $x'=(s',\bm m',t')$ and $\bm a\in d_H(x)$, the transition kernel is
\[
\tilde{\mathcal P}(x'\mid x,\bm a)\defeq
\begin{cases}
\mathcal P(s'\mid s,\bm a), & \begin{aligned}[t] &\textnormal{if }t'=t+1 and\\ &\phantom{\textnormal{if }}\bm m'=\delta_c(\bm m,\mathcal L(s'));\end{aligned}\\[4pt]
0, & \textnormal{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
\]
The game has a terminal payoff of
$
R(s,\bm m,t)\defeq\big(f_i(\mathbf 1[\bm m\models F_i])\big)_{i=1}^n.
$
\end{definition}
Definition~\ref{def:product_stochastic_game} makes both the temporal objectives and the horizon Markovian. The episode ends at $T$ with the expected terminal payoff being $u_i$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:coherence}. Retaining $t$ allows strategies to depend on the remaining horizon. We take each $\Sigma_i$ to consist of randomised strategies that are memoryless on $(s_t,\bm m_t,t)$, with independent randomisation across agents.
\begin{restatable}[Markov Sufficiency in the Composite Product]{theorem}{markovsufficiencythm}
\label{thm:markov_sufficiency_product}
For finite-horizon $\LTLf$ objectives $\PathObj_1,\dots,\PathObj_n$ and every strategy profile $\StrategyProfile\in\Sigma$,
the satisfaction probabilities $\big(p_i(s_0,\StrategyProfile)\big)_{i=1}^n$ and utilities $\utilVec(s_0,\StrategyProfile)$
can be realised by a memoryless strategy profile on $\widehat{\mathcal M}_H$, and every such memoryless profile induces a profile in $\Sigma$ with the same payoffs.
\end{restatable}
Theorem~\ref{thm:markov_sufficiency_product} identifies the admissible strategies with memoryless product policies while preserving their objective values. Proof is provided in Appendix~\ref{appsec:proof_markov_sufficiency}.

\subsection{Discount Factor and Effective Horizon} \label{sec:discount_factor}
The product above represents undiscounted utilities. Our experiments use terminal rewards with $\gamma\in\{0.99,1\}$ at the original stopping time $T$. The relationship of discounted rewards to satisfaction probability depends on whether episode length is fixed.

The following results apply to terminal acceptance rewards for $\PathObj_i$ under lower-bound requirements and for $\neg\PathObj_i$ under upper-bound requirements. We first consider fixed-length episodes:
\begin{restatable}[Fixed-Horizon Terminal Reward Is Invariant to Discounting]{theorem}{terminalinvariancethm}
\label{thm:terminal_invariance}
Consider episodes of fixed time indices $t = 0, \dots, H$ and a terminal reward of the form $r_t=0$ for $t<H$ and $r_H = \mathbf{1}[\varphi] \in \{0,1\}$.
Then for any strategy profile $\StrategyProfile$,
$
\mathbb{E}_{\StrategyProfile}\!\left[\sum_{t=0}^{H}\gamma^t r_t\right]
= \gamma^{H}\,\Pr_{\StrategyProfile}(\varphi)
$.
In particular, for fixed $H$ and $\gamma\in(0,1)$, any strategy profile that maximises the discounted return also maximises $\Pr_{\StrategyProfile}(\varphi)$. Exact Nash equilibria are unchanged, but exploitability in discounted-return units is $\gamma^H\Exploit$.
\end{restatable}
Proof in Appendix~\ref{appsec:proof_terminal_invariance}. Commonly in RL, when the goal is achieved early or some irrecoverable failure state is reached, the episode terminates and resets. With early termination, maximising discounted return is not exactly maximising satisfaction probability, as it favours earlier satisfaction.

\begin{restatable}[Discounted Return under Early Termination]{proposition}{earlytermdiscountprop}
\label{prop:early_term_discount}
Let $T\le H$ be the (random) termination time of an episode and let $A$ be the acceptance event.
If the reward is terminal, i.e., $r_t=0$ for $t<T$ and $r_T=\mathbf{1}[A]$, then the discounted return is
$
G_\gamma \;=\; \sum_{t=0}^{T}\gamma^t r_t \;=\; \gamma^{T}\mathbf{1}[A].
$
Hence for any strategy profile $\StrategyProfile$, we have
$
\mathbb{E}_{\StrategyProfile}[G_\gamma] \;=\; \mathbb{E}_{\StrategyProfile}\!\big[\gamma^T\mathbf{1}[A]\big]
$.
\end{restatable}
Proof in Appendix~\ref{appsec:proof_early_term_discount}. However, by Corollary~\ref{cor:early_term_bounds}, even with early stopping the discounted terminal-reward objective is a lower-biased surrogate for $\Pr_{\StrategyProfile}(A)$
that additionally encodes a preference for earlier satisfaction:
\begin{restatable}[Discounted Objective Bounds Satisfaction Probability]{corollary}{earlytermboundscor}
\label{cor:early_term_bounds}
Under the assumptions of Prop.~\ref{prop:early_term_discount} and $T\le H$, we have
$
\gamma^H \Pr_{\StrategyProfile}(A)\ \le\ \mathbb{E}_{\StrategyProfile}[G_\gamma]\ \le\ \Pr_{\StrategyProfile}(A).
$
\end{restatable}
Proof in Appendix~\ref{appsec:proof_early_term_bounds}. Thus, when episodes terminate early upon success or failure, the discounted objective ranks policies by a \emph{discount-weighted} satisfaction probability, i.e., it may prefer earlier satisfaction even if the raw probability is unchanged.
If one wishes to optimise \emph{exactly} $\Pr_{\StrategyProfile}(A)$ under early termination, one can set $\gamma=1$, which still yields a finite return in episodic settings. Otherwise, the bias introduced can also be bounded by $\xi$ per Corollary~\ref{cor:gamma_epsilon_discount_bias}:
\begin{restatable}[$\xi$-Bounded Discount Bias under Early Termination]{corollary}{gammaepsilondiscountbiascor}
\label{cor:gamma_epsilon_discount_bias}
Under the assumptions of Corollary~\ref{cor:early_term_bounds}, to bound the discount-induced bias by at most $\xi \in [0,1]$ uniformly over strategy profiles, it suffices to choose
$
\gamma \ge (1-\xi)^{1/H}.
$
That is, for every strategy profile $\StrategyProfile$,
$
0 \le \Pr_{\StrategyProfile}(A)-\mathbb{E}_{\StrategyProfile}[G_\gamma]\le \xi.
$
\end{restatable}
Proof is available in Appendix~\ref{appsec:proof_gamma_epsilon_discount_bias}.
\subsection{PAC-Style Guarantees by Sampling} \label{sec:sampling_guarantees}
We give finite-sample certificates for probabilistic constraints $\Prob_{\bowtie q}[\varphi]$ under a \emph{fixed} strategy profile $\StrategyProfile$ in a finite-horizon setting. Since the dynamics are unknown, we estimate $p$ from rollouts of the fixed profile and bound the estimation error. Such certificates are needed because convergence to an equilibrium does not ensure that probabilistic thresholds are met.

Fix $\mathcal M$, a start state $s$, a path formula $\varphi$, and a strategy profile $\StrategyProfile$. Let $p \ \defeq \ \Pr^{\StrategyProfile}_{\mathcal M,s}(\varphi) \: \in[0,1]$. Consider $n$ i.i.d.\ rollouts (episodes): $\pi_1,\dots,\pi_n \sim \Pr^{\StrategyProfile}_{\mathcal M,s}$ and define the binary satisfaction indicators
$
X_j \ \defeq \ \mathbf 1[\mathcal M,\pi_j\models \varphi] \in\{0,1\},
$
where $j=1,\dots,n$ so that $\mathbb E[X_j]=p$. Let $\hat p \defeq  \frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^n X_j$.

\begin{restatable}[Hoeffding Confidence Interval for Satisfaction Probability]{theorem}{hoeffdingcithm}
\label{thm:hoeffding_ci}
For any confidence parameter $\delta\in(0,1)$, define the half-width
$
\rho_n(\delta) \ \defeq \ \sqrt{\frac{\ln(2/\delta)}{2n}},
$
and the clipped bounds
\[
L_n \ \defeq \ \max\{0,\hat p-\rho_n(\delta)\},
\qquad
U_n \ \defeq \ \min\{1,\hat p+\rho_n(\delta)\}.
\]
Then
$
\Pr\!\big(p \in [L_n,U_n]\big)\ \ge\ 1-\delta,
$
where the probability is over the randomness of the $n$ sampled rollouts.
\end{restatable}
Proof is available in Appendix~\ref{appsec:proof_hoeffding_ci}. Since $\hat p$ is the fraction of the $n$ rollouts that satisfy $\varphi$, Theorem~\ref{thm:hoeffding_ci} states that $|\hat p-p|\le\rho_n(\delta)$ with probability at least $1-\delta$, without knowledge of $\mathcal P$, where $\rho_n(\delta)=O(n^{-1/2})$. 

\begin{restatable}[One-Sided Certification of Threshold Constraints]{corollary}{thresholdcertcor}
\label{cor:threshold_cert}
Fix a threshold $q\in[0,1]$. Under the assumptions of Theorem~\ref{thm:hoeffding_ci}, with probability at least $1-\delta$ over the validation rollouts, all four implications hold:
\[
\begin{aligned}
L_n>q &\ \Longrightarrow\ p>q, & U_n<q &\ \Longrightarrow\ p<q,\\
L_n\ge q &\ \Longrightarrow\ p\ge q, & U_n\le q &\ \Longrightarrow\ p\le q.
\end{aligned}
\]
Thus the probability of issuing any false threshold certificate is at most $\delta$.
\end{restatable}
Proof is available in Appendix~\ref{appsec:proof_threshold_cert}. Corollary~\ref{cor:threshold_cert} certifies $\Prob_{\ge q}[\varphi]$ only when $L_n\ge q$, and symmetrically for upper bounds. If $L_n<q\le U_n$, the sample is inconclusive rather than evidence that $p<q$.

\begin{restatable}[Joint Certification via Bonferroni Adjustment]{corollary}{jointconfidencecertcor}
\label{cor:joint_confidence_cert}
Fix an objective profile
$\ObjVec=(\Obj_1,\dots,\Obj_m)$, where
$\Obj_i=\Prob_{\bowtie_i q_i}[\PathObj_i]$, and a fixed strategy profile $\StrategyProfile$.
Before validation, fix sample sizes $n_i\ge1$ and failure budgets $\delta_i\in(0,1)$ such that $\sum_{i=1}^m\delta_i\le\delta$ for a joint budget $\delta\in(0,1)$.
For each $i$, let
$p_i=\Pr^{\StrategyProfile}_{\mathcal M,s}(\PathObj_i)$ and construct a Hoeffding interval
$[L_i,U_i]$ as in Theorem~\ref{thm:hoeffding_ci}, from $n_i$ i.i.d.\ rollouts and with marginal failure probability $\delta_i$.
Then
\[
\Pr\!\left(\bigcap_{i=1}^m\{p_i\in[L_i,U_i]\}\right)\ge 1-\delta.
\]
Consequently, with probability at least $1-\delta$, all endpoint-based certificates from Corollary~\ref{cor:threshold_cert} are simultaneously sound. In particular, if, for every $i$, the relevant condition
\[
\begin{aligned}
&L_i>q_i \ (\bowtie_i={>}), &\qquad& U_i<q_i \ (\bowtie_i={<}),\\
&L_i\ge q_i \ (\bowtie_i={\ge}), && U_i\le q_i \ (\bowtie_i={\le})
\end{aligned}
\]
holds, then $\mathcal M,s,\StrategyProfile\models\ObjVec$ is jointly certified with confidence at least $1-\delta$.
The uniform Bonferroni choice $\delta_i=\delta/m$ gives half-width
$
\rho_{n_i}(\delta/m)=\sqrt{\ln(2m/\delta)/(2n_i)}.
$
The rollouts used for different objectives need not be independent. In particular, the same rollouts may be evaluated against every $\PathObj_i$.
\end{restatable}
Proof is available in Appendix~\ref{appsec:proof_joint_confidence_cert}. Corollary~\ref{cor:joint_confidence_cert} certifies all agents' constraints simultaneously for a fixed profile, regardless of how it was learned when validation uses fresh i.i.d\ rollouts. 

\paragraph{Boundary Probabilities.}
At $p=q$, strict requirements ($p>q$ or $p<q$) are false, while non-strict requirements ($p\ge q$ or $p\le q$) hold but leave no margin for separating the confidence bound from the threshold using finitely many samples. A useful additional feasibility condition, analogous to~\cite{ohlmann2025synthesis}, is satisfaction with positive slack:
\begin{definition}[Threshold Strengthening]\label{def:threshold_strengthening}
For a positive rational $\eta$, let $T(\psi,\eta)$ replace $\Prob_{\ge d}$ by $\Prob_{\ge d+\eta}$ and $\Prob_{\le d}$ by $\Prob_{\le d-\eta}$ (and similarly for $>$ and $<$), provided the strengthened threshold remains in $[0,1]$.
\end{definition}
Definition~\ref{def:threshold_strengthening} moves each threshold in the more demanding direction.
\begin{assumption}[Generalised Slater Condition]\label{ass:gslater}
There exist a positive rational $\eta$ and a strategy profile $\StrategyProfile^\star\in\Sigma$ such that, for every agent $i$, $T(\Obj_i,\eta)$ is defined and $\mathcal M,s_0,\StrategyProfile^\star\models T(\Obj_i,\eta)$.
\end{assumption}
Assumption~\ref{ass:gslater} expresses joint feasibility with positive slack. It neither places the witness at equilibrium nor ensures that learning finds it. Rather, it ensures the existence of a profile whose thresholds hold with a positive certification margin.

\subsection{Existence of Equilibria} \label{sec:equilibria_existence}
For infinite-horizon concurrent stochastic games with qualitative objectives, equilibrium existence is only known for certain low levels of the Borel hierarchy (e.g., safety objectives). For example, existence of $\EpsNE$ for general $n$-player concurrent reachability games remains an open question~\cite{Chatterjee2007ErrataCSL04}. In the finite-horizon case, however, existence is classical: every finite extensive-form game with perfect recall has a subgame-perfect equilibrium~\cite{selten1975reexamination}, and a Markov one is obtained by backward induction, solving at each state and remaining time the one-shot stage game whose payoffs are the continuation values, which has a mixed equilibrium by Nash's theorem~\cite{Nash1950} (see e.g.,~\cite{fudenberg1991game}). We restate this standard result for our setting in Proposition~\ref{prp:spne_finite_horizon}:
\begin{restatable}[Existence of $\SPNE$ in Finite Horizon~\cite{selten1975reexamination}]{proposition}{spnefinitehorizonprop}
\label{prp:spne_finite_horizon}
Every fully observed concurrent stochastic game with finite state and action spaces, Markovian stage and terminal rewards, and horizon bounded by $H$ admits a \emph{subgame-perfect Nash equilibrium} ($\SPNE$) in randomised strategies that is \emph{Markov}, i.e.\ each agent's strategy depends only on the current state and the remaining time $H-t$. In particular, this holds for the fully observed product game $\widehat{\mathcal M}_H$ with utilities from Definition~\ref{def:objective_utilities}.
\end{restatable}
Proof in Appendix~\ref{appsec:proof_spne_finite_horizon}. Proposition~\ref{prp:spne_finite_horizon} ensures an equilibrium in the unrestricted class of randomised policies over the fully observed product state. However, existence does not guarantee feasibility for agent $i$ in Definition~\ref{def:rational_synthesis}: $\mathcal E_\zeta$ may be nonempty while $\mathcal F_\zeta^i$ is empty.

\subsection{Convergence Guarantees}
Many of the methods considered in our experiments, including \algo{IPPO}, \algo{IQL}, and \algo{PR2}, have no general guarantee of convergence to Nash equilibria under non-linear function approximation and concurrent policy updates~\cite{tsitsiklis1996analysis}. We instead analyse tabular \algo{Nash-Q}~\cite{hu2003nash}, which backs up equilibrium payoffs of successor-state games.

In finite horizon, convergence proceeds backwards from terminal outcomes as continuation values stabilise. The subtlety is equilibrium selection: when several Nash equilibria exist, small estimation errors may change which one \algo{Nash-Q} chooses. We therefore require vanishing errors in the stage-game payoffs to yield vanishing errors in the selected continuation values.

We use the fully observed product $\widehat{\mathcal M}_H$ of Definition~\ref{def:product_stochastic_game}. A state is \emph{reachable} if it has positive probability under some legal joint action sequence from $x_0$. At each non-terminal $x$, a stage game $g$ assigns a payoff vector in $[0,1]^n$ to each $\bm a\in d_H(x)$. In particular, $Q(x)$ denotes the game $\bm a\mapsto Q(x,\bm a)$.
\begin{definition}[Selector and Selector Profile]\label{def:selector_profile}
A \emph{selector} $\Sel$ maps each non-terminal state $x$ and stage game $g$ at $x$ to a Nash equilibrium $\Sel_x(g)$ of $g$, with value $\Val_x(g)\defeq \mathbb E_{\bm a\sim\Sel_x(g)}[g(\bm a)]\in[0,1]^n$. Its \emph{selector profile} $\StrategyProfile_{\Sel}$ is obtained by backward induction: $V^{\Sel}(x)\defeq R(x)$ for terminal $x$, and, at each non-terminal $x$ in time layers $t=H-1,\dots,0$,
\[
\begin{aligned}
Q^{\Sel}(x,\bm a)&\defeq \sum_{x'}\tilde{\mathcal P}(x'\mid x,\bm a)\,V^{\Sel}(x'),\\
\StrategyProfile_{\Sel}(x)&\defeq \Sel_x\big(Q^{\Sel}(x)\big),\qquad
V^{\Sel}(x)\defeq \Val_x\big(Q^{\Sel}(x)\big).
\end{aligned}
\]
\end{definition}
Definition~\ref{def:selector_profile} states the equilibrium choices used throughout backward induction. Intuitively, the selector fixes one Nash-equilibrium continuation at each state when several are available, and backward induction propagates the corresponding payoff vector to earlier states. In our experiments, we use a selector that ranks the equilibria returned by the stage-game solver by their total expected Q-value. Ties favour the equilibrium with the highest minimum individual value.

\algo{Nash-Q} with selector $\Sel$ maintains $Q^k(x,\bm a)\in\mathbb R^n$ and, after observing a transition $(x,\bm a,x')$ at iteration $k$, updates
$
Q^{k+1}(x,\bm a)=\big(1-\alpha_k(x,\bm a)\big)Q^k(x,\bm a)+\alpha_k(x,\bm a)\,V^k(x'),
$
where $V^k(x')=R(x')$ if $x'$ is terminal and $V^k(x')=\Val_{x'}(Q^k(x'))$ otherwise. All other entries are unchanged ($\alpha_k=0$). At non-terminal states, the extracted profile is $\StrategyProfile_k(x)\defeq \Sel_x(Q^k(x))$.

\begin{restatable}[Convergence of Nash-Q Values and Exploitability]{theorem}{nashqconvergencethm}
\label{thm:nashq_convergence}
Let $\Sel$ be a selector, and suppose that (i) $Q^0(x,\bm a)\in[0,1]^n$ for all $(x,\bm a)$; (ii) for every reachable non-terminal $x$ and $\bm a\in d_H(x)$, the step sizes satisfy $\alpha_k(x,\bm a)\in[0,1]$, $\sum_k\alpha_k(x,\bm a)=\infty$ and $\sum_k\alpha_k(x,\bm a)^2<\infty$ almost surely; (iii) each $\Sel_x$ is Borel measurable, the updated pair and step sizes are measurable functions of the past, and, conditional on that past, the successor is sampled from $\tilde{\mathcal P}(\cdot\mid x,\bm a)$; and (iv) for every reachable non-terminal $x$, $\Val_x$ is continuous at $Q^{\Sel}(x)$, which holds in particular if $\Sel_x$ is continuous at $Q^{\Sel}(x)$ or if all Nash equilibria of $Q^{\Sel}(x)$ have the same value vector. Then $\StrategyProfile_{\Sel}$ is a Markov $\SPNE$ of $\widehat{\mathcal M}_H$ and, almost surely, $Q^k\to Q^{\Sel}$ on all reachable non-terminal states, $u_i(s_0,\StrategyProfile_k)\to u_i(s_0,\StrategyProfile_{\Sel})$ for every agent $i$, and $\Exploit(s_0,\StrategyProfile_k)\to0$ (Definition~\ref{def:exploitability}). In particular, for every $\varepsilon>0$, $\StrategyProfile_k$ is an $\EpsNE$ for all sufficiently large $k$. If moreover $\Sel_x$ is continuous at $Q^{\Sel}(x)$ for every reachable non-terminal $x$, then almost surely $\StrategyProfile_k\to\StrategyProfile_{\Sel}$ on all reachable non-terminal states.
\end{restatable}
Proof in Appendix~\ref{appsec:proof_nashq_convergence}. Theorem~\ref{thm:nashq_convergence} assumes that the selected payoff map $\Val_x$ is continuous at $Q^{\Sel}(x)$. Together with (i)--(iii), this yields convergence of Q-values and payoffs and vanishing exploitability. The stronger assumption that $\Sel_x$ is continuous there also yields convergence of action probabilities. Payoff continuity alone allows switching between equilibria with the same payoff vector.

Since ties are broken only after maximising the total expected Q-value, the resulting backup may satisfy fewer thresholds than another available equilibrium if high values for some agents compensate for low values for others. Hence, equilibrium convergence does not ensure satisfaction of the probabilistic thresholds, which must be checked using Corollary~\ref{cor:threshold_cert} (or Corollary~\ref{cor:joint_confidence_cert} for joint certification).

\section{Experimental Analysis}
\label{sec:experimental_eval}

We evaluate Algorithm~\ref{alg:approach}, targeting $\zeta=\EpsNE$ with $\varepsilon=0.05$ (Definition~\ref{def:eps_ne}) as a practical tolerance for unilateral improvements. Improvements below this tolerance are treated as practically negligible relative to finite-sample payoff estimation, approximate best-response training, and stochastic learning dynamics. Appendix~\ref{appsec:hyperparams} provides full hyperparameters, and our code is available at \url{https://github.com/nightly/learning-rational-strategies} for reproducibility.

