\begin{abstract}
    Given a joint distribution $P_{XY}$ on a finite alphabet, the
    information bottleneck method seeks a representation $T$ of $X$
    that is as informative as possible about $Y$ while compressing $X$,
    where $T$ and $Y$ are conditionally independent given $X$. We show
    that computing the optimal value
    $\IB(P_{XY},R) = \sup_T \{ I(Y;T) : I(X;T) \le R \}$ of the information
    bottleneck problem, as well as its Lagrangian relaxation
    $L_{\beta}(P_{XY}) = \sup_T \big[I(Y;T) - \beta I(X;T)\big]$, is
    NP-hard. Moreover, unless $\mathrm{P} = \mathrm{NP}$, neither $\IB(P_{XY},R)$ nor
    $L_{\beta}(P_{XY})$
    can be approximated in polynomial time within an additive error of
    order $|\cX|^{-3/2}$.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}


Extracting compact representations that preserve information relevant to a
task is a central problem in information theory, statistics, and machine
learning. The information bottleneck (IB) method, introduced by Tishby,
Pereira and Bialek in 1999~\cite{TPB}\footnote{However, we should note 
that an equivalent optimization problem  has previously been studied in the literature on information theory 
\cite{WZ,W, WW}.}, formulates this problem as a
trade-off between compressing an observed random variable $X$ and retaining
information about a target variable $Y$. 

Applications of IB include clustering and document
classification~\cite{TPB}, as well as the design of representations for
neural classifiers and generative models~\cite{GP}. Its operational
interpretation through remote source coding under logarithmic loss,
together with extensions to distributed learning and communication,
connects these applications to information-theoretic performance
limits~\cite{Z+}. The IB tradeoff thus serves both as an objective for
constructing representations and as a benchmark for assessing their
quality.
The vast literature on the Information Bottleneck problem and of its numerous
applications cannot be synthesized here; we refer the reader to the excellent review papers
\cite{As+,GP,Hu+,Z+,ZL}.

Formally, for a joint distribution $P_{XY}$ on finite alphabets
$\cX\times\cY$ and a  budget $R\geq 0$, the IB problem corresponds to the following optimization problem:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:IB_def}
    \IB(P_{XY},R)
    =\sup_{\substack{P_{T\mid X}:\,T-X-Y\\ I(X;T)\leq R}} I(Y;T).
\end{equation}
The supremum ranges over all finite representation alphabets $\cT$ and
stochastic encoders $P_{T\mid X}$; the Markov condition $T-X-Y$ means
that $T$ and $Y$ are conditionally independent given $X$. The quantities
$I(X;T)$ and $I(Y;T)$ measure the information retained about the input
and the target, respectively. We also consider the Lagrangian formulation
\begin{equation}\label{eq:L_beta_def}
    L_{\beta}(P_{XY})
    =\sup_{P_{T\mid X}:\,T-X-Y}
    \bigl[I(Y;T)-\beta I(X;T)\bigr],
    \qquad \beta>0.
\end{equation}
All information quantities are measured in bits.


The authors of~\cite{TPB}  propose an Arimoto-Blahut
iterative algorithm to compute \eqref{eq:L_beta_def}. They prove convergence,
but do not establish that a global optimum can be computed in
polynomial time. A basic question is therefore whether the optimal values
in~\eqref{eq:IB_def} and~\eqref{eq:L_beta_def} admit efficient computation
or approximation with provable accuracy for arbitrary finite input
distributions.

To the best of our knowledge, 
exact characterizations of the information bottleneck curve are known in a handful of cases only.
 For binary sources observed through a
binary symmetric channel, the exact tradeoff follows from Mrs.~Gerber's
lemma~\cite{WZ}; extensions cover binary-input memoryless symmetric
channels with uniform input~\cite{CS1989,SSZ2005}.
For nonbinary Hamming channels with uniform input,
Benger, Asoodeh and Chen~\cite{BAC2023} characterize the entire curve as
the upper concave envelope of an explicit scalar function.
Other exact cases include discrete deterministic targets $Y=f(X)$,
for which
$\IB(P_{XY},R)=\min\{R,H(Y)\}$ for all $R\ge 0$
\cite[Sec.~3]{KTV2019},
and when $Y$ is obtained from $X$ through an erasure channel
with erasure probability $\varepsilon$, independently of the
input symbol. In this case,
$\IB(P_{XY},R)=(1-\varepsilon)R$ for $0\le R\le H(X)$,
as stated in~\cite[Lemma~1]{As+}.
In the continuous setting, Chechik \textit{et al}.~\cite{CGTW2005} obtain an analytic
solution for jointly Gaussian vectors, with optimal representations given
by noisy linear projections onto canonical-correlation directions.
Dikshtein, Weinberger and Shamai~\cite{DWS2022} determine the optimal
asymptotic relevance rate for jointly stationary Gaussian processes through
spectral water filling.

The above exact solutions exploit structural assumptions
on the joint distribution, whereas the present work addresses the
computational complexity of the problem for arbitrary finite-alphabet
distributions.
More precisely, we establish computational hardness for both formulations \eqref{eq:IB_def} and \eqref{eq:L_beta_def}.
We prove that their threshold decision problems are NP-hard,  the NP-hardness of
\eqref{eq:L_beta_def} is first  obtained with the
Lagrangian multiplier fixed at $\beta=1/2$, then extended to general rational $\beta\in (0,1)$. Moreover, unless
$\mathrm{P}=\mathrm{NP}$, no polynomial-time algorithm can approximate
$L_{\beta}(P_{XY})$ within additive error $1/(216\,|\cX|^{3/2})$, or
$\IB(P_{XY},R)$ within additive error $1/(576\,|\cX|^{3/2})$
(Theorems~\ref{thm:IBL_hard} and~\ref{thm:IBT_hard}). These results hold
in the standard bit model of computation, with rational input
distributions and rational rate budgets.

\remove{Both reductions from \clique{} use a distribution in which $X$ is uniform
on oriented graph edges and $Y$ is a uniformly chosen endpoint of $X$.
