The Information Bottleneck (IB) problem, introduced by Tishby, Pereira and
Bialek~\cite{TPB}, consists in extracting from an input random variable $X$ a compressed representation $T$ that retains as much information as possible about a target variable $Y$. More formally, given a joint distribution $P_{XY}$ of $X$ and $Y$ over a finite alphabet $\cX\times\cY$, and a budget $R\geq 0$, the IB problem corresponds to the following optimization problem:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:IB_def}
    \IB(P_{XY}, R)=\sup_{\substack{P_{T\mid X}:T-X-Y\\
    I(X;T)\leq R}} I(Y;T),
\end{equation}
where $T$ is a random variable on a finite alphabet $\cT$ such that $T-X-Y$ is a Markov chain, that is, $T$ and $Y$ are conditionally independent given $X$. We also observe that, since a constant random variable $T$ is a feasible solution, the supremum in~\eqref{eq:IB_def} always has a finite value.

Throughout the paper, since the random variable $T$ is a compression of $X$, we often call $T$ an \textit{encoder} of $X$.

A natural relaxation of the problem~\eqref{eq:IB_def} through the Lagrangian with multiplier $\beta>0$ yields the following optimization problem:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:L_beta_def}
    L_{\beta}(P_{XY})=\sup_{P_{T\mid X}:T-X-Y} [I(Y;T)-\beta I(X;T)].
\end{equation}
Both~\eqref{eq:IB_def} and~\eqref{eq:L_beta_def} have been quite studied in the literature. Thus, it is natural to ask whether $\IB(P_{XY}, R)$ and $L_{\beta}(P_{XY})$ can be computed, or approximated, efficiently. In this work, we demonstrate that computing both~\eqref{eq:IB_def} and~\eqref{eq:L_beta_def}, with $\beta=1/2$, is NP-hard. Moreover, unless $\text{P}=\text{NP}$, no polynomial-time
algorithm can approximate $L_{1/2}(P_{XY})$ within an additive error of
$1/(216|\cX|^{3/2})$, or $\IB(P_{XY},R)$ within an additive error of
$1/(576|\cX|^{3/2})$, where $\cX$ is the alphabet of $X$.
