\firstsection{Introduction}
\maketitle

{\let\thefootnote\relax\footnote{Arjun Dadhwal and Brian Summa are with Tulane University. E-mail: author@example.org, author@example.org. This article extends work accepted as a poster at TopoInVis Connect 2026. Relative to the reviewed workshop submission, it computes persistence-diagram distances with explicit implementations of the standard bottleneck and order-2 Wasserstein definitions, uses a corrected implementation of fixed-pair supervision, and adds the full loss study and additional validation.}}Scientific super-resolution (SR) is commonly optimized and evaluated using pointwise reconstruction fidelity, even though scientific interpretation often depends on connected regions, extrema, and structures that evolve across scalar thresholds. In wind-field applications, reconstructing such fine-scale structure supports renewable-energy assessment, extreme-event characterization, atmospheric analysis, and scientific visualization. However, many widely available datasets remain too coarse to resolve local ridges, high-speed regions, and connected structures directly~\cite{Stengel2020,Buster2024,Ding2025}.

Deep learning methods, including convolutional neural networks (CNNs) and generative adversarial networks (GANs), can substantially improve spatial resolution. Their evaluation remains difficult, however, because conventional measures such as peak signal-to-noise ratio (PSNR), mean absolute error (MAE), root-mean-square error (RMSE), and structural similarity (SSIM) primarily quantify pointwise or local reconstruction fidelity. These measures do not necessarily determine whether a reconstructed field preserves the scalar structures most relevant to scientific interpretation.

This limitation is related to the broader perception--distortion trade-off. CNNs trained primarily with reconstruction losses often produce conservative and comparatively smooth estimates with strong pointwise accuracy. Adversarial models can generate sharper and more spatially structured outputs, but they may also introduce unsupported fragmentation or visually plausible features absent from the ground truth. Blau and Michaeli~\cite{Blau2018} formalize the conflict between minimizing distortion and maximizing perceptual quality, while scientific-downscaling studies report corresponding disagreements between numerical-error rankings and visual, spatial-frequency, or distributional assessments. Stengel et al.~\cite{Stengel2020}, for example, observe that adversarial wind and solar reconstructions can appear sharper and more spatially detailed than CNN outputs even when pixelwise measures favor the CNN. H\"ohlein et al.~\cite{Hoehlein2020} likewise discuss limitations of MSE-based wind-field evaluation, noting that MSE can overweight high-speed magnitude errors while underweighting directional errors in low-speed regions. Annau and Cannon~\cite{Annau2023} further show that GAN-based wind downscaling can generate plausible fine-scale structures that need not coincide with the exact high-resolution reference, and that pixelwise metrics such as MAE/MSE can disagree with spatial-frequency and distributional measures of generated-field quality.

Topological descriptors provide a complementary means of examining such reconstructions. For a scalar field, a persistence diagram (PD) summarizes critical-point pairings and the scalar-value intervals over which corresponding features persist. A merge tree (MT) additionally represents how connected components evolve and merge across scalar thresholds. The two descriptors are therefore related but not interchangeable: persistence diagrams emphasize feature lifetimes, whereas merge trees preserve more of the connectivity and hierarchical organization of the scalar field~\cite{Yan2021}. Prior work has incorporated topological information into neural objectives for reconstruction, segmentation, representation learning, and scalar-field interpolation~\cite{Gabrielsson2020,Hu2019,Moor2020,Kissi2025} and has applied topology-guided analysis to wind-speed fields~\cite{Qin2023}. Nevertheless, topology-inspired supervision remains comparatively underexplored in scientific SR.

We investigate this question using pretrained CNN and GAN models from the Physics-Informed Resolution-Enhancing (PhIRE) framework for climatological wind and solar SR introduced by Stengel et al.~\cite{Stengel2020}. The original data contain horizontal wind components $u$ and $v$; for scalar topological analysis, we evaluate the corresponding wind-speed magnitude. We first compare the pretrained models on a fixed benchmark of 168 fields, held out from all fine-tuning, using a broad evaluation suite spanning direct fidelity, physics-oriented errors, spectral and distributional structure, and topology.

The baseline comparison reveals a descriptor-dependent trade-off. The CNN is preferred on PSNR for all 168 fields and on speed SSIM for 165 of 168 fields, and is generally stronger on direct wind-speed and wind-power-density errors. The GAN is more competitive on frequency-domain behavior, gradient distributions, and the most extreme exceedance measures, consistent with its tendency to produce sharper and more variable structure. The topological evaluators do not agree with one another either. MT distance favors the CNN on 148 of 168 fields, and bottleneck PD distance, which is governed by the single worst-matched feature, also favors the CNN (129 of 168). In contrast, the order-2 Wasserstein PD distances, which accumulate mismatch over all persistence pairs, favor the GAN on 155 and 158 fields. This pattern is consistent with the sharper GAN better matching the aggregate distribution of ground-truth birth--death pairs while incurring larger individual discrepancies and preserving the merge hierarchy less consistently. Neither conventional fidelity measures nor any single topological distance provides a complete account of reconstruction quality.

These observations motivate the central question of this work: can a fidelity-oriented CNN be fine-tuned toward persistence-relevant scalar structure while retaining strong conventional fidelity? We approach this question through a loss study rather than a single proposed loss. Starting from the pretrained CNN, we fine-tune with differentiable auxiliary losses that target different aspects of scalar structure: scalar speed, gradient magnitude, soft high-speed level sets, high-speed local maxima, and \emph{fixed-pair supervision}, which uses the Topology ToolKit (TTK)~\cite{Tierny2018} offline to locate the endpoints of ground-truth persistence pairs and penalizes errors in the predicted values and persistence at those fixed locations. None of these losses computes a persistence diagram or merge tree of the prediction; PD and MT distances are used only to evaluate the trained models.

Every configuration is compared with a matched control that is fine-tuned identically (same initialization, data, optimizer, and schedule) but with the reconstruction loss alone; we call it the standard fine-tuning control. Differences from this control therefore measure the effect of the auxiliary losses rather than that of continued fine-tuning. We trained and report 16 configurations in total, including all eight combinations of the speed, gradient, and level-set losses, which allows the contribution of each term and their interactions to be estimated. The study identifies gradient supervision and fixed-pair supervision as complementary signals, and we use their combination as the final objective.

The final objective lowers all three PD distances relative to both the pretrained CNN (by 25--31\% in the mean) and the matched control (by 32--35\%), whereas standard fine-tuning on its own makes all three worse; all three PD distances are lower than the CNN's on 150 of the 168 benchmark fields. The final model retains about half of the PSNR gain of standard fine-tuning. Mean MT distance also improves, but MT gets worse on 59 of the 150 fields whose PD distances all improve, indicating that PD and MT measure complementary information.

This work provides a controlled proof of concept for topology-inspired supervision in scientific SR. Its principal contributions are:

\begin{itemize}
\item a characterization of descriptor-dependent disagreement in wind-field SR, showing that pretrained CNN and GAN reconstructions are ranked differently by fidelity, physics-oriented, merge-tree, and even different persistence-diagram distances, which motivates topology-aware supervision of the fidelity-oriented CNN;

\item a family of lightweight, differentiable topology-inspired losses for fine-tuning an SR network, culminating in an objective that combines gradient-magnitude supervision with fixed-pair supervision at TTK-identified ground-truth persistence-pair endpoints;

\item a controlled comparison with fine-tuning on the reconstruction loss alone, showing that the persistence improvements are not explained by continued fine-tuning;

\item a fully reported loss study of 16 configurations with factorial analyses, showing that gradient supervision produces the largest PD improvement among the scalar-field losses but does not improve MT agreement, whereas fixed-pair supervision gives the largest and most consistent joint PD and MT improvement; and

\item an evaluation with three PD distances and MT distance, using a validated PD-distance implementation, showing that PD and MT improvements are frequently decoupled at the level of individual fields and therefore capture complementary information.
\end{itemize}

The remainder of the paper reviews wind-field SR and scalar-field topology, describes the data and controlled fine-tuning design, introduces the auxiliary losses and the loss study, and evaluates their effects using fidelity, physics-oriented, PD, and MT measures.

\section{Background and Related Work}

\subsection{Wind-field super-resolution and evaluation}
Stengel et al.~\cite{Stengel2020} introduced the PhIRE framework for large-factor SR of climatological wind and solar data using CNN and GAN models. Subsequent work has explored transformer, diffusion, and generative approaches for wind-field downscaling and renewable-energy applications~\cite{Tang2024,Merizzi2024,Buster2024,Ding2025}. Complementary work on constrained climate downscaling has shown that physical consistency constraints can be built into neural SR models, motivating evaluation beyond visual or pointwise fidelity~\cite{harder2024hardconstraineddeeplearningclimate}. At the same time, prior studies have documented disagreement between pointwise numerical errors and visual, spatial-frequency, or distributional assessments~\cite{Hoehlein2020,Annau2023}. Such disagreement is especially consequential for scientific fields, where sharper output can represent either recovered physical structure or model-induced artifacts. We therefore evaluate not only PSNR and SSIM, but also wind-power-density (WPD), spectral, gradient, exceedance, component-structure, and topological measures.

Beyond meteorological downscaling, super-resolution has been studied directly for scientific vector-field visualization. Here, spatial SR estimates a higher-resolution field from a coarser one at the same time instant, whereas temporal SR reconstructs missing intermediate time steps. Guo et al.~\cite{Guo2020SSRVFD} proposed SSR-VFD for spatial SR of vector fields, An et al.~\cite{An2021STSRNet} joint space--time SR, and Han and Wang~\cite{Han2022TSRVFD} temporal SR of unsteady vector fields; these methods aim to reconstruct vector-valued data in a form useful for downstream visualization and analysis. Our setting is complementary. The SR network still reconstructs the complete wind vector $[u,v]$, and topology-inspired supervision is applied to the derived scalar wind speed in order to study persistence and merge hierarchy.

\subsection{Scalar filtrations and topological descriptors}
\label{sec:topology-background}
Let $s:\Omega\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$ denote a scalar wind-speed field on a spatial domain $\Omega$. Its topology can be studied by sweeping a scalar threshold through the field. The sublevel and superlevel sets are
\begin{equation}
    S^-_{\tau}=\{x\in\Omega\mid s(x)\leq\tau\},
    \qquad
    S^+_{\tau}=\{x\in\Omega\mid s(x)\geq\tau\}.
\end{equation}
As $\tau$ changes, connected components appear and merge only at critical values. Sublevel filtrations emphasize low-valued basins; superlevel filtrations provide the dual view of high-valued peaks and regions. Several of our auxiliary losses target high-speed regions and local maxima, which are most intuitive from the superlevel view, in which a prominent maximum gives birth to a high-speed component. All reported topological descriptors, and the persistence pairs used by fixed-pair supervision, instead use the sublevel convention of our TTK pipeline. The two views are dual: in a sublevel filtration of a two-dimensional field, a local maximum does not create a component but closes a cycle~\cite{EdelsbrunnerHarer2010}. Under our sublevel convention, maxima therefore appear as death endpoints of finite saddle--maximum pairs ($D_1$), one of the two pair families used in our evaluation (Sec.~\ref{sec:evaluation}). The high-speed extrema targeted by our local-maximum loss thus overlap conceptually with persistence-relevant critical values, although not every maximum ends a long-lived pair.

\paragraph{Persistence diagrams.}
A persistence diagram records birth--death pairs
\begin{equation}
    D(s)=\{(b_i,d_i)\}_{i=1}^{n},
\end{equation}
where the persistence $|d_i-b_i|$ measures the scalar interval over which a feature remains present. Points close to the diagonal $b=d$ represent short-lived features, whereas points farther from it represent more persistent structure. PDs compactly preserve paired critical values but not the complete ancestry describing which components merge into one another, nor where in the domain a feature lies.

We compare diagrams with three distances. The bottleneck distance
\begin{equation}
 d_B(D_1,D_2)=
 \inf_{\gamma}\sup_{p\in D_1}\lVert p-\gamma(p)\rVert_{\infty},
 \label{eq:bottleneck}
\end{equation}
where $\gamma$ ranges over matchings between diagram points, including matches to the diagonal, is controlled by the single largest discrepancy under an optimal matching. The order-2 Wasserstein distance with ground norm $\lVert\cdot\rVert_q$,
\begin{equation}
 W_{2,q}(D_1,D_2)=
 \Bigl(\inf_{\gamma}\sum_{p\in D_1}\lVert p-\gamma(p)\rVert_{q}^{2}\Bigr)^{1/2},
 \qquad q\in\{\infty,2\},
 \label{eq:wasserstein}
\end{equation}
instead accumulates squared discrepancies over all matched pairs. We report both $\Wi$ ($L_\infty$ ground norm, matching the bottleneck geometry) and $\Wt$ (Euclidean ground norm); the two differ only in the ground norm, not in the outer order. Because ``$W_2$'' is used for both conventions in the literature, we always write both indices. Because $d_B$ measures the worst-case mismatch and $W_{2,q}$ the accumulated mismatch, they can rank reconstructions differently: a field that matches the bulk of the diagram well but misplaces one prominent feature can have a low Wasserstein and a high bottleneck distance, and vice versa.

\paragraph{Merge trees.}
A merge tree retains the connected-component evolution together with the hierarchy of merge events. Leaves represent extrema at which components are created, internal nodes represent saddles at which components merge, and edges encode scalar intervals between events. Consequently, an MT can distinguish fields that have similar birth--death pairs but different ancestry, branch order, or connectivity~\cite{Yan2021,PontPGA_2022,PontW2_2021}.

