Recent work emphasizes that descriptor choice and distance choice both affect scalar-field comparison. Wasserstein formulations support merge-tree geodesics, barycenters, and representation learning~\cite{PontW2_2021,PontWAE_2023,WetzelsPath_2023}; complementary approaches study stable, geometry-aware, branch-independent, and deformation-based comparisons~\cite{Bollen_2023,YanGeometryAware_2021,WetzelsBranch_2022,WetzelsDeformation_2022}. These developments motivate treating PD and MT evaluations, and different distances on the same descriptor, as related but distinct evidence rather than interchangeable notions of a single ``topological quality.''

\subsection{Topology in neural learning}
\label{sec:related-learning}
Persistent topology has been incorporated into neural models through differentiable layers, regularizers, and task-specific losses. Br\"uel-Gabrielsson et al.~\cite{Gabrielsson2020} demonstrate differentiation through persistence for reconstruction and generative-model regularization, while Hu et al.~\cite{Hu2019} and Clough et al.~\cite{Clough_2022} use differentiable topological losses to reduce connectivity or shape errors in image and volume segmentation. Moor et al.~\cite{Moor2020} preserve multiscale connectivity in autoencoder latent spaces, and Kazeminia et al.~\cite{Kazeminia2026TGMIL} use a topological inductive bias to improve multiple-instance learning under data-scarce conditions. Explicit optimization has also been investigated for scalar-data simplification~\cite{KissiSimplification_2024}, and efficient persistence computation is an active topic in scientific visualization~\cite{GuillouDMS_2023}. Learned representations have been proposed for efficient merge-tree comparison; Qin et al.~\cite{QinMTNN2024} approximate MT similarity with graph neural networks and topological attention.

The critical-value component of our fixed-pair supervision is closely related to the topology-aware interpolation framework of Kissi et al.~\cite{Kissi2025}. Their training proceeds in two phases. The first combines reconstruction and gradient losses with a critical-value loss that constrains predicted values at the birth and death vertices of the target persistence pairs identified by TTK. The second keeps the reconstruction and critical-value terms, drops the gradient term, and adds a Wasserstein-type persistence-diagram loss: at each iteration the prediction's diagram is recomputed with TTK and matched to the target diagram, prediction pairs without a counterpart are pushed toward the diagonal, and the matched birth--death cost is differentiated with respect to the predicted values at the prediction's critical vertices. Our critical-value term $\Lcv$ closely follows their first-phase constraint. We differ in three respects. First, we add a persistence term $\Lpers$ that directly constrains the scalar contrast between the two endpoints of each supervised pair. Second, we do not recompute or re-match the prediction's persistence diagram during training: supervision is confined to fixed ground-truth endpoint locations and, unlike their second-phase loss, cannot remove spurious pairs that the prediction creates elsewhere. Third, we fine-tune a pretrained wind-field SR network rather than train a time-conditioned scalar-field decoder. The PD and MT distances we report are therefore evaluation measures that our training procedure never computes.

A related line of work preserves topological and other features during lossy compression, for scalar fields~\cite{Yan2024TopoSZ}, vector fields~\cite{Liang2023FeatureVF}, and as a general augmentation of existing compressors~\cite{Gorski2025TopoA}. These methods share our motivation of retaining analysis-relevant structure under an information-reducing transformation, but the problems differ: a compressor has the original high-resolution field at encoding time and can enforce guarantees on the decoded field, whereas SR receives only a coarse observation and must infer unresolved structure. We therefore treat topology-preserving compression as methodological context rather than a directly comparable baseline.

\section{Data and Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:data-setup}

\subsection{Data source and PhIRE-compatible preprocessing}
Wind fields are drawn from the NREL WIND Toolkit national gridded dataset (NREL, now the National Laboratory of the Rockies, NLR) and accessed through the Highly Scalable Data Service (HSDS). The upstream variables are 100~m wind speed and wind direction, which are converted to horizontal vector components $[u,v]$ using the standard meteorological convention. We use a Mid-Atlantic spatial crop centered near $39.5^{\circ}$N, $75.0^{\circ}$W and follow the PhIRE wind-data organization in which raw high-resolution vector fields are downsampled to form paired lower-resolution inputs and higher-resolution targets. The PhIRE data hierarchy contains a low-resolution to medium-resolution (LR$\rightarrow$MR) stage and an MR$\rightarrow$HR stage. We restrict this study to MR$\rightarrow$HR so that all loss configurations share a single pretrained model, resolution factor, and evaluation pipeline; the losses are not specific to this stage and could in principle be applied to LR$\rightarrow$MR, but we have not evaluated that setting.

The benchmark examples are represented as two-channel vector fields. Each medium-resolution (MR) input is a $100\times100\times2$ field paired with a $500\times500\times2$ high-resolution (HR) ground-truth (GT) field. The SR networks operate on normalized $u$ and $v$ components, while topology and most domain-specific analyses are computed after denormalization on scalar wind-speed magnitude $s=\sqrt{u^2+v^2}$. Every method is evaluated against the same benchmark ground truth.

For topological analysis, we use a fixed $160\times160$ top-left crop of each $500\times500$ denormalized speed field (about 10\% of its area). This crop is used consistently for persistence-diagram extraction, merge-tree evaluation, and the ground-truth persistence pairs used by fixed-pair supervision. It was introduced for computational tractability, not because the subregion has special physical significance. Conventional and domain-specific metrics are evaluated on the full reconstructed fields.

\subsection{Evaluation benchmark and fine-tuning set}
\label{sec:benchmark}
The fixed evaluation benchmark constructed for this study contains 168 consecutive hourly fields from the same spatial region. It is used for the pretrained CNN/GAN comparison and for every fine-tuned model, so all reported winner counts and mean metrics are computed on the same 168 fields. Because the benchmark is also used to compare loss configurations (Sec.~\ref{sec:selection}), it serves as both the evaluation set and the set on which the final configuration was chosen; there is no separate validation split.

For fine-tuning, we build a separate set of 2688 samples using the same spatial region, variable conversion, normalization convention, and MR$\rightarrow$HR preprocessing. It consists of sixteen non-overlapping 168-hour windows, four per season, that are temporally disjoint from the evaluation benchmark. Every fine-tuned model reported in this paper is trained on this set.

\input{figures/data_pipeline_schematic_tikz.tex}

\subsection{Baselines, matched control, and loss configurations}
The pretrained PhIRE CNN and GAN provide fixed baselines. We use the released MR$\rightarrow$HR wind models of Stengel et al.~\cite{Stengel2020}, whose generator is a fully convolutional SRGAN-derived architecture with 16 residual blocks and skip connections; batch-normalization layers are omitted, and the wind network uses two input and output channels for the horizontal wind components. The CNN baseline is this generator after content-only pretraining with a pixelwise MSE loss; GAN training then continues from the pretrained generator with an additional discriminator and adversarial loss. We use the released checkpoints without retraining them. Every fine-tuned model starts from the pretrained CNN checkpoint and is fine-tuned for three epochs with Adam, learning rate $10^{-5}$, and batch size four, on the same 2688-field training set with the same normalization. All 16 configurations fine-tune the native PhIRE (TensorFlow) CNN generator itself; earlier experiments with separate PyTorch residual refiners were development studies and are excluded from this comparison. The \emph{standard fine-tuning control} optimizes only the original vector reconstruction loss $L_{uv}$. The final topology-inspired objective and the 14 other loss configurations add auxiliary losses to $L_{uv}$ and are otherwise identical to the control. Differences between any configuration and the control therefore isolate the effect of its auxiliary losses from that of continued fine-tuning and additional data.

\subsection{Reproducibility}
The source code, experiment scripts, evaluation utilities, and configuration files for this study are available in the tagged release \url{https://github.com/Ardad2/PhIRE/releases/tag/topoinvis-2026-arxiv-v1.1}, together with a reproducibility guide that maps the reported results to their code and data. The repository includes the data-processing path, the fine-tuning implementation for all configurations, and the scripts used to compute the reported fidelity, physics-oriented, distributional, and topological evaluations. The quantitative topology tables and loss-study figures are generated by script from a frozen table of per-field distances (18 methods $\times$ 168 fields), which is released with the code and can be used to check them independently. The qualitative Sample~78 visualization is generated separately from reconstructed fields and persistence diagrams, and the conceptual diagrams are authored with the manuscript source. Retraining the models and re-extracting the topological descriptors from scratch require additional computation and intermediate data that are regenerated by the released scripts.

\section{Topology-Inspired Fine-Tuning}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Vector reconstruction and scalar wind speed}
PhIRE super-resolves the horizontal wind vector. The network receives a coarse two-channel field of normalized $u$ (eastward) and $v$ (northward) components and predicts the same two components at five times the spatial resolution. Its original reconstruction loss $L_{uv}$ is the mean squared error between the predicted and ground-truth normalized components. Because this error is computed jointly on both components, it penalizes the complete vector error and is sensitive to discrepancies in both magnitude and orientation, although it contains no separate angular term. The standard fine-tuning control is therefore not an orientation-only model. Wind speed is the magnitude of this vector. All auxiliary losses are computed after denormalization on
\begin{equation}
 s(x,y)=\sqrt{u(x,y)^2+v(x,y)^2},
\end{equation}
which is also the scalar field used for PD, MT, and most domain metrics. We analyze scalar wind speed rather than vector-field topology because the persistence-diagram and merge-tree constructions used in this study are scalar-field descriptors and wind speed is a physically interpretable quantity, central to wind-energy applications. The analysis therefore does not assess vector-field topology, such as flow critical points or separatrices, which is a distinct direction.

\subsection{Why differentiable proxies}
\label{sec:why-proxies}
Directly optimizing persistence-diagram agreement requires constructing the prediction's diagram and matching it to the target during training. Such a procedure can be made piecewise differentiable by holding the current pairing and matching fixed and differentiating the matched cost with respect to the predicted values at critical vertices, as in prior work~\cite{Gabrielsson2020,Kissi2025}. In our setting, persistence diagrams are extracted by an external TTK computation that is not part of the PhIRE training graph, so this approach would require extracting and matching a diagram for every prediction at every training step. We instead use auxiliary losses that are ordinary differentiable functions of the predicted speed field; topology enters training only through ground-truth locations that are computed once, before training. This keeps fine-tuning inside the existing training loop, but it also means that none of our losses optimizes $d_B$, $W_{2,q}$, or MT distance directly. Agreement under those distances is an empirical outcome that we measure.

We also piloted a more direct formulation: a differentiable cubical-complex persistence-diagram Wasserstein loss applied inside a small residual refiner. It made persistence supervision active during training but did not improve the final TTK-based PD or MT distances on the evaluation topology crop. Because that pilot differed from the final evaluation in crop size, model capacity, implementation framework, and diagram construction, we interpret it cautiously: it suggests that persistence-oriented losses need to be aligned with the evaluated scalar field and diagram convention, not that differentiable topology is ineffective. Fixed-pair supervision (Sec.~\ref{sec:loss-components}) satisfies this alignment by construction, since its pairs come from the same TTK convention and the same fixed $160\times160$ topology crop used for evaluation.

\subsection{Topology-inspired loss components}
\label{sec:loss-components}
\label{sec:ablation-terms}
We developed five auxiliary losses in roughly increasing order of topological specificity. The first three supervise the scalar field as a whole; the last two supervise values at locations selected from the ground-truth topology.

\textbf{Scalar speed.}
$L_{\mathrm{speed}}=\operatorname{MSE}(s_{\mathrm{SR}},s_{\mathrm{GT}})$ supervises the physical magnitude used by the topology evaluation, which $L_{uv}$ constrains only indirectly through the normalized components.

\textbf{Gradient magnitude.}
\begin{equation}
L_{\mathrm{grad}}=\operatorname{MSE}(|\nabla s_{\mathrm{SR}}|,|\nabla s_{\mathrm{GT}}|)
\end{equation}
penalizes errors in local ridges, fronts, and sharp transitions, counteracting the smoothing tendency of standard fine-tuning.

\textbf{Soft high-speed level sets.}
At thresholds $\mathcal{T}=\{5,10,15\}$~m/s, we define a differentiable occupancy mask $M_{\tau}(s)=\sigma\!\left(k(s-\tau)\right)$ with $k=10$, and compare predicted and ground-truth masks:
\begin{equation}
L_{\mathrm{levelset}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{T}|}\sum_{\tau\in\mathcal{T}}
\operatorname{MSE}\!\left(M_{\tau}(s_{\mathrm{SR}}),M_{\tau}(s_{\mathrm{GT}})\right).
\end{equation}
This term encourages agreement in the location and extent of high-speed regions across physically meaningful thresholds.

\textbf{High-speed local maxima.}
Our first attempt to target persistence-relevant critical values directly supervises prominent maxima without computing any persistence. For each ground-truth field, we select pixels that are local maxima within a $3\times3$ neighborhood and satisfy $s_{\mathrm{GT}}(x,y)\geq \mu_s+\sigma_s$, where $\mu_s$ and $\sigma_s$ are the spatial mean and standard deviation of the ground-truth speed for that sample. This per-field threshold is a simple heuristic for keeping maxima that stand out from the field's typical speed; it was not tuned. With $M_{\max}^{(b)}$ the resulting binary mask for batch element $b$,
\begin{equation}
\Lmax=
\frac{\sum_{b,x,y}M_{\max}^{(b)}(x,y)
\left(s_{\mathrm{SR}}^{(b)}(x,y)-s_{\mathrm{GT}}^{(b)}(x,y)\right)^2}
{\max\!\left(\sum_{b,x,y}M_{\max}^{(b)}(x,y),1\right)}.
\label{eq:lcrit}
\end{equation}
Under the sublevel convention these maxima are high critical values that end persistence pairs (Sec.~\ref{sec:topology-background}), but the term supervises neither the other endpoint of any pair nor its persistence. The $3\times3$ (eight-neighbor) test is also simpler than TTK's notion of a maximum: TTK uses an implicit triangulation of the regular grid together with a symbolic tie-breaking order for equal scalar values~\cite{Tierny2018}, whereas the pixel test can flag adjacent pixels on a plateau or identify a maximum that participates differently in TTK's pairing.

\textbf{Fixed-pair supervision.}
Fixed-pair supervision generalizes the local-maximum term by supervising both endpoints of actual ground-truth persistence pairs. For each ground-truth training field, TTK computes the persistence diagram of $s_{\mathrm{GT}}$ once, offline, on the same $160\times160$ topology crop and with the same convention as the evaluation. We consider finite pairs of both families ($D_0$ and $D_1$) with positive persistence, discard pairs whose persistence is below 1\% of the field's maximum persistence, rank the remainder by persistence, and retain the 64 most persistent; every field in the 2688-field training set supplies exactly 64 pairs. For each supervised pair $i$ we store the vertex $v^b_i$ at which the feature is born, the vertex $v^d_i$ at which it dies, and the ground-truth values $b_i=s_{\mathrm{GT}}(v^b_i)$, $d_i=s_{\mathrm{GT}}(v^d_i)$, and $p_i=|d_i-b_i|$. During training, the prediction is read at those fixed vertices, $\hat b_i=s_{\mathrm{SR}}(v^b_i)$ and $\hat d_i=s_{\mathrm{SR}}(v^d_i)$, and two terms are computed:
\begin{align}
\Lcv &= \tfrac12\bigl[\operatorname{MSE}(\hat b,b)+\operatorname{MSE}(\hat d,d)\bigr],\\
\Lpers &= \operatorname{MSE}(\hat p,p), \qquad \hat p_i=|\hat d_i-\hat b_i|,
\end{align}
where each MSE averages over all supervised pairs in the mini-batch; because every field contributes 64 pairs, fields are weighted equally. The critical-value term $\Lcv$ constrains the scalar values at both endpoints of each ground-truth pair, which include saddle-side as well as extremal critical points; it closely follows the critical-value constraint of Kissi et al.~\cite{Kissi2025}. The persistence term $\Lpers$ constrains the scalar contrast across each pair, which is the quantity that determines a feature's distance from the PD diagonal. Figure~\ref{fig:objective-schematic} summarizes the construction.

Fixed-pair supervision is a proxy, not a persistence-diagram loss. No diagram of the prediction is computed during training, the stored pairs are never re-matched to the prediction's own critical points, and nothing in the loss prevents the prediction from creating additional persistence pairs at other locations.

\input{figures/fixed_pair_objective_tikz.tex}

\subsection{Loss study design}
\label{sec:study-design}
Each configuration adds a subset of these losses to $L_{uv}$ with fixed weights: $0.01\,L_{\mathrm{speed}}$, $0.05\,L_{\mathrm{grad}}$, $0.25\,L_{\mathrm{levelset}}$, $0.001\,\Lmax$, and, for fixed-pair supervision, $0.004\,\Lcv+0.002\,\Lpers$. In tables and figures we name configurations by the losses they add: speed (S), gradient (G), level-set (L), local maximum (M), and fixed-pair (FP). The 16 configurations are the standard fine-tuning control; the seven other combinations of S, G, and L; M added to the control, to G, and to S+G+L; and FP added to the control, to G, to G+L, to S+G+L, and to S+G+L+M.

Because all eight combinations of S, G, and L were trained, the average effect of each of these losses, and their interactions, can be estimated (a $2^3$ factorial design). Two further $2\times2$ designs cross FP with M (both added to S+G+L) and with L (both added to G). All 16 configurations were trained once each at the 2688-sample scale, and we report every one.

\textbf{Loss weights.}
Coefficients were calibrated against the numerical scale of the unweighted losses and should not be read as measures of importance (Appendix~\ref{app:weights}). For example, the raw local-maximum loss was about $191\times$ larger than $L_{uv}$ in a calibration diagnostic at the pretrained checkpoint, so a coefficient of $0.001$ still contributed about 19\% of the reconstruction-loss magnitude; the speed, gradient, and level-set terms contributed about 40\%, 91\%, and 27\%. We chose coefficients that kept the auxiliary terms on a broadly comparable scale without severely degrading reconstruction fidelity. During development, a residual-refiner experiment with fixed-pair coefficients of $0.04$ and $0.02$ degraded fidelity (PSNR 30.56~dB versus 31.19~dB for the pretrained CNN), so both coefficients were reduced tenfold before the final experiments. No systematic hyperparameter search was performed. The reported coefficients are therefore not intended as universal constants. For a new dataset or architecture, we would measure the unweighted loss magnitudes at the pretrained checkpoint on a representative calibration set, choose coefficients that place the weighted auxiliary terms on a scale comparable to the reconstruction loss, and select among those settings on a separate validation set; level-set thresholds and pair-selection parameters should likewise be adapted to the scalar range of the target data.

\subsection{Final objective}
\label{sec:final-objective}
The loss study (Secs.~\ref{sec:ablation}--\ref{sec:selection}) identifies gradient supervision and fixed-pair supervision as complementary signals. The final objective combines them:
\begin{equation}
L = L_{uv}+0.05\,L_{\mathrm{grad}}+0.004\,\Lcv+0.002\,\Lpers .
\label{eq:f1-objective}
\end{equation}
Gradient supervision counteracts the smoothing of standard fine-tuning and gives the largest PD improvement among the scalar-field losses; fixed-pair supervision constrains values and persistence at ground-truth pair endpoints and provides the strongest joint PD and MT improvement. In tables and figures this configuration is G+FP.

\section{Evaluation Measures}
\label{sec:evaluation}
We report four groups of measures. Direct fidelity includes PSNR on the two denormalized wind components $[u,v]$, speed MAE/RMSE, and SSIM on scalar speed. For PSNR, the mean squared error is computed over both denormalized components, with the peak range defined by the minimum and maximum ground-truth component values of each field. Speed SSIM is computed on scalar wind-speed magnitude with a per-field data range spanning both the ground truth and the reconstruction. Physics-oriented metrics, distributional measures, component curves, and topological descriptors are computed on scalar wind-speed magnitude derived from the denormalized vector components. Physics and distributional measures include WPD errors and Wasserstein distance, power-spectral-density (PSD) errors, gradient errors and distributional statistics, and high-speed exceedance error. A component-curve $L_1$ measure compares connected-component counts over a dense threshold sweep and serves as a topology-adjacent structural proxy.

\textbf{Topology.}
For each reconstruction, TTK extracts the sublevel persistence diagram of the $160\times160$ speed crop. We keep the two finite pair families separately, $D_0$ (minimum--saddle) and $D_1$ (saddle--maximum), and exclude the single non-finite global pair. With our own implementation of Eqs.~\ref{eq:bottleneck}--\ref{eq:wasserstein}, we compute $d_B$, $\Wi$, and $\Wt$ between the reconstruction and ground-truth diagrams separately for $D_0$ and $D_1$, with unmatched points matched to the diagonal, and combine them as
\begin{equation}
 d_B=\max\bigl(d_B^{D_0},d_B^{D_1}\bigr),\qquad
 W_{2,q}=\Bigl[\bigl(W_{2,q}^{D_0}\bigr)^2+\bigl(W_{2,q}^{D_1}\bigr)^2\Bigr]^{1/2}.
 \label{eq:combine}
\end{equation}
This is equivalent to an optimal matching on the pooled finite diagram that pairs points only within the same family. Implementing the definitions explicitly avoids ambiguity from software-specific distance conventions. MT distance is computed on the same crops with TTK's merge-tree distance between join trees (merge trees of sublevel sets); for readability we call it MT distance throughout. All four distances are lower-is-better. Persistence thresholding is used only to reduce clutter in qualitative figures.

\textbf{Validation of the PD distances.}
Because the PD distances are our own implementation, we validated them along two independent paths. First, GUDHI's distance functions, applied to the same TTK-extracted $D_0$ and $D_1$ diagrams across 51 archived reconstruction runs and 168 fields per run (8568 ground-truth--reconstruction comparisons), reproduce our bottleneck distances exactly; the maximum absolute discrepancy is $1.02\times10^{-12}$ for $\Wi$ and $6.40\times10^{-14}$ for $\Wt$. This validates the distance computation rather than TTK's diagram construction. Second, to test sensitivity to the diagram-construction backend, we rebuilt the diagrams of the pretrained CNN, the standard fine-tuning control, and the final model with GUDHI's cubical complexes for all 168 fields. These diagrams are not identical to TTK's, because the two libraries use different discretization and pairing conventions, but they preserve our conclusions: the ranking of the three models agrees on 161, 165, and 164 of 168 fields for $d_B$, $\Wi$, and $\Wt$, and the direction of the final-model-versus-CNN comparison agrees on 98.8\%, 100\%, and 99.4\% of fields. For merge trees, an independent join-tree construction reproduces TTK's trees, after root normalization, for all 504 SR trees examined. This validates tree construction but not TTK's merge-tree distance itself, which we did not re-implement.

\textbf{Win counts.}
When we say that one method wins on a field, it has the higher PSNR/SSIM or the lower error or distance on that field; win counts are out of the 168 benchmark fields. We report means in the main text; medians (Appendix~\ref{app:medians}) lead to the same conclusions.

\section{Results}

\subsection{Pretrained CNN--GAN trade-off}
The pretrained models expose the evaluation tension that motivates this work. Table~\ref{tab:baseline-winners} reports per-field win counts for the complete evaluation suite. The CNN dominates direct errors, WPD MAE/RMSE, and gradient MAE. The GAN is preferred on PSD log-$L_2$, gradient Wasserstein distance, and slightly more often at the p95 and p99 exceedance thresholds.

The topology rows do not reduce to a single verdict either. MT distance prefers the CNN (148 of 168 fields), and so does bottleneck PD distance (129 of 168; mean $d_B$ 3.124 versus 5.059), whereas both Wasserstein PD distances prefer the GAN (155 and 158 of 168; mean $\Wt$ 17.46 versus 24.55). One reading, consistent with the GAN's sharper output and its behavior on the gradient and spectral measures, is that the GAN better matches the aggregate distribution of birth--death pairs that the Wasserstein distances accumulate, while producing larger worst-case discrepancies and a less faithful merge hierarchy. We offer this as an interpretation rather than an established mechanism. Either way, the preferred reconstruction depends on whether the evaluation emphasizes pointwise error, distributional sharpness, accumulated or worst-case persistence mismatch, or merge hierarchy.

