\subsection*{AI use statement}
Generative AI assisted with proofreading this paper. The Triton kernels were optimized using AI tools for maximum performance. A simple Autoresearch \cite{autoresearch} prompt was used in combination with manual inspection and direction. Our prompt is included in the supplementary materials. After the kernels were optimized, the rest of the supporting software (including PyTorch autograd integration, experimentation and accuracy validation) was written manually with minor help from AI tooling. Experiments were run manually to ensure accuracy.

\subsection*{Reproducibility statement}
The supplementary materials include the BitSparse implementation (\texttt{lib\_sparse}), benchmark scripts (\texttt{experiments}), and language-model experiments (\texttt{nanogpt} and \texttt{nemotron}).

Section~\ref{sec:methodology} describes the storage format, GPU kernels, and shared buffer; Algorithm~\ref{alg:relu-linear} gives the forward and backward passes. Appendix~\ref{app:proofs} contains the proofs of the entropy and compression results. Appendix~\ref{app:implementation} specifies hardware, software versions, random seeds, and baselines. Appendices~\ref{app:mlp-training} and~\ref{app:llm-memory-runtime} detail the MLP and language-model workloads, precision settings, and timing and memory measurements. Appendix~\ref{app:llm-sparsity} describes the data and aggregation used to measure activation sparsity, and Appendix~\ref{app:additional} provides the full-training comparison, additional benchmark results, and ablations.

\subsection*{Ethics statement}
BitSparse compresses intermediate activations during model training and does not itself collect data or introduce a new data source. Reducing GPU memory requirements may make larger training workloads more accessible. Beyond this, any privacy, bias, and misuse risks depend on the use case, rather than BitSparse. Its environmental impact depends on the memory savings and runtime overhead of the complete training workload.

