Tabular foundation models (TFMs) seem very well suited to this regime. Pre-trained on many synthetic datasets, models such as TabPFN and TabICL predict in-context and now outperform gradient-boosted trees for classification and regression, in particular on small datasets \citep{hollmann2023tabpfn,hollmann2025tabpfn,grinsztajn2025tabpfn,qu2025tabicl,qu2026tabiclv2,zhang2025mitra}. This recipe has been extended beyond standard supervised learning to causal effect estimation \citep{robertson2025pfn,balazadeh2025causalpfn,ma2025foundation}. Survival analysis has not yet benefited from this progress. Recent survival TFMs either recast survival as classification over a time grid for off-the-shelf TFMs \citep{kim2026tabular}, or are pre-trained on synthetic survival data \citep{seletkov2026survival,bohm2026survpfn,qi2026survivalpfn} but do not beat the Cox model or random survival forests (Figure~\ref{fig:win_rate}).

\textbf{Building a High-Performing TFM for Survival Analysis.} We introduce SurvICL, a tabular foundation model for in-context survival analysis, built on the TabICLv2 backbone \citep{qu2026tabiclv2}. 
Similar to SurvivalPFN \citep{qi2026survivalpfn}, it handles censoring in the data generation rather than the loss.
During pre-training, SurvICL sees censored datasets in its context, but is supervised with the latent, uncensored event times of the simulator: it learns to correct for censoring in-context, so that a plain quantile-regression loss suffices. SurvICL predicts for each individual a continuous quantile function of the event time, from which we can directly infer a monotone survival function.

\textbf{Scaling Evaluation of Survival Models.} We evaluate SurvICL in the largest benchmark to date for survival analysis: 103 real-world datasets from healthcare, genomics, economics and engineering, with up to 52{,}000 samples and 15{,}000 features, against 20 baselines spanning classical, tree-based, deep and foundation models. For comparison, survival papers at machine-learning venues typically report results on 5 to 20 datasets, and the largest evaluations of survival foundation models span 48 to 81 datasets \citep{kim2026tabular,lyu2026survfm,pham2026adaptation,qi2026survivalpfn}. All design choices were made on 40 development datasets, and all results are reported on 63 held-out datasets never used during development.
On these held-out datasets, SurvICL is the best method both in prediction accuracy and in discrimination (Figure~\ref{fig:win_rate}): it wins 82.9\% of pairwise comparisons for the integrated Brier score (IBS), against 71.3\% for the runner-up, the Cox model, and in 77.7\% for the C-index, against 71.6\% for random survival forests (see Figure \ref{fig:winplotcindex}). On small datasets, where no previous method surpasses the Cox model, SurvICL is the first to rank above it. On the largest datasets, gradient boosting still maintains a slight edge. 
Importantly, these gains do not come at the expense of computational efficiency. Across predictive metrics and dataset-size regimes, SurvICL lies
on or close to the performance-runtime Pareto frontier (Appendix \ref{sec:other_results}).
Thus, SurvICL combines strong predictive performance with efficient out-of-the-box inference.
We commit to releasing code and model in an open-source license.

\textbf{Contributions.} Our contributions are threefold:
\begin{enumerate}
[leftmargin=1.2em,itemsep=0.5pt,topsep=1pt]
    \item \textbf{We design a foundation model for survival analysis} that, using in-context learning, outperforms survival-analysis models (classical, tree-based, deep, all three existing survival foundation models as well as an adaptation of a classification foundation model), on integrated Brier score (IBS) and C-index.
    \item \textbf{We identify key ingredients} of in-context survival analysis through extensive ablations: (i)~a \textbf{proportional-odds (PO) prior} with a flexible, multi-modal baseline hazard, which outperforms Cox, accelerated failure time, and existing TFM priors; and (ii)~\textbf{event-only targets}, rather than also learning the censoring distribution.
    \item \textbf{An extensive benchmark} of 103 survival datasets with a strict separation between development and held-out datasets, covering high-dimensional genomics cohorts ($p > n$) and large registries, and comparing 21 methods on four metrics.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}
\textbf{Deep Learning for Survival Analysis.} The toolbox of survival analysis has been considerably extended in the last years to include deep architectures \citep{katzman2018deepsurv,lee2018deephit,kvamme2019time} that can accommodate a wide variety of data and event types, including time series, competing events, images and text \citep{zhu2016deep,thorsen2022discrete,bleistein2024dynamical, wiegrebe2024deep,alberge2024survival,jeanselme2024review}. Our work builds on this research by tailoring deep transformers and training procedures for tabular data, which is the most common form of survival data.     

\textbf{Deep Learning for Tabular Data.} Gradient-based decision tree models have long dominated benchmarks on tabular data \citep{gorishniy2021revisiting,grinsztajn2022tree,ye2024closer}. Tabular foundation models have recently challenged their superiority and sparked a new line of research that focused on designing tailored architectures and training procedures \citep{hollmann2023tabpfn,hollmann2025tabpfn,qu2025tabicl,qu2026tabiclv2,kim2025table,dingtabula,zhang2025mitra}. We build on these architectural advances and adapt them to calibrated survival prediction. Once pre-trained, our method performs in-context inference without fine-tuning or weight updates. 

\textbf{Tabular Foundation Models for Survival Analysis} is a burgeoning field of research. Efforts follow three distinct strategies.
\emph{(i) Reducing survival to a task that generic TFMs already solve.} \citet{kim2026tabular} frame survival prediction as a series of binary classification problems over a time grid, so that any TFM that handles classification can be deployed for survival analysis. \citet{vieyra2026staying} instead plugs an off-the-shelf TFM regressor into a log-normal accelerated failure time model and impute censored times iteratively, while SurvFM \citep{lyu2026survfm} converts censored follow-up into pseudo-observations of the restricted mean survival time (RMST), which are then regressed in-context by unmodified TFMs. These approaches inherit the strengths of generic TFMs, but their backbone has never seen censored data: they either depend on a time discretization, impose a parametric form on the event-time distribution, or only output a summary statistic (the RMST) rather than a full survival function.
\emph{(ii) Adapting generic TFMs with a survival head.} \citet{pham2026survivalaware,pham2026adaptation} train classical survival heads (Cox, DeepHit, multi-task logistic regression) on top of frozen or fine-tuned representations of TabPFN, TabDPT \citep{ma2025tabdpt}, and TabICL, and systematically compare these adaptation interfaces on 74 datasets. They find that zero-shot reformulations work best on small datasets, whereas trained heads take over as the sample size grows. This line of work matches the common practice of fitting shallow survival models on top of deep foundation models, and gives up the pre-training + in-context learning recipe: the head must be fitted, and possibly tuned, on each new dataset.
\emph{(iii) Pre-training on synthetic survival data.} Closest to our work, \citet{seletkov2026survival}, \citet{bohm2026survpfn} and \citet{qi2026survivalpfn} pre-train a tabular foundation model on synthetic survival data, following previous work in the field of tabular foundation models \citep{hollmann2023tabpfn,qu2025tabicl,qu2026tabiclv2}. All three models predict on a discretized time grid (see Table~\ref{tab:comparison}), while SurvICL outputs a continuous quantile function of the event time. Finally, foundation models for time-to-event prediction have also been pre-trained on large real-world electronic health records \citep{steinberg2024motor}; such models are tied to a specific data modality and coding system, whereas we target generic tabular data through a synthetic prior.

\section{Survival Analysis Settings: Censored Targets, Small Samples}
\label{sec:background}
\subsection{Censored Data and Survival Models}

\textbf{Survival Data.} In survival analysis, data comes in the form of i.i.d. samples $\mathcal{D}_n = \{ (\xb_i, t_i, \delta_i) \}_{i=1}^n$. For each instance $i$, $\xb_i \in \mathbb{R}^d$ denotes the feature vector and $t_i >0$ is a positive valued time. Unlike standard supervised learning, the target is subject to \textit{right-censoring}, meaning that their true event time is not necessarily observed but might be censored, in which case we only observe the censoring time $c_i >0$. This proxy time yields weak information on the true event time, since it will necessarily happen \textit{after} censoring. We denote by $t_i^\star$ the possibly unobserved true time of event, such that
\begin{equation*}
    t_i := \min\{t_i^\star,c_i\} \quad\textnormal{and}\quad \delta_i := \mathds{1}_{t_i^\star \leq c_i}.
\end{equation*}

\textbf{Predicting with Survival Models.} Our goal is to approximate the survival function
\begin{align*}
  S(t\given \xb_i) := \mathbb{P}(T^\star > t\given X = \xb_i)
\end{align*}
at any time $t >0$. This stands in contrast to classification or regression, where the model's goal is to output a value $\widehat{y}_i$ which approximates the binary or continuous outcome $y_i$. The survival function can be used to predict the time of event $\hat{t}_i$ for individual $i$ as the first time it crosses the threshold~$0.5$.

\textbf{Hazard-based Models.} Following the seminal work of \citet{cox1972regression}, an extensive family of models parametrized the hazard of the survival process, the time-wise derivative of the event probability:
\begin{flalign*}
\text{Hazard:} &&    h(t\given \xb_i) := \lim\limits_{\varepsilon \to 0} \frac{\mathbb{P}(T^\star \in [t,t+\varepsilon) \given X = \xb_i, T^\star \geq t )}{\varepsilon} \quad = -\partial_t \log S(t\given \xb_i) &&
\end{flalign*}
and trains it via (partial) likelihood maximization \citep{friedman1982piecewise,fornili2013piecewise,katzman2018deepsurv,kvamme2019time,kvamme2021continuous,bleistein2024dynamical}. Once the hazard is learned, it can be used to predict the survival function using numerical integration.  

\textbf{Discrete-Time Models.} On the other side, some models bin time to simplify the computation of the model's likelihood and frame survival analysis as a classification task \citep{yu2011learning,fotso2018deep,lee2018deephit,kim2026tabular}. More precisely, in their approach, time is discretized into $B+1$ bins $([s_k,s_{k+1}[)_{k=0}^B$ for $0 = s_0 < \dots < s_B = +\infty$. The tuple $(\xb_i,t_i,\delta_i)$ is then expanded into a series of points by creating a series of binary outcomes $y_{i,k} := \mathds{1}_{t_i \leq s_k}$ and considering the expanded dataset $\{((\xb_i,s_k),y_{i,k})_k\}_{i=1}^n$. For individuals who experience the event, $B$ points are created, while for censored individuals only the bins prior to the censoring time are added to the expanded dataset. This approach is the dominant approach in survival TFMs (see Table~\ref{tab:comparison}). While flexible and easy to train, unconstrained formulations of these models can output non-monotonic survival functions, requiring post-hoc workarounds such as clipping probabilities \citep{kim2026tabular}. 

\subsection{Design goals for a Survival TFM}

\textbf{Performance on Small Datasets.} Small datasets are ubiquitous in survival analysis~\citep{o2025systematic}, notably because time-to-event data is expensive and time-consuming to collect (to analyze event times that span over $N$ years, one first needs to wait for $N$ years) and because datasets often originate from healthcare~\citep{indrayan2021importance}. State-of-the-art models perform well on large samples ($n \geq 2000$), but struggle on small datasets \citep{zhao2026tackling}.

\textbf{Out-of-the-Box Calibration.} In survival analysis, accurate probability estimates are crucial for decision-making \citep{goldstein2020x,alberge2026calibration}. Yet, models are often trained and evaluated chasing the C-index, which has been criticized as it does not assess calibration and is not a proper metric  \citep[not minimized for true probabilities,][]{lillelund2025stop}. We train SurvICL to provide calibrated predictions without hyperparameter tuning. 

\section{SurvICL Architecture: Quantiles Supervised by Latent Times}
\label{sec:architecture}

\textbf{The TabICLv2 Backbone.} Our architecture inherits the major components of TabICLv2 \citep{qu2026tabiclv2}. In particular, we use \textit{repeated feature grouping}, which bags groups of features before tokenizing them and \textit{target-aware embedding}. We adapt the original architecture of \citet{qu2026tabiclv2} to handle triplets as inputs, which correspond to features, censoring indicators and observed times. More details on TabICLv2 and our modifications can be found in Appendix \ref{sec:app:architecture}.

\textbf{Continuous Quantile Head.} To obtain calibrated and accurate predictions, we use a multi-quantile pinball loss following TabICLv2. Given an input $\xb \in \mathbb{R}^d$, our model outputs $\hat{\bm{q}}(\xb) = (\hat{q}_\tau(\xb))_{\tau \in \mathcal{T}} \in [0,+\infty)^{M-1}$, whose entries are the predicted quantiles of the event time at the levels $\mathcal{T} = \{1/M, 2/M,\dots, (M-1)/M\}$. We then compute 
\begin{align*}
    \mathcal{L}(\hat{\bm{q}}(\xb),t^\star) := \frac{1}{M-1} \sum\limits_{\tau \in \mathcal{T}} \ell_\tau\big(\hat{q}_\tau(\xb),t^\star\big) \,\textnormal{ where }\,\ell_\tau(\hat{q},t^\star) := \begin{cases}
\tau\,(t^\star - \hat{q}) & \text{if } t^\star \ge \hat{q} \\[4pt]
(1-\tau)\,(\hat{q} - t^\star) & \text{if } t^\star < \hat{q}
\end{cases}
\end{align*}
where $t^\star$ is the latent event time drawn from the prior (see below). We use $M=1000$, i.e., $999$ quantile levels as in TabICLv2.
To ensure monotonicity, we sort the predicted quantiles except during pre-training. Experiments with other numbers of percentiles are reported in Figure \ref{fig:main_results}. This loss is one of the major design choices that drive the capabilities of our model, and distinguishes it from previous models, which use classification losses or the hybrid loss of \citet{lee2018deephit}.

\textbf{Latent Time Supervision.} The target $t^\star$ of the pinball loss is never observed in real data: it is the latent event time generated by our prior (Section~\ref{sec:pretraining}). During pre-training, the context only contains censored observations $(\xb_i,t_i,\delta_i)$, while the loss is computed on the uncensored $t_i^\star$ of the query points. The model thus learns to infer the event-time distribution from censored data in-context. The survival-specific knowledge lives in the prior, so that the above regression loss is valid: we need neither a censored likelihood, nor inverse-probability-of-censoring weights, nor a discretization of time. SurvivalPFN \citep{qi2026survivalpfn} also supervises with latent times, but over a discretized time grid and with censoring times as an additional target, which we find to be detrimental (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}).

\textbf{Mean-Duration Scaling.} We rescale time, to cater for the wide variety of time range across datasets. Our strategy is to divide survival times by the mean observed duration.

\textbf{Event-Only Targets.} In survival analysis, censoring times are often considered to be hurdles to efficient estimation. Interestingly, \citet{qi2026survivalpfn} use the censored times as an alternative target: their model learns to approximate two distributions (one for censored times and one for event times) by predicting the event and censoring times during pre-training. We ablate this mixed prediction task and show that it degrades the performance of our model.

