\section{Pretraining: Synthetic Data Priors with Proportional-Odds}
\label{sec:pretraining}

A key novelty of our work is our synthetic data prior, illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:prior}.

\textbf{Proportional-Odds (PO) Prior.} We start by sampling numerical and categorical features $(\xb_i)_{i=1}^n$ using the structural causal prior of \citet{qu2026tabiclv2}. The number of nodes (from 2 to 32) and features (from 1 to 100) span a wide range to allow for diverse datasets. We then sample survival times from a novel proportional-odds prior. Like the Cox model, it combines a baseline with an individual effect $\eta(\xb_i)$, but on the odds rather than the hazard scale: hazard ratios then vary over time and may vanish in the long run, a common pattern in clinical data that Cox priors cannot generate. For the baseline hazard, we set
\[
            h_0(t)
            = a\,\operatorname{softplus}\!\,\bigl[f(t/t_{\max})\bigr]
            + \sum_{k=1}^{K} A_k
              \exp\!\left(-\frac{(t-\mu_k)^2}{2\sigma_k^2}\right),
        \]
with $a$, $(A_k)_{k=1}^K$, $(\mu_k)_{k=1}^K$ and $(\sigma_k)_{k=1}^K$ positive values, and $t_{\max}>0$. The parameter $K$ is taken randomly between $1$ and $5$. This parametrization adds a random number of positive Gaussian bumps at random times, each with its own width and height, directly on top of the smooth trend. We then integrate this baseline hazard to obtain $S_0(t)=\exp\!\big(-\int_0^t h_0(u)\,du\big)$.
To generate the individual specific part of the hazard function, we apply a random function $\eta(\xb_i)$ to the features as in \citet{qu2026tabiclv2}. We then define the individual-specific survival function as
\begin{align*}
    S(t\given \xb_i) := \frac{1}{\frac{1-S_0(t)}{S_0(t)}\exp(\eta(\xb_i))+1}.
\end{align*}
Setting $\eta(\xb_i)=0$ recovers the baseline hazard, while positive values increase the odds of experiencing the event by any time. This prior draws on a long line of work on mixture models in survival analysis \citep{mclachlan1994role,de1999mixture,li2024bayesian}. We sample event times by inverting the cumulative distribution function, with $U \sim \mathcal{U}([0,1])$:
\begin{align*}
    t_i^\star = H_0^{-1}\Big(\log\big[1+\exp(-\eta(\xb_i))\,(U^{-1}-1)\big]\Big)
\end{align*}
with $H_0(t) := -\log S_0(t)$. We draw $5$ times per individual to speed up convergence.

\textbf{Generating Censoring Times.} We add two sources of censoring to our model. First, in $50\%$ of datasets, we apply a shared end-of-study cutoff to a randomly sampled set of individuals. Secondly, we generate a concurrent censoring time distribution by sampling another pair of baseline and individual specific hazards and combining them as for the event time distribution. We then sample from this second distribution, and set the observed time to be the minimum between the two. Lastly, to introduce tied times, we move some individual times to a common time grid with low probability.    

\textbf{Filtering.} We remove datasets where more than 95\% of samples are censored, as well as \textit{non-predictable} datasets, adapting the filter of \citet{qu2026tabiclv2}: a dataset is non-predictable if predicting the mean survival time achieves a lower IBS (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}) than an ExtraTrees-based survival model \citep{geurts2006extremely}. Every pretraining dataset thus carries exploitable signal.         

\textbf{Training.} During training, we split every generated dataset into a context (train) set $\mathcal{D}_c$ of size $n_c$ and a query (test) set $\mathcal{D}_q$ of size $n_\ell$ with $n=n_c+n_\ell$. We then compute and take the gradients of
\begin{equation*}
    \frac{1}{n_\ell}\sum_{i \in \mathcal{D}_q} \mathcal{L}\big(f_\theta(\xb_i,\mathcal{D}_c),t_i^\star\big),
\end{equation*}
where $f_\theta(\xb_i,\mathcal{D}_c)$ denotes the quantiles predicted from the context $\mathcal{D}_c$; the loss is computed on the query set only. The context size varies during pre-training. 
We adopt the pre-training setup from \cite{qu2026tabiclv2} and only use stage 1 for 200K steps, which takes around 72 hours on two H100 GPUs.

\section{Large Scale Evaluation on 63 Datasets}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Survival Metrics}

We evaluate our models on four different metrics to overcome the pitfalls of single-metric evaluation \citep{lillelund2025stop}. In the following, $N \geq 1$ denotes the size of the evaluation dataset. All metrics are computed using the \texttt{SurvEval} package \citep{qi2023survivaleval}

\textbf{Integrated Brier Score (IBS).} The IBS is computed by integrating the Brier score at time $t >0$, which is equal for an individual to the difference between the predicted survival curve and the observed binary survival indicator function, over time. It is hence equal to 
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{N_{test} t_\textnormal{max}}\sum\limits_{i=1}^N\int_0^{t_\textnormal{max}} \textnormal{BS}_i(t)dt \quad\textnormal{ where }\;\; \textnormal{BS}_i(t) := \frac{\widehat S(t\given \xb_i)^2 \mathds{1}_{t_i \leq t, \delta_i = 1}}{\widehat G(t_i)} + \frac{(1-\widehat S(t \given \xb_i))^2\mathds{1}_{t_i > t}}{\widehat G(t)}. 
\end{align*}
Unlike the C-index, the IBS measures the accuracy of predicted probabilities, not just rankings. 

\textbf{C-index at the 50th Quantile.}
We evaluate discrimination using an IPCW concordance index truncated at the median observed training follow-up time $t_{50}=\operatorname{Quantile}_{0.5}\{t_i^{\mathrm{train}}\}$, where $t_i^{\mathrm{train}}$ includes both event and censoring times. For each test individual, we use the predicted risk $r_i=1-\widehat S(t_{50}\given\xb_i)$.
Concordance is computed over comparable pairs with an observed event before $t_{50}$ and weighted by $\widehat G(t_i)^{-2}$, where $\widehat G$ is the Kaplan--Meier estimate of the censoring survival function fitted on the training data. This corresponds to a truncated IPCW concordance estimator.

\textbf{MAE-Hinge.} We use absolute error for event times and a one-sided hinge error for censored times:
\begin{equation*}
    \mathrm{MAE}_{\mathrm{Hinge}}
    = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}
    \left[
        \delta_i |t_i-\hat t_i|
        + (1-\delta_i)\max(0,t_i-\hat t_i)
    \right],
\end{equation*}
where $\delta_i$ indicates an observed event and $\hat t_i$ is the
predicted median survival time. For censored individuals, only
predictions earlier than the censoring time are penalized.

\textbf{KM-Calibration.} We assess marginal calibration with the normalized integrated squared error, in~$[0,1]$, between the average predicted survival curve $\frac{1}{N_{test}}\sum_{i} \widehat S(t\given \xb_i)$ and the Kaplan--Meier curve of the test set \citep{chapfuwa2020calibration}.

\subsection{Baselines: From Cox to Foundation Models}

All models are described in more detail in Appendix \ref{sec:app:details_baselines}, we refer to Table \ref{tab:all_baselines} for a synthetic overview.  

\textbf{Shallow models.} We include Cox model \citep{cox1972regression} and KM estimator \citep{kaplan1958nonparametric} as baselines. The latter does not use covariates, and hence any model is expected to outperform it.

\textbf{Deep Networks.} We include five deep networks tailored for survival analysis \citep{lee2018deephit, katzman2018deepsurv,nagpal2021deep,rindt2022survival,kirpichenko2026tabsurv}. These models are fitted on each dataset and use various modeling techniques (from Bayesian prior to a MLP that predicts the survival function at different times). 

\textbf{Tree-Based Models.} Following the seminal work of \citet{ishwaran2008random} on random survival forests (RSF) and \citet{grinsztajn2022tree}, a body of work has adapted tree-based methods to survival analysis. On top of RSF, we evaluate SurvivalBoost \citep{alberge2024survival} and alternative gradient-boosted tree-based methods implemented in \texttt{sksurv} \citep{polsterl2020scikit}. 

\textbf{Tabular Foundation Models (TFMs).} We include four open-weight tabular foundation models in our benchmark: SurvPFN \citep{bohm2026survpfn}, SurvivalPFN \citep{qi2026survivalpfn}, Survival-IC (SIC) \citep{seletkov2026survival} and TFM4Survival \citep{kim2026tabular}.

\subsection{Datasets and Hold-Out Protocol}

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{iclr/figures/illustrations/datasets-2.pdf}
