In total, we consider 103 datasets with varying properties, see Figure \ref{fig:datasets_overview}. The datasets are partitioned into 40 development datasets used during model development and 63 held-out datasets at random. For development, we source 2 PyCox \citep{kvamme2019time}, 25 SurvSet \citep{drysdale2022}, one from a \href{https://challengedata.ens.fr/challenges/162}{challenge} and 12 cBioPortal \citep{cerami2012cbio,gao2013integrative,de2023analysis} datasets. For held-out datasets, we use 6 from Pycox, 39 from SurvSet and 18 datasets from cBioPortal.

\textbf{Protocol.} 
All design choices of SurvICL (prior, target, and checkpoint) were made using the 40 development datasets; the 63 held-out datasets were kept for the final evaluation. We evaluated each method over ten event-stratified 80/20 train--test splits (test sets capped at 4,096 observations).
Baseline hyperparameters were selected using a budget of up to 50 random search steps per dataset, minimizing the integrated Brier score on an event-stratified 80/20 split within the seed-0 training partition. The selected configuration was reused across all ten evaluation splits, with each estimator refitted on the corresponding training split. Methods without tunable hyperparameters were evaluated directly. Search spaces and method-specific budgets are detailed in Appendix~\ref{sec:app:details_baselines}. Cox regression uses an $\ell_2$ penalty to regularize estimation, including when $p>n$.

SurvICL uses each dataset's training split as context, with at most 4,096 labeled observations selected by event-stratified subsampling, without fine-tuning or dataset-specific hyperparameter tuning. Benchmark results for all methods and metrics are in Appendix~\ref{sec:other_results}. %
Baseline runtimes measure fitting and survival-curve prediction, excluding hyperparameter search and metric computation.

\subsection{Results: SurvICL Is Best Overall}

\textbf{SurvICL is the best survival model overall.} On the 63 held-out datasets, SurvICL wins 82.9\% of pairwise comparisons in IBS, against 71.3\% for the runner-up, the Cox model (Figure~\ref{fig:win_rate}), and the difference is statistically significant (Figure~\ref{fig:main_results}a, ranks and winrates are related by an affine transform). It also achieves the highest win rate in C-index, 77.7\% against 71.6\% for random survival forests (Figures~\ref{fig:main_results}b and~\ref{fig:winplotcindex}). All existing survival TFMs trail behind classical methods. That the runners-up are the Cox model and random survival forests, with few parameters and strong inductive biases, underlines how hard survival analysis is for flexible models, which overfit small censored datasets.

\textbf{SurvICL is the first model to rank above Cox on small datasets.} 69.8\% of our held-out datasets have fewer than 1{,}000 samples, making this regime central and not a niche. SurvICL is the first to rank above Cox in IBS (win rate 84.9\% against 77.2\%, Figure~\ref{fig:winplotibssmall}) and it matches Cox in C-index, although these differences are not statistically significant (Figures~\ref{fig:cdplotibssmall} and~\ref{fig:cdplotcindexsmall}).

