\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:comparison}

\textbf{Designing Efficient Survival TFMs.} SurvICL differs from existing survival TFMs along three axes (Table~\ref{tab:comparison}). \emph{Output and supervision.} Unlike previous survival TFMs that rely on discrete time bins such as TFM4Survival \citep{kim2026tabular} and SurvivalPFN \citep{qi2026survivalpfn}, which use classification or likelihood losses over time bins, SurvPFN \citep{bohm2026survpfn} uses a censored likelihood with a ranking term over 1{,}000 bins, and Survival-IC \citep{seletkov2026survival} uses the DeepHit loss \citep{lee2018deephit} over 10 bins. SurvICL is the first to output a continuous quantile function. Quantile outputs are not new per se: TabICLv2 \citep{qu2026tabiclv2} uses a pinball loss for regression, and censored quantile regression \citep{portnoy2003censored,pearce2022censored} modifies this loss to account for censoring. With latent-time supervision, no such modification is needed, as censoring is handled by the prior. SurvivalPFN also supervises with latent times, but over a time grid and with censoring times as an additional target, which our ablations show to be detrimental. \emph{Prior.} SurvivalPFN draws times from random noised neural networks with several censoring mechanisms, SurvPFN draws Weibull times from a risk score and a dataset-specific shape, and Survival-IC mixes an extended hazard and a discrete-time model, with covariate-independent and administrative censoring. Our PO prior instead combines a flexible, multi-modal baseline hazard with a random risk function, for both event and censoring times; in our ablations, it outperforms the SurvPFN prior as well as Cox and AFT priors. \emph{Backbone and evaluation.} Like Survival-IC, built on TabICL \citep{qu2025tabicl}, we reuse an existing architecture, TabICLv2, which is not our contribution. Finally, we provide, to our knowledge, the first comparison of all four survival TFMs, on 63 datasets.

\textbf{Implications.} On the small cohorts that dominate clinical research, SurvICL is the first model to rank above the Cox model, the default of practitioners for half a century, without any training or tuning, while remaining among the best-calibrated methods. This makes foundation models a credible default for survival analysis.

\textbf{Limitations and outlook.} On the largest datasets, gradient boosting remains slightly stronger, calling for longer contexts or light fine-tuning. Baseline hyperparameters are tuned on the first split and reused across splits; as tuning data then overlaps other splits' test sets, this protocol, if anything, favors the baselines. %
SurvICL addresses right-censored, single-event data; competing risks and time-varying covariates are natural extensions of the prior. We release our code and models to foster research on foundation models for time-to-event data.

